"""
Curated historical context, source citations, and reference data per country.

Unlike the quantitative indicators (pulled live from the World Bank API),
this is hand-researched and fact-checked via web search against primary/news
sources (IMF, Reuters, AP, Al Jazeera, Bloomberg, Wikipedia, CFR, Britannica,
government/parliamentary research services) as of August 2026. It is a
curated snapshot of major events, not a live feed — dates and figures are
verified against the cited source for each entry, but this list is
illustrative, not exhaustive.

Each historical event is a tuple: (year, event_text, source_name, source_url)
"""

HISTORICAL_CONTEXT = {
    'AFG': [(2021, "Taliban forces took Kabul on August 15, 2021 as the Western-backed government collapsed and President Ashraf Ghani fled, ending two decades of US/NATO-backed rule. The US immediately froze roughly $9bn of Afghan central bank (Da Afghanistan Bank) assets held abroad and European governments froze about $2bn more, while donors who had financed some 75% of the prior government's budget halted aid overnight. The abrupt aid and reserves freeze triggered a banking-system run, weekly withdrawal caps, and a currency collapse that together produced what the UN called an economy collapsing 'before our eyes.'", 'PBS Frontline / NPR', 'https://www.pbs.org/wgbh/frontline/article/taliban-takeover-how-frozen-assets-foreign-aid-impacts-afghanistan'), (2022, "In February 2022 the Biden administration moved to split the roughly $7bn in frozen Afghan central bank reserves, and in September 2022 formally established the 'Fund for the Afghan People,' a Swiss-based non-profit foundation holding $3.5bn of those assets to be disbursed for currency and price stabilization independent of the Taliban and the central bank. The other $3.5bn remained potentially subject to claims from 9/11 victims' families in US courts. The arrangement left Afghanistan's most basic monetary-stabilization tool outside sovereign control, a structural constraint still unresolved as of 2026.", 'United States Institute of Peace / CNN', 'https://www.usip.org/publications/2022/09/us-move-afghanistans-frozen-central-bank-reserves-new-swiss-fund'), (2023, 'A series of 6.3-magnitude earthquakes struck Herat province starting October 7, 2023, killing more than 1,480 people, damaging over 3,300 homes and directly affecting 1.6 million people. The UN estimated $217 million in direct physical damage plus $78.9 million in economic losses, against recovery needs of roughly $403 million -- needs that went largely unmet given the collapse in international donor engagement with the Taliban government. The disaster compounded an already fragile humanitarian and economic situation with almost no functioning state safety net.', 'UN OCHA / UN Afghanistan', 'https://afghanistan.un.org/en/261118-un-reports-staggering-us-4029-million-recovery-needs-following-last-year%E2%80%99s-earthquakes-herat'), (2025, "On January 20, 2025 the incoming Trump administration issued an executive order suspending nearly all US foreign aid pending a 90-day review; the US had accounted for 43.7% of the $1.71bn in foreign funding to Afghanistan in 2024 and roughly two-thirds of what had been disbursed so far in 2025. By late January 2025 the Taliban's own deputy economy minister admitted more than 50 aid organizations had ceased operations across 28 provinces, hitting health, agriculture and education services. The freeze exposed how dependent Afghanistan's residual service economy remained on Western aid even under Taliban rule.", 'Afghan Witness / Security Council Report', 'https://www.info-res.org/afghan-witness/reports/us-aid-freeze-leaves-afghan-economy-and-aid-sector-in-crisis/'), (2025, 'From October 2025, escalating cross-border clashes with Pakistan over militant sanctuaries closed key crossings for extended periods, cutting bilateral trade from about $2.46bn in 2024 to $1.77bn in 2025. Afghan exporters adapted by rerouting trade through Iran and Central Asia -- for example, Afghan grape exports to Pakistan collapsed from 44,225 tonnes worth $13.8 million in 2025 to just 256 tonnes worth about $100,000 in the equivalent 2026 period even as overall Afghan trade held up via new routes. The episode shows the Taliban administration actively diversifying away from historic dependence on the Pakistani transit corridor.', 'Modern Diplomacy / Ariana News', 'https://moderndiplomacy.eu/2026/01/06/afghan-trade-holds-steady-in-2025-despite-pakistan-border-closures/'), (2021, "Following the Taliban takeover, the US and allies cut Afghanistan's central bank (Da Afghanistan Bank) off from the international financial system and froze roughly $9.5 billion in reserves, triggering a severe cash and dollar-liquidity crisis. Banks and businesses could not access foreign currency, hunger and unemployment spiked, and the UN warned of a 'colossal' collapse of the banking system within months. This immediate financial paralysis set the template for Afghanistan's ongoing isolation from formal international finance, a structural constraint still shaping its sovereign risk profile today.", 'CNN / HRW', 'https://www.cnn.com/2021/11/22/investing/un-afghanistan-banking-collapse/index.html'), (2022, "In April 2022 the Taliban imposed a nationwide ban on opium poppy cultivation, and by the 2023 harvest cultivation had collapsed by roughly 95% (from 233,000 to 10,800 hectares), cutting opium supply from 6,200 to 333 tons and farmer income from about $1.36 billion to $110 million. The ban devastated rural households reliant on poppy income, with UNODC finding most farmers in provinces like Badakhshan received no adequate replacement livelihood. The episode shows the Taliban's capacity for rapid, enforceable policy shifts, but also the absence of any substitute formal economy to absorb the shock — a structural vulnerability for any future engagement or aid strategy.", 'UNODC', 'https://www.unodc.org/unodc/en/press/releases/2023/November/afghanistan-opium-cultivation-in-2023-declined-95-per-cent-following-drug-ban_-new-unodc-survey.html'), (2023, "Despite international isolation, the afghani appreciated roughly 14% in 2023 and a further 15.7% in 2024 against the US dollar, at one point ranking among the world's best-performing currencies, driven by strict Taliban capital controls (capping cash exports at $500 by land/$5,000 by air), a fixed banknote supply, and continued inflows of UN humanitarian cash aid (about $2.34 billion between August 2021 and April 2023). The apparent currency stability masks deep repression of trade and investment rather than genuine economic strength. For risk analysts this is a cautionary example of how administrative currency controls can produce misleadingly favorable FX metrics under an otherwise fragile, aid-dependent economy.", 'Bloomberg / Policy Circle', 'https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2023-09-25/taliban-controls-world-s-best-performing-currency-this-quarter'), (2023, "Since late 2023 Pakistan has forcibly returned over 1.4 million Afghans under its 'Illegal Foreigners Repatriation Plan,' including at least 844,499 deportations by February 2025, later expanding in August 2025 to include holders of UNHCR-registered Proof of Registration cards despite earlier assurances they would be spared. The mass returns have strained Afghanistan's already-collapsed labor market and public services while removing a significant remittance-sending diaspora population. This returnee crisis compounds Afghanistan's humanitarian and fiscal burden just as international aid funding has been sharply cut, deepening its dependence on a shrinking pool of external support.", 'Amnesty International / CFR', 'https://www.amnesty.org/en/latest/news/2025/03/opaque-illegal-foreigners-repatriation-plan-targeting-afghan-refugees-must-be-withdrawn/'), (2023, "In January 2023 Afghanistan's Taliban administration signed its first major extraction deal with a foreign company, a 25-year, $540 million oil contract with China's CAPEIC to develop the Amu Darya basin — but by August 2025 the Taliban had terminated the contract, citing the company's failure to meet investment and production commitments. The episode highlights both the Taliban's eagerness to attract Chinese capital as a substitute for Western engagement and the practical difficulties of translating diplomatic goodwill into functioning extractive investment under sanctions and weak institutions. It signals continued volatility in Afghanistan's efforts to monetize its natural resource base.", 'The Diplomat / Global Voices', 'https://thediplomat.com/2025/08/taliban-ends-chinese-oil-field-contract-in-afghanistan/'), (2024, "In July 2024, Chinese state-owned MCC finally broke ground on the long-stalled Mes Aynak copper project — one of the world's largest untapped copper deposits, originally contracted in 2007 — after years of delay from security concerns and inadequate power infrastructure; as of the ribbon-cutting, meaningful progress remained limited due to continuing infrastructure gaps. The project is central to Taliban hopes of building a legitimate mineral-export economy, but its 17-year history of stalled implementation illustrates the execution risk facing any large foreign investment in Afghanistan. It remains a bellwether for whether China will convert diplomatic engagement into real capital deployment.", 'Foreign Policy / RFE/RL', 'https://foreignpolicy.com/2024/09/10/china-taliban-copper-afghanistan-economy-minerals-mes-aynak/'), (2024, "The Taliban's ban on girls' education beyond grade six (since September 2021) and women from university (since December 2022) has denied more than 2.6 million girls a secondary education, and UNICEF estimates the restrictions on girls' education and women's employment cost Afghanistan's economy roughly $84 million a year, equivalent to about 2.5% of annual GDP, with the female civil-service share falling from 21% to 17.7% between 2023 and 2025. Beyond the humanitarian toll, the policy is projected to cost up to 25,000 female teachers and health workers by 2030, permanently degrading human capital. This is a structural, self-inflicted drag on long-run growth potential that donors and investors continue to cite when assessing Afghanistan's development trajectory.", 'UNICEF', 'https://www.unicef.org/press-releases/restrictions-girls-education-and-womens-employment-afghanistan-could-lead-loss-over'), (2025, "On July 3, 2025, Russia became the first country to formally recognize the Taliban as Afghanistan's legitimate government, accepting the credentials of Kabul's new ambassador after Moscow's Supreme Court removed the Taliban from its terrorism list in April 2025. The recognition, driven partly by shared concern over ISIS-Khorasan, offers the Taliban a diplomatic breakthrough after four years of international isolation, potentially paving the way for other states to follow. For sovereign risk purposes it marks an inflection point in Afghanistan's re-entry into formal interstate relations, though it has not yet translated into IMF/World Bank engagement or unblocking of frozen assets.", 'Washington Post / Bloomberg', 'https://www.washingtonpost.com/world/2025/07/04/russia-afghanistan-recognize-taliban-rule/'), (2025, "A 6.0-magnitude earthquake struck Kama district, Nangarhar province, on August 31, 2025, with the death toll ultimately exceeding 2,200 and over 3,600 injured across Nangarhar, Kunar, Laghman, and Nuristan provinces — the deadliest quake to hit Afghanistan since 1998, destroying over 6,700 homes. The disaster hit just as international humanitarian funding for Afghanistan had collapsed from $3.3 billion (2022) to about $1 billion (2025), leaving the Taliban-run relief response severely under-resourced. The event underscores Afghanistan's compounding vulnerability to natural disasters at precisely the moment donor fatigue and aid cuts have gutted its emergency response capacity.", 'CBS News / PBS NewsHour', 'https://www.cbsnews.com/news/afghanistan-earthquake-deaths-rescue-kunar-nangarhar-survivors/')],
    'ARE': [(2020, "In a joint announcement on August 13, 2020, the US, Israel, and the UAE agreed to normalize relations, and the Abraham Accords were formally signed at the White House on September 15, 2020, making the UAE the first Gulf state to establish full diplomatic relations with Israel. The accords opened the door to direct flights, tourism, and -- later -- a full trade agreement, while signaling the UAE's growing willingness to decouple bilateral economic interests from the Palestinian issue. Three years on, bilateral non-oil trade had grown into the billions of dollars, though the relationship faced renewed strain after the October 2023 Gaza war.", 'Al Jazeera', 'https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2023/9/17/three-years-on-how-have-the-abraham-accords-done-for-the-uae'), (2021, "The UAE's Expo 2020 world's fair, originally scheduled for October 2020, was postponed by a full year to October 2021-March 2022 after a two-thirds majority of Bureau International des Expositions member states approved Dubai's request amid the COVID-19 pandemic. The delay came just as the pandemic and the 2020 Saudi-Russia oil price war had already depressed government revenue, compounding pressure on a flagship project meant to showcase Dubai's post-oil economic model to some 24 million expected visitors. The eventually successful running of the Expo helped restore confidence in Dubai's tourism and real-estate sectors heading into 2022.", 'Al Jazeera', 'https://www.aljazeera.com/economy/2020/5/4/expo-2020-dubai-postponed-to-2021-due-to-coronavirus'), (2022, "The UAE and Israel signed a Comprehensive Economic Partnership Agreement (CEPA) on May 31, 2022, which entered into force April 1, 2023, eliminating or reducing tariffs on over 96% of goods and opening Israeli financial services, tourism, and telecoms markets to UAE firms. The deal aimed to lift bilateral non-oil trade -- about $2.5 billion in 2022 -- beyond $10 billion within five years, building directly on the 2020 Abraham Accords. It was the UAE's first free-trade-style agreement with Israel and part of a broader CEPA program since extended to more than two dozen partners as Abu Dhabi diversifies trade relationships beyond hydrocarbons.", 'PwC', 'https://www.pwc.com/m1/en/services/tax/me-tax-legal-news/2023/uae-israel-cepa.html'), (2023, "ADNOC Gas listed 5% of its shares on the Abu Dhabi Securities Exchange in March 2023, raising $2.5 billion in the largest IPO globally that year and drawing $124 billion in orders -- more than 50 times oversubscribed -- at an implied company valuation near $50 billion. The listing extended Abu Dhabi's strategy, pioneered with Aramco's 2019 IPO in Saudi Arabia, of monetizing state hydrocarbon assets through domestic capital markets to deepen liquidity on the ADX. It reinforced the UAE's position as the Gulf's most active IPO market alongside Saudi Arabia's Tadawul.", 'Bloomberg', 'https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2023-03-03/abu-dhabi-s-adnoc-gas-raises-2-5-billion-in-year-s-biggest-ipo'), (2023, "The UAE's first federal corporate tax, enacted under Federal Decree-Law No. 47 of 2022, took effect on June 1, 2023, levying 9% on business profits above AED 375,000 while leaving smaller businesses untaxed -- the UAE's first broad-based corporate tax after decades as a near-zero-tax jurisdiction. The move was driven partly by the OECD's global minimum tax push and partly by a desire to diversify government revenue away from oil and free-zone fees. It marked a structural shift in the UAE's fiscal model, even as authorities emphasized the rate remains among the lowest globally to preserve the country's appeal as a business hub.", 'UAE Ministry of Finance', 'https://mof.gov.ae/en/news/the-ministry-of-finance-announces-the-introduction-of-a-corporate-tax-in-the-uae/'), (2023, "Dubai hosted COP28, the UN Climate Change Conference, from November 30 to December 13, 2023, drawing roughly 85,000 participants including over 150 heads of state or government. The summit produced the first global agreement to 'transition away from fossil fuels' in 28 years of UN climate negotiations, operationalized a Loss and Damage Fund with over $726 million pledged, and set goals to triple renewable capacity and double energy efficiency by 2030. Hosting the summit -- while ADNOC's chief executive simultaneously served as COP President -- highlighted the tension between the UAE's fossil-fuel-dependent economy and its diversification into renewables and climate diplomacy.", 'Carbon Brief / UN', 'https://www.carbonbrief.org/cop28-key-outcomes-agreed-at-the-un-climate-talks-in-dubai/'), (2024, "FATF removed the UAE from its 'grey list' of jurisdictions under increased monitoring on February 23, 2024, roughly two years after adding it in March 2022 over anti-money-laundering and terrorist-financing deficiencies, after Abu Dhabi implemented all 15 of the watchdog's recommended actions. The EU subsequently removed the UAE from its own high-risk AML list in 2025 following the FATF exit. The delisting reduced compliance friction for UAE banks and investors dealing with international counterparties and reinforced Dubai and Abu Dhabi's ambitions as global financial and wealth-management hubs.", 'Norton Rose Fulbright', 'https://www.nortonrosefulbright.com/en/knowledge/publications/eb06aa7c/uae-removed-from-the-fatf-grey-list'), (2024, "The UAE agreed a $35bn Ras El-Hekma coastal development deal with Egypt (Feb 23, 2024), the single largest FDI inflow in Egyptian history, converting a chunk of Egypt's frozen deposit liabilities into equity in a flagship real-estate project. The deal arrived at the exact moment Egypt's currency and reserves crisis was most acute, and the IMF expanded its own EFF to $8bn within weeks -- the two moved in tandem to backstop the pound. It illustrates how Gulf capital increasingly acts as first-responder financing before IMF programs are finalized.", 'The Washington Institute', 'https://www.washingtoninstitute.org/policy-analysis/imf-and-uae-swoop-ease-egypts-economic-crisis'), (2025, "UAE non-oil foreign trade hit a record roughly AED 3 trillion in 2024, with CEPA partnerships helping drive growth; India remained the top non-oil export destination, taking 13.5% of UAE non-oil exports in 2024 (up 75.2% year-on-year), while bilateral UAE-India trade reached about $65 billion. Non-oil trade continued accelerating into 2025-26, with China remaining the UAE's largest overall trading partner. The pattern underscores how CEPA-driven non-oil trade diversification, rather than oil exports alone, is increasingly central to the UAE's growth model and its bid to become a global logistics and re-export hub.", 'The National', 'https://www.thenationalnews.com/business/economy/2025/02/05/uae-foreign-trade-hits-record-dh3-trillion-in-2024-as-cepas-drive-growth/'), (2019, "The UAE introduced the Golden Visa in 2019, offering renewable 5- or 10-year residency to investors, entrepreneurs and skilled professionals independent of employer sponsorship. By mid-2024 over 158,000 Golden Visas had been issued in Dubai alone, driving real-estate demand and non-oil sector growth by attracting high-net-worth individuals and capital. It marked a structural shift toward a residency-for-investment model that now underpins the UAE's non-oil FDI and property-market resilience.", 'Arabian Business', 'https://www.arabianbusiness.com/opinion/will-uae-golden-visa-reforms-lead-to-more-expat-spending-and-investment'), (2021, "Abu Dhabi's Mubadala Investment Company closed 2021 with AUM of Dh1.04 trillion, up from Dh845bn in 2020, and posted Dh122bn in comprehensive income -- its best performance in its 20-year history, driven by public and private equity gains. The results illustrated how Gulf sovereign wealth funds used the 2021 global recovery to accelerate outbound diversification into technology and renewables. Mubadala's scale and returns are a key buffer analysts cite when assessing Abu Dhabi's capacity to absorb oil-price shocks.", 'Gulf News', 'https://gulfnews.com/business/markets/abu-dhabi-wealth-fund-mubadala-closes-2021-with-assets-of-dh104t---and-best-performance-in-20-year-history-1.1652263227115'), (2022, "On 17 January 2022 Houthi forces struck Abu Dhabi's Musaffah industrial area and the international airport with drones and missiles, killing three people -- the first serious attack of its kind on the UAE despite a series of regional Houthi strikes since 2017. The Dubai Financial Market fell nearly 2% in the aftermath, and the attack accelerated UAE investment in air-defense systems. It demonstrated that Gulf hydrocarbon and logistics infrastructure remains exposed to Yemen-conflict spillover, a risk factor still relevant given continued Houthi Red Sea activity.", 'CNBC', 'https://www.cnbc.com/2022/02/11/attacks-on-uae-shows-its-strengths-more-than-vulnerabilities-analysts.html'), (2026, "UAE real GDP grew 3% year-on-year in Q1 2026 to Dh485bn, with non-oil GDP expanding 4.8% and non-oil activity now contributing 79.4% of the economy, up from 78% a year earlier; the Central Bank projected 5.6% full-year growth. Financial and insurance activities were the fastest-growing segment, up 17.3%, reflecting continued momentum from the 2023 corporate-tax and CEPA-driven diversification push. The figures reinforce the UAE's position as the most diversified, and therefore most oil-price-insulated, economy among Gulf sovereigns in this dataset.", 'Gulf News', 'https://gulfnews.com/business/economy/uae-gdp-grows-3-to-dh485-billion-in-first-quarter-of-2026-1.500630054')],
    'BGD': [(2013, "On April 24, 2013 the eight-story Rana Plaza garment-factory building collapsed in Savar, killing more than 1,100 workers and injuring thousands, after inspectors had flagged structural cracks that owners ignored. The disaster forced revisions to Bangladesh's Labour Act enabling trade-union registration without owner permission and the creation of the European-led Accord on Fire and Building Safety and the North American Alliance for Bangladesh Worker Safety, which together inspected thousands of factories. It reshaped -- without derailing -- the ready-made-garment (RMG) sector that remains the backbone of Bangladesh's export economy today.", 'ILO / NPR', 'https://webapps.ilo.org/infostories/en-GB/Stories/Country-Focus/rana-plaza.html'), (2023, 'The IMF Executive Board approved a $4.7bn, 42-month program for Bangladesh on January 30, 2023, combining $3.3bn under blended Extended Credit Facility/Extended Fund Facility arrangements with $1.4bn under the new Resilience and Sustainability Facility -- making Bangladesh the first Asian country to access the RSF. The program targeted depleting reserves, high inflation, weak revenue collection and banking-sector governance gaps. It set fiscal and reserve-related conditionality that has since shaped budget policy under both the Hasina and interim Yunus governments.', 'IMF / Business Standard', 'https://www.tbsnews.net/economy/imf-approves-bangladeshs-45-billion-loan-proposal-576942'), (2024, "Prime Minister Sheikh Hasina was ousted on August 5, 2024 and fled to India after weeks of deadly state repression against student-led protests that began over quota-based public-sector hiring preferences, ending her 15-year Awami League rule. Nobel laureate Muhammad Yunus was sworn in on August 8, 2024 to lead an interim government tasked with administrative and electoral reform. The transition triggered capital flight and a sharp reassessment of Bangladesh's sovereign risk profile just as its IMF program was underway.", 'House of Commons Library / Wikipedia', 'https://commonslibrary.parliament.uk/research-briefings/cbp-10096/'), (2024, "In December 2024 the interim government sought additional IMF funding on top of its existing package while simultaneously holding the fiscal deficit to 3.6% of GDP -- lower than in prior years -- signaling a deliberate pivot toward fiscal restraint aligned with Fund conditionality. This combination of seeking more support while tightening spending reflected the interim administration's effort to rebuild credibility with both the IMF and foreign investors after the political shock. It set the stage for the historic mid-2025 budget contraction.", 'News on Air (India)', 'https://www.newsonair.gov.in/next-general-election-can-be-held-between-end-2025-to-mid-2026-bangladesh-interim-govt-head-yunus/'), (2025, "In June 2025 Bangladesh's interim government presented a national budget that shrank in nominal terms for the first time in the country's history, a break from decades of steadily expanding annual budgets. The contraction reflected both IMF-linked fiscal discipline and the interim administration's more cautious revenue projections amid political uncertainty. It underscored how deeply the 2024 political transition altered fiscal policy management even a year on.", 'The Diplomat', 'https://thediplomat.com/2025/06/bangladeshs-interim-government-shrinks-annual-budget-for-the-first-time-in-history/'), (2025, "Bangladesh's ready-made garment (RMG) exports totalled $39.35bn in FY2024-25 (July 2024-June 2025), up 8.84% year-on-year, with RMG anchoring roughly 80% of the country's approximately $48bn in total exports. The EU took just over 50% of RMG exports (about $19.7bn) while the US remained the single largest national buyer at around $7.5bn. The figures showed the sector's resilience through the political transition, though growth later slowed to roughly 1% year-on-year by FY2025-26.", 'Apparel Resources / BSS News', 'https://www.bssnews.net/at-a-glance/291544'), (2026, "Bangladesh is scheduled to hold its 13th parliamentary elections on February 12, 2026, alongside a national referendum on constitutional and electoral reforms -- the first national vote since Hasina's August 2024 ouster and the first electoral cycle administered under an interim-government framework since the caretaker-government system was abolished in 2011. The Awami League's registration was suspended by the Election Commission ahead of the vote. The outcome will determine whether the IMF program and reform agenda continue under a democratically mandated government or face renegotiation.", 'IFES / DD News', 'https://www.ifes.org/tools-resources/election-snapshots/elections-bangladesh-2026%20Parliamentary-Elections-National-Referendum'), (2022, "Bangladesh's gross foreign exchange reserves fell from a peak of about $48 billion in August 2021 to roughly $33 billion by end-2022, while the taka depreciated sharply (from about 86 to over 120 per dollar by 2025), driven by an import surge, falling remittances, and export weakness; the crisis was compounded by an $3.1 billion loan-default scandal centered on Islami Bank and ten other lenders. The reserve depletion was the proximate trigger for Bangladesh's approach to the IMF later in 2022-23. This currency and banking stress exposed long-standing weaknesses in financial-sector governance and external buffer management that continue to weigh on the country's credit profile.", 'Business Standard / TBS News', 'https://www.tbsnews.net/economy/banking/bangladesh-sees-biggest-devaluation-single-day-dollar-rate-raised-tk7-846156'), (2023, "In May 2023, Fitch and Moody's both downgraded Bangladesh's sovereign credit rating, citing a sustained weakening of external buffers — reserves that had fallen roughly 35% from their June 2022 level to $21.8 billion by mid-2024, covering only about 3.3 months of import cover. This marked Bangladesh's entry into a multi-year downgrade cycle even as it began IMF-supported reforms, showing that early-stage fund support did not immediately arrest credit deterioration. The downgrades raised Bangladesh's external borrowing costs at a time when it most needed affordable financing.", 'Business Standard / TBS News', 'https://www.tbsnews.net/economy/banking/fitch-rating-downgrades-bangladesh-b-outlook-stable-861546'), (2024, "Student-led protests against a reinstated civil-service job quota system escalated dramatically after July 15, 2024, when Awami League-affiliated Chhatra League members attacked peaceful Dhaka University demonstrators; violence spread nationwide, over 200 people were killed within ten days (75 in a single day, July 19), and the government imposed a nationwide internet blackout and deployed the army and RAB under a shoot-on-sight curfew. The economic shutdown from the communications blackout halted RMG export operations and shook investor confidence in the middle of an IMF program. This protest wave was the direct precursor to Sheikh Hasina's ouster weeks later and remains a defining marker of the fragility of Bangladesh's political-economic order.", 'Amnesty International / Al Jazeera', 'https://www.aljazeera.com/news/longform/2024/8/7/how-bangladeshs-gen-z-protests-brought-down-pm-sheikh-hasina'), (2024, "Moody's downgraded Bangladesh's long-term rating to B2 from B1 in November 2024 — its second consecutive downgrade in under two years — and separately cut the banking-system outlook to negative, citing rising asset risks and deteriorating economic conditions following Sheikh Hasina's ouster. The downgrade reflected concern that the political transition and banking-sector fragilities (nonperforming loans, governance gaps) would slow the reform momentum needed to stabilize the balance of payments. It signaled that political instability, not just macro fundamentals, was now a first-order driver of Bangladesh's credit trajectory.", "The Daily Star / Moody's Ratings", 'https://www.thedailystar.net/business/news/moodys-cuts-banking-outlook-negative-3845906'), (2025, "Facing a threatened 35% US tariff that industry warned would be 'extremely difficult' to absorb, Bangladesh negotiated a reciprocal trade agreement with Washington signed February 9, 2026, reducing the tariff to 19% (later 20%) with limited duty-free access for garments made from US cotton/synthetic fiber. Garment exports nonetheless kept falling through late 2025 (down 4.75% in August, 5.66% in September, 8.39% in October) amid the tariff uncertainty, threatening an industry that supplies over 80% of export earnings and employs about four million workers. The episode illustrates Bangladesh's acute exposure to a single-sector, single-market export model just as it seeks to graduate from LDC status.", 'Al Jazeera / bne IntelliNews', 'https://www.aljazeera.com/economy/2026/2/9/us-and-bangladesh-set-trade-deal-with-tariffs-at-19-percent'), (2025, "On November 17, 2025, Bangladesh's International Crimes Tribunal convicted ousted PM Sheikh Hasina in absentia of crimes against humanity for orchestrating the lethal crackdown on the 2024 student uprising (which killed roughly 1,400 people), sentencing her and former home minister Asaduzzaman Khan Kamal to death; Hasina, in exile in India, denounced the tribunal as illegitimate. The verdict deepens the rupture between the interim government and India (which continues to shelter Hasina) and raises the political stakes ahead of Bangladesh's scheduled 2026 elections. For sovereign risk purposes, it signals continued institutional volatility and unresolved questions about accountability, extradition, and the durability of the interim order.", 'CNN / Human Rights Watch', 'https://www.cnn.com/2025/11/17/asia/bangladesh-sheikh-hasina-verdict-intl-hnk')],
    'BHR': [(2011, "Beginning in February 2011, tens of thousands of Bahraini protesters occupied Manama's Pearl Roundabout demanding constitutional reform amid the broader Arab Spring. On March 14, 2011, roughly 1,000 Saudi troops and 500 UAE troops entered Bahrain as the core of the Gulf Cooperation Council's Peninsula Shield Force at the invitation of King Hamad, and the government declared martial law the next day; the crackdown left over 100 Bahrainis dead and the Pearl Roundabout monument itself was demolished. The uprising and its suppression cemented Bahrain's political dependence on Saudi and UAE security backing, a dynamic that has since extended into the fiscal domain through repeated GCC bailout packages.", 'Middle East Monitor', 'https://www.middleeastmonitor.com/20210214-remembering-the-bahraini-uprising/'), (2018, "Facing a deepening fiscal crisis after years of low oil prices and elevated debt, Bahrain received a $10 billion economic aid package from Saudi Arabia, the UAE, and Kuwait, signed in Manama on October 4, 2018, structured as a long-term, interest-free loan disbursed in stages. The package, worth roughly a quarter of Bahrain's GDP, was conditioned on a new Fiscal Balance Program targeting elimination of the budget deficit by 2022 through measures including VAT introduction and subsidy reform. It marked the clearest instance of Bahrain's economic dependence on wealthier Gulf patrons, a pattern that recurred through subsequent VAT increases and credit downgrades.", 'Forbes', 'https://www.forbes.com/sites/dominicdudley/2018/10/05/bahrain-reset-economy-10b-aid-package/'), (2020, "Bahrain signed the Abraham Accords with Israel on September 15, 2020, becoming (alongside the UAE) one of the first two Gulf states to formally normalize relations, establishing diplomatic ties, embassy exchanges, and cooperation frameworks in trade, tourism, and security. The move, closely coordinated with Saudi and UAE regional diplomacy, reflected Manama's alignment with Abu Dhabi and Riyadh's foreign-policy priorities given its financial dependence on both. Bilateral trade and investment ties with Israel have since grown modestly, though far more slowly than the UAE's.", 'AJC', 'https://www.ajc.org/abrahamaccordsexplained'), (2022, "Bahrain doubled its value-added tax from 5% to 10% effective January 1, 2022, one of the fiscal-consolidation commitments tied to the 2018 GCC support package, a move S&P estimated could raise VAT receipts to about 3% of GDP from 1.7%. Despite the increase, Bahrain's public debt had already climbed to about 133% of GDP by 2021 (from 102% in 2019) according to the IMF, reflecting the limits of tax measures alone against a persistently oil-price-sensitive budget. The VAT hike illustrated the ongoing tension between Gulf-neighbor-imposed austerity conditionality and Bahrain's structurally narrow non-oil revenue base.", 'Gulf News', 'https://gulfnews.com/business/bahrain-increases-vat-from-5-to-10-1.1638968042498'), (2024, "S&P downgraded Bahrain's long-term sovereign credit rating to 'B' from 'B+' citing challenging fiscal and debt dynamics, joining Fitch in classifying Bahrain's debt firmly in junk territory; Fitch separately projected Bahrain's deficit would still be 9.2% of GDP by 2027 with debt-to-GDP surpassing 150%. The downgrades reflected persistent structural fiscal weakness despite the 2018 GCC aid package and 2022 VAT hike, underscoring that Bahrain's creditworthiness remains the weakest of the six GCC states by a wide margin.", 'Investing.com / S&P Global', 'https://www.investing.com/news/world-news/bahrains-credit-rating-lowered-to-b-at-sp-on-high-debt-and-fiscal-deficits-93CH-4373781'), (2026, "Fitch downgraded Bahrain again to 'B' from 'B+' on February 23, 2026, estimating government debt had reached 146.8% of GDP by end-2025 -- the second-highest of any Fitch-rated sovereign globally -- and projecting a further climb to 153.3% by end-2027. The downgrade came despite a fiscal consolidation package Bahrain launched in December 2025 that raised fuel and energy prices, utility tariffs, and government service fees while increasing required dividends from state-owned enterprises. The repeated downgrades despite Gulf-neighbor support highlight Bahrain's position as the GCC's most fiscally fragile sovereign, structurally reliant on continued Saudi, UAE, and Kuwaiti backing to avoid a deeper crisis.", 'Al Arabiya / Fitch Ratings', 'https://english.alarabiya.net/business/economy/2026/02/23/fitch-downgrades-bahrain-to-b-on-rising-debt-wider-deficits'), (2016, "Fitch downgraded Bahrain's sovereign rating to junk status (BB+ with stable outlook) in 2016 as the oil-price collapse sharply widened the fiscal deficit, with S&P and Moody's also holding sub-investment-grade-adjacent ratings (BB and Ba2) at the time. This was the first of a multi-year string of downgrades that would culminate in the 2018 GCC bailout. It marked Bahrain's transition into the weakest credit tier among Gulf sovereigns, a position it has not since escaped.", 'Gulf House', 'https://gulfhouse.org/en/posts/9930/'), (2020, "In August 2020 Fitch cut Bahrain's rating to B+ -- four notches below investment grade and its first downgrade in over two years -- as collapsing oil prices combined with the Covid-19 shock to strain public finances further. The downgrade came despite the 2018 GCC fiscal-support package, showing that Gulf aid alone had not resolved Bahrain's underlying structural deficit. It reinforced Bahrain's status as the most fiscally fragile GCC sovereign heading into the pandemic recovery period.", 'Business Standard', 'https://www.business-standard.com/article/international/bahrain-gets-first-rating-downgrade-in-2-years-says-need-more-gulf-aid-120081700030_1.html'), (2020, "On 11 November 2020 King Hamad appointed Crown Prince Salman bin Hamad Al Khalifa as Prime Minister, consolidating economic policymaking authority under the architect of Bahrain's 'Economic Vision 2030' diversification strategy. The move streamlined decision-making around fiscal reform and investment promotion at a moment of acute fiscal stress. It set the governance framework under which subsequent VAT hikes and 2025 fuel-subsidy reforms were pushed through.", 'Crown Prince of Bahrain (official site)', 'https://www.crownprince.bh/en/about/'), (2022, "Bahrain's economy grew 4.9% in 2022, with non-hydrocarbon GDP up 6.2%, as high oil prices and continued fiscal-reform momentum (later reflected in the IMF's July 2023 Article IV conclusion) improved fiscal and external balances. The improvement was cyclical rather than structural, riding elevated global energy prices rather than a change in Bahrain's oil-dependent revenue base. This oil-price sensitivity remains a core vulnerability distinguishing Bahrain from better-diversified Gulf peers.", 'IMF', 'https://www.imf.org/en/News/Articles/2022/06/30/pr22243-imf-executive-board-concludes-2022-article-iv-consultation-with-the-kingdom-of-bahrain'), (2025, "In November 2025 Bahrain unveiled its largest financial and tax reform package in its history, liberalizing fuel prices by linking them to global markets for the first time, aimed at addressing public debt that had reached roughly $50bn (109% of GDP). The IMF simultaneously urged Bahrain to adopt a general corporate income tax and further reduce energy subsidies. This marks the most significant subsidy-reform attempt in Bahrain's modern fiscal history and is a critical test of the government's capacity to close its structural deficit without further Gulf bailouts.", 'The Media Line', 'https://themedialine.org/life-lines/bahrain-unveils-massive-reform-package-to-tackle-50-billion-debt-crisis/'), (2025, 'On 29 December 2025 Bahrain approved a further package of fiscal reforms -- including corporate income tax adjustments and public spending cuts -- as gross government debt was projected to reach roughly BHD25.4bn ($68bn), with debt-to-GDP at 131% in 2025 heading toward a projected 140% by 2028. Oil still accounts for over 60% of government revenue, and the IMF estimated Bahrain needs roughly $130/bbl to balance its budget against actual prices near $70/bbl. This persistent structural gap is the central reason Bahrain remains the weakest-rated sovereign in this dataset.', 'AGBI', 'https://www.agbi.com/economy/2025/12/bahrain-approves-fiscal-reforms-to-rein-in-debt/')],
    'BTN': [(2010, "In 2010 Bhutan and India agreed to jointly develop the 1,020 MW Punatsangchhu-II hydropower project for an initial budget of Nu 37 billion, financed 30% by Indian grant and 70% by Indian loan at 10% annual interest, targeting completion by 2017. Alongside the earlier, larger Punatsangchhu-I project (whose costs later nearly tripled from about Nu 35 billion to roughly Nu 93-100 billion amid geological slope-stability accidents), these India-financed mega-dams became the structural core of both Bhutan's growth model and its debt burden. Punatsangchhu-II finally neared completion around October 2024 -- nearly seven years behind schedule.", 'Scroll.in / The Wire', 'https://scroll.in/article/1045362/indias-renewable-energy-ambitions-has-ended-up-wedding-bhutan-to-hydropower-despite-rising-costs'), (2023, "King Jigme Khesar Namgyel Wangchuck announced the 'Gelephu Mindfulness City' project on National Day, December 17, 2023 -- a planned special administrative region spanning over 2,500 square kilometers on the Indian border, intended as an economic corridor linking South Asia and Southeast Asia around energy, connectivity and skills development. Bhutan halved its daily tourist levy to $100 the same year to revive arrivals, and by 2025 the government had launched its first-ever domestic bond offering to help fund related infrastructure including an airport and high-speed rail links to India. It represents Bhutan's most ambitious attempt yet to diversify beyond hydropower and tourism.", 'TIME / Business Standard (Bangladesh)', 'https://time.com/7204652/gelephu-mindfulness-city-bhutan-economy/'), (2024, "The IMF concluded its 2024 Article IV consultation with Bhutan on September 9, 2024, noting public debt of roughly Nu 293 billion (about 109.8% of GDP) as of March 2024, with hydropower loans from India -- now roughly 60-65% of external debt -- the dominant driver. Hydropower contributes about 63% of Bhutan's total exports, almost all to India, which also takes over 80% of Bhutan's total imports and exports combined; the Ngultrum's peg to the Indian rupee further binds the two economies. GDP growth of 4.9% in FY2023/24 was driven by services, tourism, finance and real estate rather than hydropower alone that particular year.", 'IMF', 'https://imf.org/en/News/Articles/2024/09/19/pr-24336-bhutan-imf-concludes-2024-article-iv-consultation'), (2026, "The IMF concluded its 2025 Article IV consultation with Bhutan on January 26-27, 2026, with total public debt at Nu 303.9 billion -- about 100.5% of GDP as of June 2025, still overwhelmingly hydropower-related. The consultation was a surveillance exercise rather than a lending program, consistent with Bhutan never having entered a full IMF-supported program. The persistently triple-digit debt-to-GDP ratio, almost entirely to a single creditor (India) for a single sector, remains the central structural risk in Bhutan's sovereign profile even amid otherwise solid growth.", 'IMF', 'https://www.imf.org/en/news/articles/2026/01/26/pr-2619-bhutan-imf-concludes-2025-article-iv-consultation'), (2020, "Bhutan sealed its international borders on March 22, 2020 after detecting its first COVID-19 case, halting tourism — which employed roughly 50,000 people (16% of the workforce) and supported livelihoods for about 150,000 people in a country of 750,000. GDP growth, which had been 4.4% in 2019, turned negative (around -2.1%) in 2020 as the sector most exposed to international travel collapsed almost overnight. The episode underscores Bhutan's acute economic concentration risk in tourism and its vulnerability to any external shock disrupting cross-border travel.", 'Asia Society / ORF', 'https://asiasociety.org/magazine/article/how-bhutan-tamed-covid-19'), (2021, "Bhutan's youth unemployment rate spiked to 28.6% in 2022 amid the pandemic-driven economic slowdown and returning overseas workers, then, as borders reopened, plunged to 15.9% in 2023 before rising again to 17.93% in 2024 — but the underlying story was a surge in emigration, with 9,352 civil servants resigning between 2021-2023 and monthly departures from Paro airport jumping from under 500 pre-pandemic to over 5,000 by early 2023. Over half of Bhutanese migrants hold university degrees (versus about 7% of the domestic workforce), and Bhutan's diaspora in Australia alone doubled from 12,424 (2020) to 25,363 (2024). This brain-drain trend represents one of the most significant long-term structural risks to Bhutan's human capital base and its ability to staff its own civil service and skilled professions.", 'ORF / Scroll.in', 'https://scroll.in/article/1064266/why-the-exodus-from-bhutan-of-young-people-and-qualified-workers-is-a-worry-for-india'), (2022, "When Bhutan reopened its borders to tourists in September 2022 after the pandemic closure, it more than doubled its Sustainable Development Fee to $200 per person per night as part of its 'high-value, low-volume' tourism strategy — but with arrivals lagging (about 130,000 in 2023 versus 315,599 in pre-pandemic 2019), the government halved the fee to $100 per night from September 1, 2023 to attract visitors. The fee reversal illustrates the tension in Bhutan's tourism model between maximizing per-visitor revenue for its hydropower-dependent, low-diversification economy and maintaining the volume needed to sustain related employment. Tourism-fee policy remains a key lever and a bellwether of Bhutan's fiscal balancing act.", 'CNBC / GeoEx', 'https://www.cnbc.com/2024/05/21/bhutan-may-revise-its-sustainable-development-fee-if-travel-picks-up.html'), (2022, "Bhutan's Parliament enacted the Gyalsung Act in November 2022, establishing a mandatory one-year national service program for all citizens turning 18, combining military-style training with vocational education in agriculture, entrepreneurship, and technology; the program formally launched on September 5, 2024. Framed as addressing both youth unemployment and national resilience, Gyalsung represents a significant fiscal commitment (new training academies) at a time of severe brain-drain pressure and constrained public finances. Its success or failure will be a meaningful indicator of Bhutan's capacity to retain and productively employ its young workforce.", 'BhutanWiki / Business Bhutan', 'https://gyalsung.bt/gyalsung-bhutan/'), (2023, "Bhutan graduated from the UN's Least Developed Country category on December 13, 2023, becoming only the seventh country ever to do so, after meeting income and Human Assets Index thresholds since 2015-2018 with a five-year preparatory period. Graduation ends Bhutan's eligibility for LDC-specific trade preferences and concessional financing terms, even as the UN extended some support measures through 2028 under its 13th National Development Plan. The transition is a genuine development milestone but also removes a longstanding cushion just as Bhutan grapples with youth emigration and high hydropower debt — a trade-off central to its post-graduation risk profile.", 'UN DESA / UNCDF', 'https://policy.desa.un.org/themes/cdp-news-and-events/news/bhutan-graduates-from-ldc-status?language_content_entity=en'), (2024, "The People's Democratic Party won 30 of 47 seats in Bhutan's January 9, 2024 general election, returning Tshering Tobgay to the premiership for a second term (he previously led the government 2013-2018), sworn in January 28, 2024, against a backdrop of acute economic pressure from youth unemployment and emigration. The relatively smooth, competitive democratic transition (Bhutan's fourth general election since its 2008 shift to constitutional monarchy) signals continued political stability even as the new government confronts urgent economic diversification challenges. Tobgay's return is notable given his prior experience and explicit campaign focus on reversing the emigration wave.", 'The Diplomat / Al Jazeera', 'https://thediplomat.com/2024/01/peoples-democratic-party-wins-elections-in-bhutan/'), (2022, "Bhutan's Punatsangchhu-I hydropower project, originally budgeted at Nu 35-40 billion (~$570 million) for completion by 2016, has ballooned to nearly Nu 93-100 billion (~$1.44 billion) after repeated geological setbacks and slope failures, with no confirmed commissioning date even after 17 years of construction; by end-2022, hydropower debt (60% loan-financed by India at 10% interest) accounted for nearly 70% of Bhutan's external debt and over 80% of GDP. The prolonged delay means Bhutan continues servicing interest without corresponding generation revenue, a textbook illustration of project-execution risk translating directly into sovereign debt sustainability pressure. It remains one of the single largest contingent liabilities shaping Bhutan's credit profile.", 'The Wire / Scroll.in / dialogue.earth', 'https://dialogue.earth/en/energy/bhutan-failed-hydropower-goal-and-geopolitics-of-energy/'), (2024, "Since 2019, Bhutan's sovereign wealth fund Druk Holding and Investments has mined Bitcoin using surplus hydropower, building holdings that peaked around 13,000 BTC in late 2024 (a substantial share of GDP) as a foreign-currency liquidity buffer; by 2025-2026 Bhutan had reportedly sold roughly 70% of that stash (down to about 3,954 BTC, ~$280 million) amid a broader crypto downturn, though DHI disputed the sell-off reports, and in December 2025 the government pledged up to 10,000 BTC toward funding the Gelephu Mindfulness City project. This unusual sovereign strategy of monetizing surplus renewable energy via crypto mining is a genuinely novel and volatile addition to Bhutan's reserve-management risk profile, exposing the state balance sheet to Bitcoin price swings.", 'CoinDesk', 'https://www.coindesk.com/markets/2025/07/14/bhutan-quietly-sells-59m-in-bitcoin-as-btc-hits-123k-still-holds-over-14b-in-reserves'), (2025, "After 15 years of delay from its original 2010 financing agreement, Bhutan's Punatsangchhu-II hydropower project finally began commissioning in phases from December 2024 (Units 1-2) through August 27, 2025 (final Unit 6), progressively adding generation and export revenue to India. The phased revenue ramp-up (Nu 12 billion or less expected for 2025 given partial-year operation) offers Bhutan much-needed fiscal relief, since hydropower taxes and dividends already constitute over 40% of national revenue. Full-year operation from 2026 onward will be an important data point for whether Bhutan's hydropower-led growth model can finally start reducing its debt burden rather than adding to it.", 'The Bhutanese', 'https://thebhutanese.bt/bhutan-to-export-p-ii-power-to-india-from-20th-september-2025/')],
    'DZA': [(2019, "On February 22, 2019, mass protests (the 'Hirak') erupted after ailing President Abdelaziz Bouteflika announced a bid for a fifth term; the leaderless, largely peaceful movement grew to encompass all 48 provinces within weeks. Under army pressure, Bouteflika resigned on April 2, 2019, ending a 20-year rule and opening a prolonged period of political uncertainty that delayed foreign investment decisions and any serious diversification of Algeria's hydrocarbon-dependent economy. The Hirak's demands for an end to military-backed rule remain unresolved and continue to weigh on Algeria's governance-risk score.", 'France 24 / Al Jazeera', 'https://www.france24.com/en/live-news/20210824-algeria-severs-diplomatic-ties-with-morocco-citing-hostile-actions'), (2019, "Algeria passed a new hydrocarbons law in late 2019 improving fiscal terms and contract flexibility to attract international oil and gas majors back to a sector that accounts for roughly 98% of exports and about 58% of government revenue via state firm Sonatrach. The reform was widely read as an admission that Algeria's aging fields needed foreign technical investment to sustain output, but the law retained the 51/49 domestic-ownership requirement for 'strategic sectors' including energy, limiting the scale of foreign capital it could actually attract.", 'Lexology / trade.gov', 'https://www.trade.gov/country-commercial-guides/algeria-oil-and-gas-hydrocarbons'), (2021, "On August 24, 2021, Algeria severed diplomatic relations with Morocco, citing 'hostile actions' including alleged Moroccan support for Kabyle separatists and normalization with Israel, deepening a Western Sahara-rooted rivalry dating to a border closure in 1994. Algeria also cut gas supply through the Maghreb-Europe pipeline that transited Morocco to Spain, redirecting flows to the Medgaz pipeline instead. The rupture illustrates how the Algeria-Morocco rivalry over Western Sahara routinely spills into energy infrastructure and regional trade routes, a persistent tail risk for both economies.", 'Al Jazeera', 'https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2021/8/24/algeria-cuts-diplomatic-ties-with-morocco'), (2022, "Russia's invasion of Ukraine triggered a European scramble for alternative gas supply, and in April-May 2022 Italy's Eni and Algeria's Sonatrach agreed to raise Transmed pipeline volumes to Italy toward 9 billion cubic meters/year by 2024-25; Italy now absorbs over 60% of Algerian gas exports and has overtaken Russia as Italy's top gas supplier. The windfall from higher gas prices and volumes boosted Algeria's foreign reserves (reaching an estimated $71bn in 2024) and gave Algiers unusual fiscal space, but it also increased Algeria's economic exposure to European energy-security decisions and gas-price volatility.", 'Energy Connects / AGBI', 'https://www.energyconnects.com/news/gas-lng/2022/may/eni-and-sonatrach-sign-deal-to-boost-algeria-gas-exports-to-italy/'), (2024, "President Abdelmadjid Tebboune was re-elected in September 2024 for a second term with army backing, following roughly $114 billion in 2024 public spending — including a 47% civil-service wage increase — aimed at shoring up social support ahead of the vote. The pre-election spending surge pushed Algeria's budget deficit from about $45bn in 2024 toward a projected $62bn in 2025, eroding the fiscal buffers built up during the 2022 gas windfall and setting up the fiscal-consolidation debate that now dominates IMF Article IV consultations.", 'Atalayar / TIMEP', 'https://www.atalayar.com/en/articulo/economy-and-business/algeria-approves-budget-with-largest-deficit-in-its-history/20241014132446206269.html'), (2024, "In July 2024, French President Emmanuel Macron sent a letter to Morocco's King Mohammed VI backing Rabat's Western Sahara autonomy plan as the 'only basis' for resolution, reversing decades of French neutrality; Algeria immediately withdrew its ambassador from Paris and cancelled a planned Tebboune state visit. Weeks later, Franco-Algerian writer Boualem Sansal was arrested in Algiers (November 2024) and later sentenced to five years for comments on colonial-era borders, triggering the 'most serious' France-Algeria diplomatic crisis since independence, including mutual expulsions of diplomats and, by August 2025, French visa restrictions on Algerians. Sansal was eventually pardoned in November 2025 following German mediation, but the underlying rupture — tied directly to the Western Sahara dispute — has damaged trade and migration cooperation with Algeria's largest historical European partner.", 'Middle East Eye / Military Africa', 'https://www.middleeasteye.net/news/france-algeria-most-serious-diplomatic-crisis-end-colonial-rule'), (2025, "China overtook France as Algeria's largest source of imports, with Chinese exports to Algeria reaching $11.68bn in 2024 and Sino-Maghreb trade hitting $31.5bn, underpinned by Belt and Road projects including the completed 1,216km East-West highway and a new $7bn Bled El Hadba phosphate megaproject launched in 2024. This reflects Algeria's deliberate diversification of infrastructure and non-hydrocarbon trade partners away from France amid the parallel political rupture with Paris, positioning China as an increasingly central economic partner alongside Italy (energy) in Algeria's external relations.", 'Silk Road Briefing / SASAC', 'http://en.sasac.gov.cn/2024/07/25/c_17553.htm'), (2026, "The IMF's 2026 Article IV mission (completed July 2026) found growth at an estimated 3.9% in 2025 but flagged that Algeria's fiscal deficit, while narrowing to 10.5% of GDP in 2025 from a projected 11.5%, still pushed public debt to 52.1% of GDP amid large financing needs and falling hydrocarbon prices. Because Algeria maintains a 'no-borrowing' policy and has not sought IMF financing, its Article IV reviews are adopted on a lapse-of-time basis with no program attached — meaning Algeria's risk buffer is its own hydrocarbon-funded reserves rather than any external backstop, a vulnerability if oil and gas prices fall further.", 'IMF', 'https://www.imf.org/en/news/articles/2026/07/06/pr26236-algeria-imf-staff-completes-2026-article-iv-mission'), (2022, "Algeria's Investment Law No. 22-18 (July 2022) abolished the long-standing '51/49' rule that had required Algerian nationals to hold majority ownership of new foreign-invested companies since 2009, opening most sectors to 100% foreign ownership (a handful of 'strategic' sectors excepted). The reform was designed to attract non-hydrocarbon FDI as Algeria sought to reduce dependence on oil and gas exports, though implementation and bureaucratic friction have continued to temper investor uptake.", 'Norton Rose Fulbright / Gide', 'https://www.gide.com/en/news-insights/algeria-to-revise-49-51-rule-to-boost-foreign-investment/'), (2024, "Algeria applied to join the BRICS New Development Bank with a pledged $1.5bn contribution in 2023, and formally became the NDB's ninth member in August 2024, even as its bid for full BRICS bloc membership remained pending. The move reflected Algiers' strategy of diversifying financial and diplomatic partnerships toward China and non-Western institutions, though it has not materially altered Algeria's reliance on hydrocarbon export revenue or eased its widening fiscal gap.", 'VOA News / Rio Times', 'https://www.voanews.com/a/algeria-joins-brics-new-development-bank/7767238.html'), (2025, "The IMF's 2025 Article IV consultation flagged Algeria's fiscal deficit at roughly 11.5% of GDP for 2025 (rising toward 12.4% in 2026) -- the largest of any MENA oil exporter -- and assessed the overall risk of sovereign stress as 'high,' warning that depletion of the Revenue Regulation Fund (the oil-surplus buffer exhausted in 2024) now leaves deficit financing dependent almost entirely on domestic debt issuance. This raises medium-term risks of crowding out private credit or resorting to unconventional monetary financing if oil prices soften.", 'IMF Article IV Consultation 2025', 'https://www.imf.org/en/news/articles/2025/09/18/pr-25305-algeria-imf-executive-board-concludes-2025-article-iv-consultation'), (2025, "In April 2025, Algeria shot down a Malian military drone near the border town of Tin Zaouatine, prompting Mali, Burkina Faso and Niger's ruling juntas (the Alliance of Sahel States) to jointly recall their ambassadors from Algiers; Algeria reciprocated and closed its airspace to Malian aircraft. The rupture with its southern neighbors -- following Algeria's earlier mediating role in Mali's Tuareg conflict -- added a new source of regional security and border risk alongside the pre-existing standoff with Morocco and France.", 'France 24 / Al Jazeera', 'https://www.france24.com/en/africa/20250407-sahel-alliance-recalls-ambassadors-from-algiers-over-downed-drone')],
    'EGY': [(2011, "The 18-day uprising that began January 25, 2011 forced President Hosni Mubarak to resign on February 11 after three decades in power, driven by grievances over unemployment, food-price inflation, and inequality. The transition period devastated tourism (hotel occupancy roughly halved through 2013) and slowed GDP growth from a pre-revolution trend near 7% to below 5%, while capital flight and a collapsing investment climate drained foreign reserves. The episode established the template of political-instability-driven currency and reserve crises that has recurred in Egypt's risk profile ever since.", 'Britannica', 'https://www.britannica.com/event/Egypt-Uprising-of-2011'), (2013, 'In July 2013, army chief Abdel Fattah el-Sisi led the military ouster of elected President Mohamed Morsi following mass protests against Muslim Brotherhood rule. Saudi Arabia, the UAE, and Kuwait moved immediately to backstop the new government, providing roughly $12 billion in grants and central-bank deposits within months and around $30 billion cumulatively by 2016. This cemented a pattern — repeated in 2016, 2022, and 2024 — in which Gulf sovereign deposits act as first-line balance-of-payments support ahead of, or alongside, IMF financing.', 'The Washington Institute', 'https://www.washingtoninstitute.org/policy-analysis/aid-security-gulf-egypt-dynamic-supporting-egypts-economy'), (2016, "Facing a severe dollar shortage and a thriving black market, Egypt floated the pound and secured a three-year, $12 billion IMF Extended Fund Facility approved November 11, 2016, tied to subsidy cuts and a VAT introduction. The float roughly halved the pound's value overnight and pushed inflation above 30%, but it unlocked IMF and bilateral financing and became the reference template for the 2022-2024 crisis-and-devaluation cycle. It also demonstrated the recurring political cost of subsidy removal that shapes Egypt's reform pace to this day.", 'IMF', 'https://www.imf.org/en/news/articles/2016/11/11/pr16501-egypt-executive-board-approves-12-billion-extended-arrangement'), (2024, "On February 23, 2024 the UAE's ADQ sovereign fund agreed a $35 billion Ras El-Hekma coastal development deal, converting roughly $11 billion of frozen UAE central-bank deposits into equity — the largest single FDI transaction in Egyptian history. Days later, on March 6, Egypt floated its currency again (the pound fell toward EGP 50/$1), hiked rates 600bps, and the IMF expanded its EFF from $3bn to $8bn, unlocking an immediate $820 million tranche. The near-simultaneous Gulf-and-IMF rescue illustrates how Gulf capital now acts as first-responder financing that de-risks IMF board approval.", 'Reuters via KSAT', 'https://www.ksat.com/business/2024/03/06/egypt-floats-its-currency-and-agrees-with-the-imf-to-increase-a-bailout-loan-to-8-billion/'), (2024, "Following Ras El-Hekma, Qatar's Qatari Diar signed a roughly $29.7 billion investment framework and long-term Gulf deposits at the Central Bank of Egypt held steady at $9.3bn (Saudi $5.3bn, Kuwait $4.0bn) as of mid-2024, while Egypt's external debt fell to $152.9bn in June 2024 (down $11.8bn y/y) largely due to the UAE deposit-to-equity conversion. These mega-deals met IMF asset-sale and FX-inflow benchmarks and materially rebuilt reserves after the 2022-2023 crisis, but they also increased Egypt's reliance on episodic Gulf capital injections rather than structural current-account improvement.", 'Daily News Egypt', 'https://www.dailynewsegypt.com/2025/08/23/cbe-reports-developments-in-egypts-external-debt-in-2024/'), (2025, "Houthi attacks on Red Sea shipping, which escalated from late 2023, cut Suez Canal transit revenue by roughly half year-on-year by early 2026 as shipping lines rerouted around the Cape of Good Hope — from about $800 million/month at peak to roughly $380 million/month. This widened Egypt's current account deficit (around 3.5% of GDP in 2025) and forced a harder policy choice between further pound depreciation and reserve drawdown, showing how a geopolitically exogenous shock (Gaza/Red Sea war) can undo IMF-program gains achieved through domestic reform.", 'Al Majalla / The National', 'https://en.majalla.com/node/329123/business-economy/egypt%E2%80%99s-economy-2025-tests-and-2026-bets'), (2025, "The Grand Egyptian Museum, a roughly $1 billion, two-decades-in-the-making project near the Giza Pyramids, fully opened November 1, 2025, intended as a flagship driver of tourism-sector growth and foreign-currency earnings. Its launch came as remittances from Egyptians abroad — boosted by the 2024 devaluation narrowing the parallel-market gap — surged 40.5% in 2025 to about $41.5 billion, becoming, alongside tourism, one of Egypt's most important sources of hard currency. Both trends underscore Egypt's structural dependence on non-tradeable services (tourism, remittances, canal fees) rather than goods exports for external financing.", 'Euronews / CBE', 'https://euronews.com/culture/2025/11/01/20-years-in-the-making-the-grand-egyptian-museum-officially-opens'), (2026, "On July 30, 2026 the IMF Executive Board completed Egypt's seventh EFF review and second RSF review, unlocking about $1.8 billion (bringing total disbursements under the $8bn arrangement to roughly $7.3bn) even as the economy absorbed spillovers from the Iran-Israel war. The Central Bank of Egypt raised its FY2025/26 growth forecast to about 4.6-5%, citing exchange-rate flexibility and fiscal restraint as having cushioned regional shocks. The review's completion despite the Red Sea/regional disruption signals the IMF's continued willingness to keep Egypt's financing bridge open, but the program remains contingent on canal revenue and regional stability that Egypt does not control.", 'IMF', 'https://www.imf.org/en/news/articles/2026/07/30/pr26271-egypt-imf-completes-the-7th-review-under-eff-and-2nd-review-under-the-rsf'), (2023, 'Annual urban inflation hit a record near-40% peak (September 2023, ~38%), driven by a widening gap between the official and black-market pound rates and years of import-dependent food/fuel costs; the crisis eroded real wages and public tolerance for austerity, and directly forced the government back to the IMF for an expanded program. It underscored how currency instability, not just the budget deficit, is the core transmission channel for Egyptian sovereign risk.', 'VOA News', 'https://www.voanews.com/a/egypt-inflation-hits-record-high-of-nearly-40-/7262060.html'), (2024, 'On March 6, 2024, Egypt floated its pound a second time (following the 2016 float), letting it fall to roughly EGP 50/USD, while the IMF simultaneously more than doubled its Extended Fund Facility from $3bn to $8bn; the move was paired with a steep interest-rate hike and followed the record-breaking Ras El-Hekma investment inflow. It marked the clearest signal yet that Cairo would accept market-driven FX pricing to unlock external financing, materially easing the acute dollar shortage that had paralyzed importers.', 'The National / Al Jazeera', 'https://www.thenationalnews.com/business/economy/2024/03/06/egypt-finalises-8-billion-deal-with-imf-after-currency-hits-record-low/'), (2024, 'Egypt began phasing out fuel and electricity subsidies as an explicit IMF program condition, with repeated 2024 fuel-price hikes (up to ~15% each round) and electricity tariff increases of up to 50% for households and businesses; the government targeted full fuel subsidy removal by end-2025. The reform saved billions of pounds in fiscal space but added to inflationary and social pressure, making the pace of subsidy withdrawal a recurring flashpoint for both IMF review timing and domestic political risk.', 'Ahram Online / The National', 'https://english.ahram.org.eg/News/524463.aspx'), (2024, "Fitch upgraded Egypt's sovereign rating to 'B' from 'B-' with a stable outlook in November 2024 -- its first positive rating action on Egypt since 2019 -- citing the currency float, fiscal consolidation, and the Ras El-Hekma-driven boost to reserves; S&P followed with its own upgrade before trimming the outlook back to stable in April 2025 on debt-servicing cost concerns. The back-and-forth illustrates how conditional and reversible ratings agencies still view Egypt's reform credibility, with any backsliding on FX flexibility flagged as the key downgrade trigger.", 'S&P Global Ratings / AllAfrica', 'https://allafrica.com/stories/202411040474.html'), (2026, "The Suez Canal Economic Zone (SCZone) reported roughly $7bn in new investment commitments in a single fiscal year (part of a cumulative ~$16bn drawn from 28 countries over nearly four years), including a Chinese consortium's planned $2bn industrial complex, as Cairo pushed to diversify away from canal-transit revenue lost to Red Sea Houthi attacks. The inflows partly offset the Suez toll collapse but remain contingent on continued reform credibility and global manufacturing relocation trends.", 'AGBI / Daily News Egypt', 'https://www.agbi.com/logistics/2026/08/egypts-suez-canal-economic-zone-draws-7bn-from-investors/')],
    'IND': [(2016, "On November 8, 2016 the Modi government abruptly demonetized all Rs500 and Rs1,000 notes -- about 86% of currency in circulation -- to combat black money and counterfeit currency. India's GDP growth slowed from 8% in FY2015-16 to 7.1% in FY2016-17, with quarterly growth dropping roughly 2 percentage points in the immediate aftermath before recovering by mid-2017 as households shifted to digital payments. It remains a reference case for the real economic cost of sudden, large-scale monetary interventions in a cash-heavy economy.", 'NBER / Economic Survey', 'https://www.nber.org/digest/feb19/indias-demonetization-reduced-employment-and-economic-activity'), (2020, "A deadly clash between Indian and Chinese troops in the Galwan Valley of Eastern Ladakh on June 15, 2020 killed 20 Indian soldiers amid an unresolved Line of Actual Control dispute, marking the worst India-China border violence in decades. India's near-immediate economic response included banning dozens of Chinese mobile apps (including TikTok) and tightening screening of Chinese investment, moves that persist in modified form today. The episode hardened India's approach to Chinese economic engagement even as bilateral trade in goods continued to grow.", 'Carnegie Endowment', 'https://carnegieendowment.org/research/2021/03/the-road-from-galwan-the-future-of-india-china-relations'), (2025, "A terror attack on tourists in Pahalgam, Indian-administered Kashmir on April 22, 2025 killed 26 civilians; India blamed Pakistan-based militant groups. India responded on May 7, 2025 with 'Operation Sindoor,' missile and drone strikes on alleged militant infrastructure inside Pakistan, triggering four days of cross-border strikes before a ceasefire. Indian equity markets (Sensex/Nifty) dipped only briefly and closed higher within the same session, while defense stocks like Hindustan Aeronautics rallied, reflecting investor confidence that the conflict would stay contained -- a marked contrast to the much larger hit to Pakistani markets.", 'Stimson Center / Business Standard', 'https://www.stimson.org/2025/four-days-in-may-the-india-pakistan-crisis-of-2025/'), (2025, "The US imposed tariffs on Indian goods reaching as high as 25% in 2025 -- described as the highest imposed on any Asia-Pacific economy -- pressuring India's roughly 4%-of-GDP goods-export exposure to US final demand. Despite the tariff overhang, India's GDP grew 8.2% year-on-year in the September 2025 quarter, and the IMF still projected growth above 6% for the country even amid global trade tension. The tariff dispute nonetheless became a defining 2025-2026 risk factor for India's external accounts.", 'CNBC / News on Air', 'https://www.cnbc.com/2025/11/28/india-economy-gdp-grows-september-quarter-tariffs-bite-trade-us-modi.html'), (2026, "A US-India trade deal announced in early February 2026 reduced the 'reciprocal' tariff rate on Indian goods from 25% to 18%, bringing India roughly into line with regional peers in the 15-19% range. Goldman Sachs projected an incremental 0.2 percentage-point annualized GDP boost from the deal and forecast 6.9% growth for India in 2026. The agreement resolved, for now, the single largest external overhang on India's growth and export outlook that had built up through 2025.", 'Goldman Sachs', 'https://www.goldmansachs.com/insights/articles/the-outlook-for-indias-economy-in-2026-amid-new-us-tradedeal'), (2017, "India rolled out the Goods and Services Tax on July 1, 2017, unifying a patchwork of central and state indirect taxes (VAT, excise, sales taxes across 29 states) into a single national tax regime with CGST/SGST/IGST components, projected by the government to add roughly 2% to GDP. While initial implementation caused compliance disruption for small businesses and informal-sector traders, the reform over time expanded the formal tax base and improved the ease of doing business. GST remains one of modern India's most consequential structural reforms and a reference point for assessing the government's capacity to execute complex fiscal overhauls.", 'IMF Finance & Development / PIB', 'https://www.imf.org/en/publications/fandd/issues/2018/06/impact-of-indias-new-gst-tax-on-the-economy-trenches'), (2019, "A Jaish-e-Mohammed suicide bombing killed 40 CRPF personnel at Pulwama on February 14, 2019; India responded on February 26 with the Balakot airstrike — its first strike inside mainland Pakistan since 1971 — after which Pakistan shot down and briefly captured an Indian pilot in a dogfight on February 27. The crisis marked a dangerous escalation between two nuclear-armed states and set the template for the more intense Pahalgam/Operation Sindoor confrontation in 2025. It remains a core case study in the India-Pakistan escalation dynamic that underpins the region's persistent geopolitical risk premium.", 'Wikipedia / War on the Rocks', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2019_Balakot_airstrike'), (2019, "On August 5, 2019, India revoked Jammu and Kashmir's special autonomous status under Article 370, splitting the state into two union territories and imposing a communications blackout alongside mass detentions of thousands of political leaders and activists. Government sources cite subsequent GSDP growth and rising tourism, while independent assessments note Kashmir's per-capita income relative to the national average has actually fallen (from 79.9% in 2013-14 to 76.6% in 2025-26) and unemployment remains nearly double the national rate. The move remains a live flashpoint in India-Pakistan relations and a persistent source of security-related risk premium for investment in the region.", 'Deccan Herald / Kashmir Times', 'https://www.newsgram.com/politics/2025/08/19/article-370-abrogation-kashmir-growth'), (2020, "India's COVID-19 lockdown drove the sharpest economic contraction in its history: GDP shrank 24% year-on-year in Q1 FY21 (April-June 2020) and 7.3% for the full 2020-21 fiscal year, with the services PMI collapsing from 49.3 in March to 5.4 in April 2020. Urban unemployment spiked from 8.8% to 20.8% year-on-year and household incomes fell roughly 40% during the strictest lockdown phase, with consumption remaining depressed well into 2021. The episode is the benchmark for assessing India's fiscal and monetary policy response capacity during acute shocks and remains relevant to pandemic-preparedness risk scoring.", 'Economics Observatory / Scroll.in', 'https://www.economicsobservatory.com/how-has-covid-19-affected-indias-economy'), (2020, "The government's three farm laws, passed in September 2020 to deregulate agricultural markets, triggered over a year of mass protests by more than 40 farmer unions camped at Delhi's borders — the largest such protests in decades, with farmer groups citing roughly 702 deaths during the standoff. Facing sustained pressure ahead of 2022 state elections, PM Modi announced the laws' repeal on November 19, 2021, and Parliament passed the repeal bill by month's end. The episode demonstrated the practical limits of the government's reform capacity when confronted with an organized rural constituency, a dynamic still relevant to assessing the feasibility of further agricultural or labor-market liberalization.", 'NPR / House of Commons Library', 'https://www.npr.org/2021/11/19/1057127412/india-will-repeal-controversial-farm-laws-that-led-to-massive-protests'), (2022, "India's economy overtook the United Kingdom's to become the world's fifth-largest by nominal GDP around end-March 2022 (India at $3.53 trillion vs UK's $3.38 trillion), a milestone achieved as the UK faced a cost-of-living crisis and recession risk while India posted around 7% growth. The symbolic overtaking — a decade after India ranked 11th globally — reinforced India's narrative as a rising economic power and anchor of South Asian growth. It remains a key reference point for India's long-term growth trajectory relative to its debt and reform capacity.", 'World Economic Forum / Statista', 'https://www.weforum.org/stories/2022/09/india-uk-fifth-largest-economy-world/'), (2023, "US short-seller Hindenburg Research published a report on January 24, 2023 alleging decades of stock manipulation and accounting fraud at the Adani Group; the ensuing selloff wiped out nearly $150 billion in market value across listed Adani companies within days. The episode raised international scrutiny of corporate governance standards, related-party lending, and regulatory oversight (SEBI) in India's conglomerate sector, and Adani later faced separate US bribery indictments in 2024. It remains a key case study for governance and disclosure risk in India's capital markets, particularly around politically connected large conglomerates.", 'Al Jazeera / Business Standard', 'https://www.aljazeera.com/economy/2024/8/12/adani-group-rocked-by-hindenburg-allegations-against-sebi'), (2024, "In India's April-June 2024 general election, Modi's BJP won only 240 seats — a drop of 63 from 2019 and short of a majority — forcing the NDA coalition (293 of 543 seats) to govern with smaller allied parties for the first time in a decade of single-party BJP dominance. The result, driven by voter concerns over unemployment and cost of living, constrains the government's ability to unilaterally push contentious reforms and requires more coalition bargaining on fiscal and economic policy. This shift toward coalition governance is a material input to assessing India's near-term policy predictability and reform pace.", 'CBS News / ISAS', 'https://www.cbsnews.com/news/india-2024-election-results-narendra-modi-wins-for-bjp-but-loses-seats/')],
    'IRN': [(2015, "Iran and the P5+1 (US, UK, France, Russia, China, plus Germany) signed the Joint Comprehensive Plan of Action on July 14, 2015, under which Iran accepted strict limits on uranium enrichment in exchange for relief from nuclear-related US, EU and UN sanctions. Implementation from January 2016 unlocked tens of billions of dollars in previously frozen Iranian assets and allowed a partial return of oil exports and foreign investment, briefly reintegrating Iran into global energy and financial markets. The deal's later unraveling would make 2015-2018 look, in retrospect, like a narrow and short-lived opening in Iran's decades-long sanctions history.", 'Britannica / Congress.gov CRS', 'https://www.britannica.com/event/Joint-Comprehensive-Plan-of-Action'), (2018, "President Trump withdrew the United States from the JCPOA on May 8, 2018, and by November 2018 had reimposed the full range of US secondary sanctions on Iran's oil, banking, shipping and petrochemical sectors as part of a 'maximum pressure' campaign, later eliminating waivers for major oil buyers including China, India, Japan and South Korea by May 2019. The rial lost roughly two-thirds of its value, inflation hit about 40%, and Iran's crude exports -- once above 2.5 million bpd -- collapsed as international companies exited rather than risk US penalties. The reimposition proved the deepest and most consequential sanctions episode in Iran's modern economic history prior to 2026.", 'Congress.gov CRS / U.S. State Department archive', 'https://www.congress.gov/crs-product/IF12452'), (2020, "Sanctions-driven economic hardship fed nationwide protests in November 2019 over a sudden fuel-price hike, met with a violent security crackdown, and tensions peaked when a US drone strike killed Islamic Revolutionary Guard Corps Quds Force commander Qassem Soleimani in Baghdad on January 3, 2020. The Trump administration followed with additional sanctions on Iran's construction, mining, textile and manufacturing sectors, compounding an economy already contracting under 'maximum pressure', while Iran's retaliatory missile strikes on US bases in Iraq briefly raised fears of all-out war. The episode illustrated how Iran's economic and security crises had become mutually reinforcing by the early 2020s.", 'Congress.gov CRS / IISS', 'https://www.iiss.org/publications/strategic-comments/2020/the-soleimani-killings-regional-implications/'), (2022, "The September 2022 death in police custody of Mahsa (Zhina) Amini, arrested for allegedly violating hijab rules, triggered the largest and most sustained protest wave in Iran since 2009, compounded by years of sanctions-driven currency collapse and inflation. Authorities imposed extensive internet shutdowns and 'digital curfews' to suppress organizing, while the US Treasury sanctioned senior officials responsible for the crackdown and internet restrictions; oil-sector workers also joined solidarity strikes. The unrest again underscored how sanctions-driven economic grievance and political repression have become intertwined drivers of Iranian instability.", 'CNN / U.S. Department of the Treasury', 'https://home.treasury.gov/news/press-releases/jy0994'), (2025, "After Iran was found to have breached JCPOA commitments -- including uranium enrichment levels and restricted IAEA access -- the UK, France and Germany (the E3) formally triggered the deal's 'snapback' mechanism on August 28, 2025, leading to automatic reimposition of pre-2015 UN Security Council sanctions on September 27, 2025 despite objections from China, Russia and Iran that the E3 lacked standing to invoke it. The reinstated measures bound all UN member states to reimpose an extensive sanctions regime on Iran's nuclear, missile and conventional-arms activities, marking the formal end of the JCPOA's sanctions-relief architecture a decade after it was signed. This set the diplomatic backdrop for the war that erupted just five months later.", 'Council of the EU / Skadden', 'https://www.consilium.europa.eu/en/press/press-releases/2025/09/29/iran-sanctions-snapback-council-reimposes-restrictive-measures/'), (2026, "The United States and Israel launched military operations against Iran on February 28, 2026, striking nuclear and military infrastructure amid an economy already battered by 50%-plus inflation and currency collapse under the newly reimposed sanctions. Iran's crude exports, roughly 2.1 million bpd in February, fell to zero in May 2026, and monthly export revenue collapsed to under $200m; the rial hit a record low near 1.9 million per dollar by late April 2026. The IMF projected Iran's economy would contract 6.1% in 2026 with inflation near 68.9%, the most severe economic shock in the Islamic Republic's history, compounding rather than replacing the pre-existing sanctions-driven crisis.", 'CNBC', 'https://www.cnbc.com/2026/04/23/iran-economy-war-charts-rial-oil-strait-hormuz-blockade.html'), (2026, "A US-Iran ceasefire mediated by Pakistan took effect April 8, 2026 and was extended indefinitely by President Trump on April 21, but the truce proved fragile: fighting resumed in July 2026 after Iran struck commercial vessels that had bypassed a preapproved transit route, effectively collapsing the ceasefire and prompting a renewed US naval blockade. By late August 2026, Iran and Oman had negotiated a temporary joint transit corridor through the Strait of Hormuz and a joint mine-clearing project, offering a narrow opening for de-escalation even as casualties continued to mount. The on-again, off-again nature of the conflict leaves Iran's economic outlook exceptionally difficult to forecast as of this writing.", 'CNN', 'https://www.cnn.com/2026/08/04/world/live-news/iran-war-trump'), (2025, "Despite the 2018-onward sanctions regime, China has continued to absorb the large majority of Iranian crude via a 'shadow fleet' and so-called teapot refineries -- independent processors concentrated in Shandong province (about 70% of China's teapot capacity) -- that by 2025 accounted for an estimated 90% of Iran's total oil exports. The US Treasury sanctioned multiple teapot refineries and intermediary trading networks through 2025, including Shandong Shengxing Chemical for processing more than $1bn of Iranian crude, part of an escalating enforcement campaign against sanctions-evasion structures that rebrand Iranian oil as Omani, Iraqi or other-origin crude via ship-to-ship transfers. This evasion architecture has been Iran's single most important economic lifeline throughout the 2018-2026 sanctions era.", 'United Against Nuclear Iran (UANI) / U.S. Department of State', 'https://www.unitedagainstnucleariran.com/blog/uncovering-chinese-purchasers-of-iranian-oil'), (2022, "Iran's rial slumped to a record low of about 363,500 to the US dollar by early November 2022, as nationwide Mahsa Amini-protest unrest coincided with the collapse of efforts to revive the JCPOA nuclear deal. The currency's slide reflected markets pricing in both prolonged sanctions and heightened domestic instability simultaneously, a pattern in which political unrest and sanctions risk compound each other in Iran's FX market. This dynamic -- currency depreciation tracking political and nuclear-diplomacy stress in near real time -- has repeated in subsequent crises through 2025-26.", 'Bloomberg', 'https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2022-11-05/iran-s-rial-slumps-to-record-low-amid-protests-nuclear-talks'), (2024, "President Ebrahim Raisi died on 19 May 2024 when his helicopter crashed near the Iran-Azerbaijan border, along with Foreign Minister Hossein Amir-Abdollahian. A snap election followed on 28 June and 5 July 2024, in which reformist Masoud Pezeshkian defeated hardliner Saeed Jalili in a runoff, campaigning on economic management and improved relations with the West. Pezeshkian's rise to the presidency represented a rare reformist opening in Iran's leadership just as the country was headed toward its most serious external military confrontation since the 1980s.", 'Britannica / NPR', 'https://www.britannica.com/event/Iranian-presidential-election-of-2024'), (2026, "During the Israeli-US strikes that began the Twelve-Day War on 28 February 2026, Supreme Leader Ali Khamenei was killed when his residence was struck, along with several family members, triggering an unprecedented succession crisis in the Islamic Republic. An interim leadership council initially assumed his constitutional role before the Assembly of Experts elected his son Mojtaba Khamenei as the third Supreme Leader on 8 March 2026, though Mojtaba's own injuries and prolonged absence from public view have compounded uncertainty over who effectively controls the state. The decapitation of Iran's supreme leadership is a first-order sovereign-risk event, raising fundamental questions about regime cohesion, command-and-control over security forces, and policy continuity that go well beyond the war itself.", 'Washington Post / CNN / RAND', 'https://www.washingtonpost.com/world/2026/03/01/iran-succession-supreme-leader-khamenei/'), (2026, "Iran's rial hit a fresh record low of roughly 1.4-1.5 million per US dollar in early January 2026, having already fallen from about 800,000 per dollar just before the June 2025-2026 war period to around 1.62 million shortly after fighting began. The compounding collapse -- layering war damage, renewed snapback sanctions, and leadership uncertainty on top of an already-weak currency -- has driven prices of basic goods sharply higher and intensified social unrest risk. The rial's trajectory functions as a real-time market verdict on regime stability and sanctions enforcement, making it one of the most closely watched indicators of Iran's sovereign risk.", 'Iran International', 'https://www.iranintl.com/en/202601061682'), (2026, "Amid the 2026 war, Iran closed and later repeatedly threatened the Strait of Hormuz and struck commercial shipping there, disrupting a chokepoint through which a large share of global seaborne oil trade passes; a subsequent US-Iran memorandum of understanding on a 60-day negotiating framework was itself derailed by renewed Iranian shipping attacks. Core disputes over Iran's nuclear program, sanctions relief, and free passage through Hormuz remain unresolved as of mid-2026. Continued brinkmanship over Hormuz keeps global energy-security risk directly tied to Iran's internal political trajectory, making Iran's domestic stability a variable with outsized effects on world oil markets.", 'Congress.gov / CFR Global Conflict Tracker', 'https://www.congress.gov/crs-product/IF13032')],
    'IRQ': [(2014, "In June 2014, Islamic State (ISIS) fighters seized Mosul, Iraq's second-largest city, and swept through much of northern and western Iraq, at one point threatening oil infrastructure near Kirkuk. The insurgency contracted Iraq's non-oil economy, with the IMF projecting roughly -0.5% real GDP growth for the year even as southern oil output kept rising, and Baghdad deployed some 100,000 security personnel to protect its southern fields from any southward extension of the conflict. The episode illustrates how thin the buffer is between Iraq's oil-exporting core and chronic security fragility elsewhere in the country.", 'Gulf News / IMF', 'https://gulfnews.com/business/iraq-economy-to-shrink-just-05-in-2014-imf-says-1.1424822'), (2016, 'Facing collapsed oil prices and the fiscal cost of the war against ISIS, Iraq signed a three-year, $5.34bn IMF Stand-By Arrangement on July 7, 2016 -- its first IMF-backed program since 2003. The arrangement conditioned disbursements on budget consolidation and exchange-rate stability, unlocking parallel financing from the World Bank and bilateral partners at a moment when reserves were under severe strain from the war effort. Iraq has not sought a new financing arrangement since, relying instead on annual Article IV surveillance.', 'Reuters via Business Standard', 'https://www.business-standard.com/article/reuters/imf-board-approves-5-34-billion-stand-by-arrangement-for-iraq-116070800049_1.html'), (2019, "Beginning October 1, 2019, mass protests centered on Baghdad's Tahrir Square erupted over corruption, unemployment (above 40% among youth) and failing public services, becoming the largest sustained social movement since 2003. Security forces killed hundreds of demonstrators over the following months, and the unrest ultimately forced Prime Minister Adel Abdul-Mahdi's resignation in late November 2019. The Tishreen movement kept structural economic grievances -- youth unemployment, oil dependence, sectarian quota governance -- at the center of Iraq's risk profile and reshaped subsequent electoral politics.", 'International Crisis Group', 'https://www.crisisgroup.org/rpt/middle-east-north-africa/iraq/223-iraqs-tishreen-uprising-barricades-ballot-box'), (2020, "With oil prices collapsing amid the COVID-19 demand shock, the Central Bank of Iraq devalued the dinar by roughly 22-23% on December 19, 2020, moving the official rate from 1,182 to 1,450 per dollar -- the largest devaluation since 2003. The move was intended to close a budget gap that could otherwise have reached 100 trillion dinars (about $84bn) in 2021 and prevent reserves being drawn down within six to seven months, but it immediately raised import prices for a population that had already gone two months without public-salary payments. It underscored how directly Iraq's fiscal solvency is hostage to global oil prices.", 'Washington Times / AP', 'https://www.washingtontimes.com/news/2020/dec/19/iraqs-central-bank-devalues-dinar-by-22-amid-publi/'), (2023, "A Paris-based arbitration tribunal ruled in March 2023 that Turkey had breached a 1973 pipeline agreement by allowing the Kurdistan Regional Government to export oil independently since 2014, prompting Ankara to halt roughly 400,000 bpd of KRG crude through the Iraq-Turkey pipeline. The shutdown, still not fully resolved by 2025-2026 despite periodic extensions of a compensation deal for international operators, cost the Kurdistan Region billions in lost revenue and became entangled with Baghdad's 2023 budget law, which conditioned the KRG's 12.6% federal budget share on delivering oil volumes and non-oil revenue to Baghdad. It exemplifies the unresolved federal-KRG revenue-sharing dispute that recurrently disrupts a meaningful share of Iraq's oil exports.", 'Atlantic Council', 'https://www.atlanticcouncil.org/in-depth-research-reports/report/kurdistan-iraq-2024-budget-not-what-it-appears-when-it-first-meets-the-eye/'), (2023, "Under U.S. Treasury pressure to stop dollar smuggling to sanctioned Iranian-linked networks, the Central Bank of Iraq barred 14 local banks from its daily dollar auction in July 2023, followed by 8 more banks in early 2024. The dinar's parallel-market rate immediately weakened, jumping from about 1,470 to 1,570 per dollar within two days of the initial ban, squeezing importers reliant on informal dollar channels. The episode reflects Iraq's structural vulnerability as a dollarized, oil-revenue economy caught between U.S. sanctions enforcement and Iran-aligned financial networks.", 'Al Jazeera / Reuters via Investing.com', 'https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2023/7/27/protesters-banks-demand-action-after-iraqi-dinar-plunges-post-us-sanctions'), (2025, "The IMF concluded its 2025 Article IV consultation with Iraq on July 9, 2025, warning that the oil price needed to balance the federal budget had risen to about $84 per barrel (from $54 in 2020) while petroleum still supplied more than 93% of government revenue. Under a downside scenario with oil near $57/barrel, the Fund projected Iraq's fiscal deficit could widen to roughly 11.5% of GDP in 2025-26, urging Baghdad to rein in a public wage bill that had ballooned since the expansionary 2023 three-year budget. The report captured Iraq's persistent fiscal fragility even before the 2026 war shock hit.", 'IMF Press Release 25/243', 'https://www.imf.org/en/news/articles/2025/07/08/pr-25243-iraq-imf-executive-board-concludes-2025-article-iv-consultation'), (2026, "The US-Israeli war on Iran that began February 28, 2026 drew in Iran-aligned Iraqi militias grouped under the Popular Mobilization Forces, who attacked U.S. positions and were struck in turn; by April 7 Baghdad reported 138 U.S. strikes had killed dozens of PMF fighters alongside Iraqi soldiers and police. Iraqi oil production, previously 4.3-4.5 million bpd (OPEC's second-largest), collapsed to just 817,000 bpd by June 2026 as regional tanker traffic and storage capacity were disrupted, and government oil revenue fell from about $7bn to roughly $1.5bn a month. The IMF and World Bank projected Iraq's 2026 budget deficit could reach 8-11% of GDP, a shock exposing how completely Iraq's fiscal survival still depends on oil exports.", 'AGBI (Gulf Business Intelligence)', 'https://www.agbi.com/opinion/economy/2026/07/as-the-war-flares-iraqs-economic-dire-straits-deepen/'), (2020, "In December 2020, Iraq's Central Bank devalued the dinar by roughly 20% (official rate moving from about 1,182 to ~1,450-1,470 per US dollar), a discrete fiscal-policy decision by then-Finance Minister Ali Allawi to plug a budget gap after oil revenue collapsed. The move instantly cut the real purchasing power of salaries for Iraq's roughly four million public-sector employees, who are paid in dinars, and pushed poverty rates higher. It illustrated how directly Baghdad's currency policy is hostage to oil prices and set a template for the more severe 2022-23 dinar crisis.", 'Gulf International Forum', 'https://gulfif.org/iraqs-depreciated-currency-holds-consequences-at-home-and-abroad/'), (2021, "Muqtada al-Sadr's bloc won a surprise plurality in the October 2021 parliamentary election, but months of failed coalition talks with the rival Iran-aligned Coordination Framework produced Iraq's longest government-formation deadlock on record. When Sadr announced his withdrawal from politics in August 2022, his supporters stormed the Green Zone and clashed with rival Shia militias, leaving at least 15 dead before a new government under PM Mohammed Shia al-Sudani was finally seated in October 2022. The 2021-22 crisis demonstrated the fragility of Iraq's post-ISIS political settlement and the continued risk of intra-Shia armed confrontation over state control.", 'Chatham House', 'https://www.chathamhouse.org/2022/10/sadrist-movement-iraq/introduction'), (2024, "In September 2024, Iraq and the United States agreed to wind down the US-led anti-ISIS coalition's mission in Iraq, with most coalition forces to leave by the end of 2025 and remaining support for Syria operations continuing from Kurdistan bases until at least September 2026. The drawdown reduces the roughly 2,500 US troops in-country to under 2,000, with many redeploying to Syria or Iraq's Kurdistan Region rather than leaving the theatre entirely. The transition raises questions about Iraq's capacity to contain an ISIS resurgence and about continued Iranian-aligned militia influence over security policy once US leverage recedes.", 'Reuters/Stars and Stripes', 'https://www.stripes.com/theaters/middle_east/2025-10-01/us-reduces-troops-iraq-19286383.html'), (2025, "Iraq held parliamentary elections on 11 November 2025 with turnout of 56%, up sharply from 43% in 2021; PM Sudani's coalition placed first with 46 seats but far short of a majority, followed by Maliki's State of Law and Qais al-Khazali's Sadiqun. The result again produced a fragmented, multi-month coalition-bargaining process typical of Iraq's post-2003 consensus system. The protracted bargaining (government not seated until April 2026) underscores continued institutional fragility and the risk that policy paralysis delays budget execution and reform implementation.", 'Chatham House / IPU Parline', 'https://www.chathamhouse.org/2025/10/iraq-elections-2025-how-votes-are-won-and-what-results-could-mean-iraqs-fragile-stability'), (2026, "After nearly six months of post-election bargaining, Iraq's Council of Representatives elected Nizar Amidi as president on 11 April 2026, who then named Coordination Framework figure al-Zaidi as prime minister-designate once Shia factions reached agreement in late April. The prolonged 2025-26 government-formation process -- echoing the 2021-22 deadlock -- again delayed budget passage and reform decisions during a period of falling oil prices and tighter US sanctions enforcement on Iran-linked financial flows. Recurrent formation delays remain a structural governance-risk marker for Iraq's sovereign profile.", 'The New Region / Dayan Center', 'https://dayan.org/content/2025-iraqi-parliamentary-election-results-dynamics-and-implications')],
    'ISR': [(2011, "Beginning July 14, 2011, tent encampments on Tel Aviv's Rothschild Boulevard sparked a nationwide wave of 'social justice' protests over housing costs and the broader cost of living, culminating in a September 3, 2011 rally that drew an estimated 400,000 people -- the largest protest in Israeli history to that point. Prime Minister Netanyahu formed a committee (the Trajtenberg Committee) to propose responses, though critics argued the resulting reforms were modest relative to the scale of the mobilization. The episode reflected underlying strains -- a housing affordability crisis and high cost of living relative to wages -- that have persisted as a structural feature of the Israeli economy.", 'Al Jazeera', 'https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2011/9/3/timeline-israel-protests'), (2020, 'Israel and the UAE announced normalization of relations on August 13, 2020, followed by Bahrain on September 11, with both agreements formally signed at the White House on September 15, 2020 as the Abraham Accords -- the first Arab-Israeli normalizations since Jordan in 1994. The accords opened direct trade, tourism, and investment channels, most substantively with the UAE, including the 2022 CEPA free-trade agreement, giving Israeli exporters and technology firms new Gulf markets. This trajectory would be severely tested by the October 2023 Gaza war and its regional fallout.', 'Al Jazeera', 'https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2023/9/17/three-years-on-how-have-the-abraham-accords-done-for-the-uae'), (2023, "From January to October 2023, mass protests against the Netanyahu government's proposed judicial overhaul drew hundreds of thousands of Israelis weekly, with prominent tech executives and reservists joining the movement over fears the reforms would weaken checks on government power. Startup funding fell by nearly 70% in the first half of 2023 relative to before the reforms were proposed, and the historic pattern of roughly 80% of Israeli startups registering domestically reversed, as foreign investors -- who provide about 90% of Israeli startup funding -- pushed companies to incorporate abroad. The crisis exposed how directly Israel's high-tech sector, which drives roughly 17% of GDP and over half of exports, depends on perceived institutional stability, a vulnerability later compounded by the October 2023 war.", 'CNBC', 'https://www.cnbc.com/2023/03/01/israel-judicial-reforms-protests-against-netanyahu-risks-to-economy.html'), (2023, "Hamas's October 7, 2023 attack on southern Israel and Israel's subsequent Gaza war triggered the sharpest quarterly economic contraction in Israel's history, with GDP shrinking an annualized 19.4% in Q4 2023 -- more than double consensus forecasts -- driven by a 26.9% drop in private consumption, an 18.3% fall in exports, and a 67.8% collapse in fixed investment, even as emergency government spending jumped 88.1%. The shekel fell sharply in the war's immediate aftermath, prompting Bank of Israel currency-market intervention, though it later recovered. Despite the brutal Q4 contraction, full-year 2023 GDP still grew 2.0%, but the war reshaped Israel's fiscal trajectory for years afterward through elevated defense spending.", 'CNN Business', 'https://www.cnn.com/2024/02/19/economy/israel-gdp-war-economy/index.html'), (2024, "All three major credit-rating agencies downgraded Israel for the first time in its history during 2024 as war costs mounted: Moody's cut Israel from A1 to A2 in February 2024 and later further to Baa1; Fitch cut Israel from A+ to A in August 2024, projecting a 2024 budget deficit of 7.8% of GDP and debt persistently above 70% of GDP; S&P also downgraded. Israeli officials estimated total war costs through 2025 at roughly $66 billion, more than 12% of GDP, reflecting the compounding fiscal burden of the Gaza war, the parallel conflict with Hezbollah in Lebanon (which escalated into direct strikes in September-November 2024), and heightened readiness against Iran.", 'Bloomberg', 'https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2024-09-27/israel-is-downgraded-by-moody-s-again-as-war-takes-economic-toll'), (2025, "A phased Gaza ceasefire and hostage-prisoner exchange, mediated by Qatar, Egypt, and the US, took effect January 19, 2025 and held with interruptions through much of the year, offering some fiscal relief even as Israel simultaneously launched Operation Rising Lion -- a 12-day air campaign against Iran's nuclear program, military leadership, and ballistic-missile capability -- beginning June 13, 2025. Iran fired roughly 525 ballistic missiles at Israel during the conflict, killing 28 civilians and causing direct damage estimated at no less than $1.1 billion, with total daily costs (military spending plus lost output) estimated around 2.5 billion shekels, or roughly 30 billion shekels over the 12 days. The near-simultaneous Gaza de-escalation and Iran war illustrated the whipsaw nature of Israel's 2023-2025 risk environment.", 'CNN', 'https://www.cnn.com/2025/06/12/middleeast/israel-iran-strikes-intl-hnk'), (2026, "The IMF's 2026 Article IV consultation, concluded in mid-2026, found Israel's public debt had risen from 60% of GDP at end-2022 to 68% at end-2025, with the fiscal deficit narrowed to 4.7% of GDP in 2025 through consolidation measures but still well above the roughly 2.5% pre-conflict average of 2013-2022. The Fund projected growth accelerating from 2.9% in 2025 to 4.8% in 2026 assuming no renewed hostilities, while flagging labor-supply and productivity challenges as the key medium-term risks compounding the fiscal legacy of the 2023-2025 multi-front war period.", 'IMF', 'https://www.imf.org/en/news/articles/2026/07/01/pr26234-israel-imf-executive-board-concludes-2026-article-iv-consultation'), (2020, "In January 2020, Leviathan-field partners began exporting natural gas to Egypt under a roughly $19.5bn, 60-billion-cubic-meter deal signed in 2019 -- the largest economic cooperation agreement between the two countries since their 1979 peace treaty. Israeli gas came to supply 15-20% of Egypt's consumption, and the launch turned Israel into a net energy exporter for the first time in its history. This gas-export income stream has since become a structural pillar of Israel's current account and a diplomatic anchor with Egypt independent of the broader Gaza conflict.", 'S&P Global', 'https://www.spglobal.com/energy/en/news-research/latest-news/natural-gas/011520-israel-begins-gas-exports-to-egypt-from-giant-leviathan-field'), (2021, "Israel held four inconclusive elections between April 2019 and March 2021, each effectively a referendum on Netanyahu, with the collapse of a national unity government over a December 2020 budget dispute triggering the fourth. In June 2021 a fragile eight-party coalition led by Naftali Bennett won a 60-59 Knesset vote, ending Netanyahu's continuous 12-year tenure (Bennett was to later hand the premiership to Yair Lapid). The two-year deadlock delayed budget passage and structural reforms, and set a precedent for the political instability that resurfaced around the 2023 judicial overhaul.", 'NBC News', 'https://www.nbcnews.com/news/mideast/netanyahu-era-ends-israel-new-government-survives-key-parliament-vote-n1055301'), (2023, "Following the 7 October 2023 Hamas attack, the Bank of Israel held its key interest rate at 4.75% rather than cutting, prioritizing shekel stability over growth support as the currency fell roughly 15.5% over the year; it then cut rates by 0.25 points to 4.5% in January 2024 as the shekel partially recovered. The central bank simultaneously trimmed its 2023 growth estimate to 2.3% and projected debt-to-GDP rising from 60.5% (2022) toward 65% by 2024. This currency-defense-first monetary stance illustrates how directly the war compressed Israel's macro-financial policy space.", 'US News (Reuters)', 'https://money.usnews.com/investing/news/articles/2023-10-23/bank-of-israel-holds-key-interest-rate-at-4-75'), (2024, "The Port of Eilat, Israel's only Red Sea gateway, declared bankruptcy in July 2024 after Houthi missile and drone attacks on shipping (beginning November 2023) cut its activity by roughly 85-90%, with 2024 income collapsing to about 42 million shekels ($12.5m) from 212 million shekels in 2023. The port sought emergency government aid to stay operational. It is a concrete, quantifiable example of how Red Sea/Houthi risk has directly damaged Israeli trade infrastructure independent of the Gaza front itself.", 'WorldCargo News', 'https://www.worldcargonews.com/news/2024/07/port-of-eilat-declares-bankruptcy/'), (2025, "In November 2025 S&P upgraded Israel's credit rating outlook to stable from negative (keeping the A/A-1 rating unchanged), citing prospects of military de-escalation following the US-brokered Gaza ceasefire, and projected 2026 growth of 5% as reservist call-ups eased labor-market strain. This was the first positive rating action on Israel since the war-driven downgrades of 2024. It signals that markets and agencies view the ceasefire as durable enough to begin unwinding the war-risk premium priced into Israeli debt.", 'Times of Israel', 'https://www.timesofisrael.com/sp-upgrades-israels-credit-rating-outlook-after-gaza-ceasefire/'), (2026, "In February 2026 Moody's upgraded Israel's outlook to stable from negative (holding the Baa1 rating), citing materially eased geopolitical risk following the June 2025 end of conflict with Iran and holding ceasefires with Hamas and Hezbollah, and projected 5% growth driven by a post-war rebound. Both major agencies now project similar growth trajectories, though Israel's Baa1/A-tier ratings remain well below their pre-October 2023 levels. The parallel Moody's and S&P outlook upgrades mark the clearest inflection point yet in Israel's post-war sovereign-risk recovery.", 'Times of Israel', 'https://www.timesofisrael.com/moodys-boosts-israels-credit-outlook-to-stable-as-ceasefires-in-gaza-lebanon-hold/')],
    'JOR': [(2011, 'Jordan saw sustained but comparatively contained Arab Spring-era protests through 2011 demanding political and economic reform; King Abdullah II responded with cabinet reshuffles and subsidy commitments rather than the regime change seen in Tunisia or Egypt. Even before the Syrian civil war began, Jordan ran a $1.44 billion budget deficit in 2011 and carried an official unemployment rate above 13%, reflecting structural weaknesses — a bloated public sector and heavy energy-import dependence — that predated and were later compounded by the refugee influx.', 'Washington Institute', 'https://www.washingtoninstitute.org/policy-analysis/jordans-economy-was-always-shaky-refugee-crisis-has-only-made-things-worse'), (2013, "The Syrian civil war, which began in 2011, drove hundreds of thousands of refugees into Jordan by 2013-2014, with the World Bank estimating the annual cost of hosting Syrian refugees at about $2.5 billion by 2016 against roughly $1.27 billion in that year's foreign aid. GDP growth decelerated sharply (a roughly 70% decline in the cumulative growth rate comparing 2004-2010 to 2011-2017) and youth unemployment approached 40% by 2016, cementing refugee hosting and the associated aid dependency as a structural, multi-decade feature of Jordan's fiscal and social risk profile.", 'Carnegie Endowment / European Parliament', 'https://carnegieendowment.org/research/2015/09/jordans-refugee-crisis'), (2016, "On August 24, 2016 the IMF approved a three-year, $723 million Extended Fund Facility for Jordan explicitly citing 'substantial strain on public finances' from the Syrian refugee burden, requiring subsidy and tax reforms including a reduction in bread subsidies the IMF itself had not recommended. The program's austerity measures triggered sustained 'bread protests' and, by 2018, broader tax-hike demonstrations that forced the government's resignation — establishing the recurring pattern in which IMF-mandated subsidy removal collides with Jordan's limited social safety net and generates political instability risk.", 'IMF / Times of Israel', 'https://www.imf.org/en/News/Articles/2016/08/25/PR16381-Jordan-IMF-Executive-Board-Approves-US-723-million-Extended-Arrangement'), (2022, "In 2022, Jordan and the United States signed a seven-year Memorandum of Understanding under which Washington committed roughly $845 million per year (including about $850m in direct budget support) — the U.S.'s largest per-capita foreign assistance to any country outside Israel — reflecting Jordan's position as a security-cooperation linchpin bordering Syria, Iraq, Israel/Palestine and Saudi Arabia. This aid, alongside Gulf deposits and remittances, remains a structural pillar of Jordan's balance of payments, though the Trump administration's 2025 foreign-aid reviews raised (ultimately unrealized) questions about its continuity.", 'Washington Institute / Times of Israel', 'https://www.washingtoninstitute.org/policy-analysis/jordan-exception-us-foreign-assistance'), (2024, "On January 10, 2024 the IMF approved a new four-year, roughly $1.2 billion (SDR 926.37m) Extended Fund Facility for Jordan, aimed at gradually reducing public debt toward an 80%-of-GDP target by 2028 from a 2024 level of about 90.2%. Program performance has stayed on track through successive reviews (the fifth review completed June 2026), giving Jordan continued access to concessional financing even as the Gaza war and subsequent regional escalations tested its economy, in contrast to Tunisia's stalled program or Egypt's crisis-driven renegotiations.", 'IMF', 'https://www.imf.org/en/news/articles/2026/06/17/pr26211-jordan-imf-completes-5th-review-under-eff-and-2nd-review-under-rsf-arrangements'), (2024, "The Israel-Gaza war that began October 2023 caused hotel bookings in Jordan to collapse to just 10-15% of capacity in early 2024 and an estimated 70,000 tourism-sector job losses, while Houthi Red Sea attacks disrupted Aqaba — the port through which roughly a third of Jordan's imports and over half its exports transit — cutting container throughput (121,843 containers in Q1 2024 vs. 148,454 in Q1 2023) and raising shipping-insurance costs that fed into consumer prices. The dual shock (tourism collapse plus port/logistics disruption) demonstrates Jordan's acute vulnerability to conflicts it is not party to, given its geographic position abutting both Israel/Palestine and the Red Sea shipping lane.", 'The National / Alfassel News', 'https://www.thenationalnews.com/business/aviation/2024/06/02/jordan-forecasts-flat-growth-in-tourist-arrivals-this-year-as-israel-gaza-war-rages-on/'), (2026, "The June 2025 Israel-Iran war and subsequent tensions again hit Jordan's tourism sector, with revenue falling 9.2% to $2.8 billion in the first five months of 2026 versus the same 2025 period, and cast what The National described as 'a shadow' over Aqaba's economic prospects in July 2026. Despite this, Jordan issued a $700 million Eurobond in November 2025 at a 5.75% coupon — the lowest spread on record for Jordan's international bond issuances — and used concessional loans to retire maturing Eurobonds early, saving an estimated $40 million annually, showing continued (if fragile) market confidence even amid repeated regional shocks.", 'Zawya / The National', 'https://www.zawya.com/en/economy/levant/jordan-govt-completes-eurobond-repayment-under-debt-management-strategy-roy4a1t6'), (2021, "On April 3, 2021, Jordanian authorities arrested some 19 people, placing former Crown Prince Hamzah bin Hussein (King Abdullah II's half-brother) under house arrest, alleging a foreign-backed plot to destabilize the kingdom; Hamzah denied conspiracy but later pledged loyalty to the king after royal-family mediation. The episode -- the most serious public rift within the Hashemite monarchy in decades -- briefly unsettled investors and underscored succession and elite-cohesion risk as a distinct, if usually latent, dimension of Jordan's political stability.", 'CSMonitor / Reuters coverage aggregated via Wikipedia', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2021_arrests_in_Jordan'), (2021, "In November 2021, Jordan, Israel and the UAE signed a Declaration of Intent for 'Project Prosperity' -- a water-for-energy swap in which a 600MW Jordanian solar plant ('Prosperity Green') would export power to Israel in exchange for up to 200 million cubic meters of Israeli-desalinated water ('Prosperity Blue'), formalized further via a COP27 MoU in 2022. The deal represented a rare economically-integrative peace dividend from the 1994 Jordan-Israel treaty, though implementation stalled after the October 2023 Gaza war strained Jordanian-Israeli relations.", 'Times of Israel / Foreign Policy', 'https://www.timesofisrael.com/israel-jordan-sign-uae-brokered-deal-to-swap-solar-energy-and-water/'), (2024, "On January 28, 2024, a one-way attack drone launched by an Iran-aligned Iraqi militia coalition struck Tower 22, a small US military outpost in northeastern Jordan near the Syrian border, killing three US soldiers and wounding over 40 -- the first fatal attack on US forces in Jordan and the first American combat deaths since the Gaza war began. The strike, and the US retaliatory strikes on Iran-linked targets in Iraq and Syria that followed, spotlighted Jordan's exposure to regional spillover risk from its role hosting US forces amid the Gaza conflict.", 'Al Jazeera / CBS News', 'https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2024/1/29/what-is-tower-22-the-jordan-based-us-outpost-targeted-in-a-drone-strike'), (2024, "Moody's upgraded Jordan's sovereign rating to Ba3 from B1 in May 2024 -- its first upgrade in 21 years -- citing fiscal consolidation progress and resilient financing access, even as the Gaza war weighed on tourism; Fitch separately affirmed Jordan at 'BB-' with a stable outlook. The upgrade signaled rating agencies' view that Jordan's institutional buffers (banking sector liquidity, pension fund assets, Gulf/US financial support) were absorbing regional shocks better than headline growth numbers alone would suggest.", 'Jordan Times', 'https://jordantimes.com/news/local/moodys-upgrades-jordans-credit-rating-ba3-stable-outlook'), (2024, "Jordan's September 10, 2024 parliamentary election saw the Muslim Brotherhood-linked Islamic Action Front (IAF) win 31 of 138 seats -- its best result since 1989 and up from just 5 in 2020 -- fueled by public anger over the Gaza war and calls to boycott Western firms seen as pro-Israel. Turnout was a modest 32.3%, but the result marked the strongest showing yet for Jordan's Islamist opposition under the kingdom's post-2022 electoral reforms, raising questions about future policy alignment with the Hashemite monarchy's Western-oriented foreign and economic policy.", 'Al Jazeera / France 24', 'https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2024/9/11/jordans-islamist-opposition-party-tops-parliamentary-elections')],
    'KWT': [(2020, "Kuwait's Emir Sheikh Sabah Al-Ahmad Al-Jaber Al-Sabah, who had ruled since 2006, died on September 29, 2020, and was succeeded by his half-brother Sheikh Nawaf Al-Ahmad Al-Jaber Al-Sabah. The succession occurred in the middle of an acute fiscal crisis, as Kuwait's parliament had for years blocked a new public debt law, leaving the government unable to borrow and forcing it to draw down the General Reserve Fund, which one finance minister said had gone from roughly $150 billion to near-depletion. The leadership change did not resolve the underlying legislative gridlock between the Emiri government and the National Assembly that has defined Kuwait's fiscal politics for a decade.", 'Carnegie Endowment for International Peace', 'https://carnegieendowment.org/middle-east/diwan/2020/09/kuwaits-emir-sheikh-sabah-al-ahmad-al-sabah-has-died?lang=en'), (2020, "Moody's downgraded Kuwait's sovereign rating to A1 from Aa2 in September 2020, citing rising government liquidity risks and weaker institutional and governance strength as the General Reserve Fund was rapidly depleted in the absence of a legal mechanism to issue new public debt. S&P followed with a downgrade in March 2020 and a further cut to A+ in July 2021, citing falling oil revenue and rising spending pressure -- the second downgrade in under two years. The episode reflected a uniquely Kuwaiti risk: unlike other Gulf states, Kuwait's fiscal flexibility was constrained not by market access but by its own National Assembly's refusal to pass enabling debt legislation.", 'Bloomberg', 'https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2020-09-23/kuwait-gets-first-downgrade-from-moody-s-over-liquidity-risks'), (2023, "Emir Sheikh Nawaf Al-Ahmad Al-Jaber Al-Sabah died on December 16, 2023, after a reign of just over three years marked by continued political gridlock, and was succeeded by Crown Prince Sheikh Mishal Al-Ahmad Al-Jaber Al-Sabah. The rapid second succession in three years occurred with Kuwait's public debt law still unresolved and the National Assembly repeatedly dissolved, underscoring the structural instability weighing on the country's ability to modernize its fiscal framework relative to Gulf peers who had already returned to global bond markets after 2020.", 'Fox News (wire)', 'https://www.foxnews.com/world/sheikh-nawaf-al-ahmad-al-sabah-ruler-oil-rich-kuwait-dead-86.print'), (2024, "Kuwait's Al-Zour refinery, the country's largest at 615,000 barrels per day and among the ten largest in the world, reached full operational capacity in February 2024 after the commissioning of its third distillation unit, lifting Kuwait's combined refining capacity to about 1.415 million barrels per day. The roughly $16 billion project, run by KIPIC (a subsidiary of state-owned Kuwait Petroleum Corporation), represents Kuwait's largest downstream diversification investment and is central to KPC's strategy of capturing more value from refined products rather than crude exports alone.", 'S&P Global Commodity Insights', 'https://www.spglobal.com/commodity-insights/en/news-research/latest-news/crude-oil/020424-kuwaits-al-zour-refinery-hits-full-capacity-for-first-time'), (2024, "On May 10, 2024, Emir Sheikh Mishal dissolved the National Assembly for the second time that year and suspended several constitutional articles for up to four years to allow a 'comprehensive review' of the democratic process, transferring legislative powers to the Emir and cabinet. The move, following decades of recurring parliamentary dissolutions that had repeatedly stalled fiscal reform (including debt-law legislation), removed the immediate domestic political obstacle to executive-led reform but raised longer-term questions about Kuwait's governance model and investor perceptions of policy predictability.", 'Al Jazeera', 'https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2024/5/11/political-turmoil-in-kuwait-as-emir-dissolves-parliament'), (2025, "With the National Assembly suspended and unable to block legislation, Kuwait's cabinet approved and enacted a new public debt law in March 2025 -- the first since the previous law lapsed in 2017 -- raising the borrowing ceiling to 30 billion Kuwaiti dinars (about $98 billion) and permitting maturities of up to 50 years. Kuwait subsequently raised $11.25 billion in a three-part sovereign bond (three, five, and ten-year tranches) in 2025, its first international bond sale in eight years, with the order book peaking at $28 billion -- 2.5 times oversubscribed. The return to global debt markets, made possible only after the suspension of parliamentary authority, marks a fundamental shift in Kuwait's public finance model after years of relying solely on reserve drawdowns.", 'Arab News', 'https://www.arabnews.com/node/2617534/amp'), (2025, "The IMF's 2025 Article IV consultation found Kuwait's non-oil growth running at 3.1% year-on-year in Q2 2025 and inflation easing to 2.4%, with a current account surplus around 23.6% of GDP confirming Kuwait's continued strong net-creditor position. The Fund welcomed enactment of the new debt/financing law as a first step in wider fiscal and structural reforms -- including energy-subsidy and mortgage-lending reform -- under Kuwait's Vision 2035 diversification program, while cautioning that reform momentum needs to be sustained given Kuwait's continued heavy dependence on oil revenue.", 'IMF', 'https://www.imf.org/en/news/articles/2026/02/23/pr-26061-kuwait-imf-executive-board-concludes-2025-article-iv-consultation'), (2021, "In February 2021 Fitch revised Kuwait's outlook to negative (its first rating action on Kuwait since 2008) while affirming AA, and in July 2021 S&P downgraded Kuwait one notch to A+ with a negative outlook, citing the government's continued inability to pass a debt law after the prior one expired in 2017. Kuwait's central government deficit reached 33% of GDP in FY2020/21, the highest of any sovereign S&P rated globally that year. The episode illustrated how domestic political gridlock, not oil prices alone, had become the primary driver of Kuwaiti sovereign risk.", 'Forbes', 'https://www.forbes.com/sites/dominicdudley/2021/07/17/kuwaits-credit-rating-is-cut-amid-concern-over-government-finances/'), (2021, 'On 15 November 2021, ailing Emir Sheikh Nawaf delegated most of his constitutional powers to Crown Prince Sheikh Meshal al-Ahmad al-Sabah, a former National Guard chief known for anti-corruption efforts, as Kuwait worked to recover from the pandemic downturn. The move effectively installed Meshal as de facto head of state nearly two years before he formally became Emir in December 2023. It set up the reformist, executive-heavy governing style Meshal later used to force through the 2024 debt-law and parliamentary changes.', 'Gulf News', 'https://gulfnews.com/world/gulf/kuwait/sheikh-meshal-al-ahmad-al-jaber-al-sabah-a-powerful-reformist-and-decision-maker-1.1702734434980'), (2023, "Kuwait's September 2022 National Assembly election was annulled by the Constitutional Court in March 2023 after less than six months in office, and a re-run in June 2023 returned largely the same lawmakers, failing to break the legislative deadlock. This repeated cycle of elections and annulments in 2022-2023 preceded and set the stage for Emir Meshal's more drastic May 2024 dissolution and constitutional suspension. It illustrates a multi-year pattern of institutional paralysis that has repeatedly blocked Kuwait's fiscal reform agenda.", 'Qawl', 'https://qawl.com/?p=1222&lang=en'), (2024, "By 2024 the Kuwait Investment Authority's assets under management approached $1 trillion, and by May 2026 reached roughly $1.07 trillion, making KIA -- founded in 1953 -- the world's fifth-largest and oldest sovereign wealth fund. This vast reserve cushion is the primary reason rating agencies have kept Kuwait investment-grade despite years of legislative paralysis over debt issuance. The KIA's scale relative to Kuwait's small population underpins the country's unusually strong net asset position among Gulf sovereigns.", 'AGBI', 'https://www.agbi.com/companies/kuwait-investment-authority/'), (2026, "Kuwait's fiscal deficit was projected by the Ministry of Finance to widen 54.7% to roughly $31.9bn (9.8bn dinars) in FY2026/27, driven by a 16.3% projected contraction in budgeted oil revenue. The government has pointed to enactment of a new Financing and Liquidity Law and reforms to energy subsidies and mortgage lending as offsetting measures. The widening gap despite Kuwait's vast KIA reserves highlights the disconnect between Kuwait's balance-sheet strength and its current fiscal-flow trajectory.", 'Arab News', 'https://www.arabnews.com/node/2632641/amp'), (2026, "The IMF's 2025 Article IV review (published February 2026) projected Kuwait's real GDP would expand 3.8% in 2026 on the unwinding of OPEC+ production cuts and robust non-oil growth of about 3%, even as the current account surplus was expected to moderate to 19.6% of GDP on lower oil exports. The Fund credited early reform momentum, including the new Financing and Liquidity Law, while urging further structural reforms to subsidies and mortgage lending. This combination of near-term growth optimism and flagged structural vulnerabilities frames Kuwait's current sovereign-risk profile.", 'IMF', 'https://www.imf.org/en/news/articles/2026/02/23/pr-26061-kuwait-imf-executive-board-concludes-2025-article-iv-consultation')],
    'LBN': [(2019, "Mass protests erupted across Lebanon on October 17, 2019 -- triggered by a proposed tax on WhatsApp calls but rooted in decades of corruption, mismanagement and unsustainable debt -- and tipped an already fragile economy into what the World Bank later called one of the world's worst financial crises since the 1850s. Banks began imposing informal capital controls within weeks, locking depositors out of their dollar savings, and the Lebanese pound -- pegged near 1,500 to the dollar for over two decades -- began the collapse that would eventually see it lose more than 98% of its value. The protests marked the end of Lebanon's decades-long facade of monetary and banking stability.", 'World Bank / Al Jazeera', 'https://www.aljazeera.com/economy/2020/12/1/deliberate-depression-world-banks-dire-warning-on-lebanon'), (2020, "Lebanon defaulted on its sovereign debt for the first time in its history in March 2020, missing a $1.2bn Eurobond payment and putting some $31bn in international bonds into default. The central bank, Banque du Liban, had for years borrowed dollars from local banks in a 'financial engineering' scheme to defend the pound and finance deficits running at 11-26% of GDP; the resulting roughly $72bn in BDL/government debt is now considered largely unrecoverable because it sits trapped inside an insolvent banking system. The default froze Lebanon out of international capital markets and triggered years of unresolved Eurobond-restructuring negotiations with foreign bondholders.", 'CIDOB / findevlab (Aberdeen/FinDev policy analysis)', 'https://www.cidob.org/en/publications/lebanon-financial-crisis-or-national-collapse'), (2020, "A massive explosion of improperly stored ammonium nitrate destroyed much of the Port of Beirut on August 4, 2020, killing over 200 people and inflicting an estimated $3.1-15bn in economic losses while destroying the entry point for roughly 80% of Lebanon's maritime imports, including the country's main grain silo. Around 31,000 businesses were directly affected, contributing to a further 2.4% GDP contraction in 2021 on top of the ongoing financial crisis. The blast, still without full domestic accountability years later, became a symbol both of institutional failure and of the compounding shocks Lebanon's economy has endured since 2019.", 'Gulf News / TIME', 'https://gulfnews.com/amp/story/world%2Fmena%2F3-billion-hit-on-lebanese-economy-from-massive-port-explosion-beirut-governor-1.1596647232360'), (2022, "The IMF and Lebanon reached a staff-level agreement in April 2022 on a four-year, $3bn Extended Fund Facility conditioned on bank restructuring, capital-controls legislation, fiscal reform and a unified exchange rate, but successive governments failed to implement enough of the required 'prior actions' for the deal to reach the IMF Executive Board. As of 2025 the agreement remained only partially implemented, with a revised program still undefined, leaving Lebanon -- alone among crisis-hit Arab states -- without any IMF-backed financing despite the severity of its collapse. This impasse, rooted in resistance from bank owners and political elites to absorbing restructuring losses, became emblematic of Lebanon's inability to convert international goodwill into an actual rescue program.", 'IMF Press Release 22/108 / ORF Middle East', 'https://www.imf.org/en/news/articles/2022/04/07/pr22108-imf-reaches-agreement-on-economic-policies-with-lebanon-for-a-four-year-fund-facility'), (2023, "Hezbollah opened a front against Israel on October 8, 2023, a day after Hamas's attack on southern Israel, initiating over a year of cross-border strikes that escalated into full-scale war by September 2024, including Israeli strikes that killed much of Hezbollah's senior leadership. Roughly 4,000 Lebanese were killed and over a million people displaced before a US-brokered ceasefire took effect November 27, 2024; the World Bank subsequently estimated total damage and losses at $14bn, including $6.8bn in physical destruction (led by $4.6bn to housing), and Lebanon's real GDP contracted 7.1% in 2024. The war added a fresh, war-driven layer of economic devastation on top of Lebanon's unresolved five-year-old financial collapse.", 'World Bank / Times of Israel', 'https://www.timesofisrael.com/liveblog_entry/world-bank-reconstruction-of-lebanon-after-israel-hezbollah-war-to-cost-11-billion/'), (2025, "Lebanon's parliament passed a Banking Sector Recovery Law and amended bank-secrecy legislation in 2025, a long-sought precondition for both an IMF program and Eurobond restructuring, though the IMF said in September 2025 the bank-resolution law still 'needs further refinement' to meet international standards. Separately, under intense US and Saudi pressure, Lebanon's government voted in August 2025 to disarm Hezbollah's militia by year-end -- a decision Hezbollah's leadership rejected -- with Washington's Middle East envoy saying Saudi Arabia and Qatar were prepared to invest in an economic development zone in south Lebanon for demobilized fighters. Both developments signal that fiscal and financial rescue is now explicitly tied to progress on Hezbollah's disarmament.", 'The National / Washington Times', 'https://www.thenationalnews.com/business/economy/2025/09/26/imf-presses-lebanon-to-improve-banking-reforms/'), (2026, "The eruption of the US-Israeli war on Iran in February 2026 drew in Hezbollah, which renewed attacks on Israel and reignited the risk of a wider Lebanon-Israel conflict even as the group faced domestic pressure to disarm. With Israeli forces still occupying points of Lebanese territory despite the November 2024 ceasefire, and Hezbollah refusing to lay down weapons, prospects for Gulf-funded reconstruction and an IMF program remain hostage to a security situation that could deteriorate again at any time. Lebanon thus enters the second half of 2026 as one of the region's starkest examples of a country where sovereign-risk analysis cannot be separated from unresolved armed-group and geopolitical dynamics.", 'Council on Foreign Relations', 'https://www.cfr.org/backgrounders/lebanon-how-israel-hezbollah-and-regional-powers-are-shaping-its-future'), (2021, "The Lebanese lira's collapse deepened through 2021, falling from about 1,600/USD in mid-2019 to roughly 19,000-27,000/USD on the black market by late 2021, with inflation near 200% by year-end. The currency lost more than 95% of its pre-crisis value, wiping out depositor savings and household purchasing power in a country still without an IMF program or bank-resolution framework. The uncontrolled depreciation, occurring alongside political paralysis, became one of the defining features of what the World Bank called one of the world's worst financial crises since the mid-19th century.", 'World Bank / BLOMINVEST', 'https://www.worldbank.org/en/news/press-release/2022/01/24/lebanon-s-crisis-great-denial-in-the-deliberate-depression'), (2023, "In February 2023, Lebanon's central bank devalued the official exchange rate by about 90%, moving the decades-old peg from roughly 1,500 to 15,000 pounds per dollar to reflect market reality. The overdue formal recognition of the currency's collapse came more than three years into the crisis, highlighting how long Lebanese authorities delayed adjusting official statistics and bank balance sheets to actual conditions. The delayed devaluation is frequently cited as emblematic of the political elite's resistance to accountability for the broader financial collapse.", 'Al Jazeera', 'https://www.aljazeera.com/economy/2023/2/1/lebanon-devalues-official-exchange-rate-by-90-percent'), (2024, "The Israel-Hezbollah war escalated from cross-border exchanges into an intense bombing campaign through 2024, killing at least 4,000 Lebanese and displacing about 1.2 million people before a US-brokered ceasefire took effect on 27 November 2024. The World Bank estimated Lebanon's physical damage and lost economic activity at roughly $8.5 billion, with around 40% of buildings in the southern border region destroyed. The war compounded Lebanon's pre-existing sovereign default and banking crisis, adding a massive reconstruction bill precisely as the state's fiscal capacity remained near zero.", 'World Bank / Al Jazeera', 'https://www.aljazeera.com/features/2026/5/22/lebanons-economy-struggles-under-renewed-war-and-global-fuel-crisis'), (2025, "On 9 January 2025, Lebanon's parliament elected army commander Joseph Aoun as president, ending a more than two-year presidential vacancy since October 2022, and Aoun then named Nawaf Salam as prime minister, who formed Lebanon's first full cabinet since 2022 in February 2025. The election, which occurred amid Hezbollah's diminished post-war political standing, was seen as enabled by US and Saudi backing and reduced Iranian-aligned leverage over the presidency. Filling the two long-vacant top offices was a precondition Western donors and the IMF had cited for resuming meaningful engagement with Beirut.", 'CSIS / Britannica', 'https://www.csis.org/analysis/lebanon-finally-elects-president'), (2025, "Following the November 2024 ceasefire, President Aoun moved to assert a state monopoly on arms, with the Lebanese Army confiscating rocket launchers, destroying weapons depots and tunnels, and securing disarmament commitments from Palestinian camp militias, while pressing Hezbollah to discuss its own arsenal. Hezbollah has conditioned any disarmament talks on Israeli withdrawal from contested border points and an end to continuing strikes, and by mid-2026 sporadic Israeli attacks and roughly 64,000 Lebanese still displaced showed the ceasefire remained only partially implemented. The unresolved disarmament question is central to Lebanon's sovereign risk profile because it determines whether the state can credibly claim a monopoly on force and unlock sustained donor and investor confidence.", 'Al Jazeera / Wikipedia', 'https://www.aljazeera.com/features/2026/2/2/a-year-after-hezbollah-israel-ceasefire-over-64000-lebanese-displaced')],
    'LBY': [(2011, "Libya's 2011 uprising against Muammar Gaddafi's 42-year rule escalated into civil war and NATO-backed intervention, ending with Gaddafi's death in October 2011 but collapsing oil output from about 1.6 million bpd before the war to as low as 22,000 bpd during the fighting. Post-war GDP settled near $35bn, roughly half its pre-war level, and the budget swung to a deficit worth 17.1% of GDP even though oil and gas still supplied about 96% of state revenue. The revolution removed Gaddafi's centralized rule without replacing it with functioning national institutions, laying the groundwork for the prolonged multi-government conflict that followed.", 'IMF (Libya Beyond the Revolution) / ScienceDirect', 'https://www.imf.org/external/pubs/ft/dp/2012/1201mcd.pdf'), (2014, "Disputed 2014 elections split Libya's institutions in two: a UN-recognized government based in Tripoli and a rival administration in the east aligned with military commander Khalifa Haftar and the House of Representatives in Tobruk. The Central Bank of Libya itself split into competing Tripoli and Bayda-based branches, with the eastern branch contracting Russia's state printer Goznak to issue roughly 10 billion dinars (about $7bn) in banknotes independently between 2015 and 2018-2020, run parallel to the Tripoli branch's own UK-printed currency -- a fracture not reunified until August 2023. This dual-institution structure has since been the central obstacle to any durable resolution of Libya's oil-revenue governance.", 'Middle East Eye / OCCRP', 'https://www.middleeasteye.net/news/libyas-rival-central-bank-branches-reunite'), (2020, "Forces loyal to Khalifa Haftar blockaded Libya's main oil export terminals and pipelines from January to September 2020, collapsing production from about 1.14 million bpd in December 2019 to roughly 100,000 bpd and cutting crude exports to around 350,000 bpd. The nine-month blockade, aimed at pressuring the Tripoli government amid renewed fighting for the capital, cost Libya over $6bn in lost revenue by some estimates and drove a 31% contraction in GDP. It was one of several episodes in which armed factions have used control of oil infrastructure, rather than direct territorial fighting, as their primary lever of political leverage.", 'Anadolu Agency (AA) / Al Jazeera', 'https://www.aljazeera.com/economy/2020/2/16/libya-faces-catastrophic-financial-crisis-due-to-oil-blockade'), (2022, "A standoff between rival prime ministers Abdulhamid Dbeibeh (Tripoli) and Fathi Bashagha (parliament-appointed) escalated in April 2022 into a fresh oil blockade by Haftar-aligned forces, shutting the major El Feel and El Sharara fields and the Brega and Zueitina export terminals; production bottomed out below 600,000 bpd in the first half of 2022, roughly half the year's starting level. The two sides struck a deal in July 2022 that replaced the head of the National Oil Corporation and ended the blockade in exchange for Bashagha dropping his claim to the premiership, restoring output. The episode again demonstrated how leadership disputes over NOC control, more than battlefield combat, now drive Libya's most damaging economic shocks.", 'globalEDGE (Michigan State University)', 'https://globaledge.msu.edu/blog/post/57442/political-conflict-in-libya-continues--halting-oil-production'), (2023, "Catastrophic flooding from Storm Daniel breached two aging dams above Derna on the night of September 10-11, 2023, killing thousands (estimates ranging up to roughly 11,000 dead or missing) and destroying roughly 18,500 homes -- about 7% of the eastern city's housing stock. A joint World Bank-UN-EU assessment in January 2024 put reconstruction and recovery needs at $1.8bn, and Libya's two rival governments each pledged billions of dinars toward Derna's reconstruction, but political division between Tripoli and the east has slowed and complicated the actual rebuilding process. The disaster starkly illustrated how Libya's political fragmentation degrades even disaster response and infrastructure maintenance capacity.", 'Human Rights Watch / ISPI', 'https://www.hrw.org/news/2023/12/06/libya-derna-flood-response-costs-lives'), (2024, "A dispute over central bank governance escalated sharply in August 2024 after the Tripoli-based Presidency Council moved to replace long-serving CBL governor Sadiq al-Kabir -- reportedly after he resisted the Dbeibah government's demands for more spending -- prompting eastern authorities to shut down oil fields producing roughly 700,000 bpd (over half of national output) in retaliation, with three-day losses estimated at $120m. Al-Kabir and senior staff fled the country citing militia threats, and the standoff froze most banking operations nationally for over a month before an October 3, 2024 deal restored oil flows and installed Naji Issa as the new governor. The crisis showed that even after 2023's central-bank reunification, Libya's oil and monetary institutions remain acutely vulnerable to elite political disputes.", 'The National / Al Jazeera', 'https://www.thenationalnews.com/news/mena/2024/08/30/how-libyas-unfolding-central-bank-feud-could-escalate-tension/'), (2026, "Despite persistent political deadlock between the UN-recognized Government of National Unity in Tripoli and the eastern Government of National Stability, Libya's oil sector attracted major new foreign investment: in January 2026 Libya signed a 25-year agreement with TotalEnergies and ConocoPhillips to expand production at the Waha concessions from roughly 340,000-400,000 bpd toward a targeted 850,000 bpd, while Eni's Mellitah joint venture aims to raise gas output to feed the Greenstream pipeline to Italy. Libya became Italy's single largest crude supplier in 2025, delivering 13.4 million tonnes -- nearly a quarter of Italian imports -- ahead of Azerbaijan and Iraq. The divergence between deepening foreign energy investment and unresolved political fragmentation (local elections repeatedly disrupted through 2025-2026) captures Libya's core sovereign-risk paradox.", 'SeaNews / LibyaReview', 'https://www.seanews.com.tr/article/libya-becomes-italys-big-crude-oil-supplier-mmkx16hk'), (2019, "In April 2019, Khalifa Haftar's Libyan National Army launched a months-long offensive to seize Tripoli from the UN-recognized Government of National Accord, killing more than 280 civilians and about 2,000 fighters and displacing some 146,000 people before the GNA's Turkish-backed counteroffensive halted the advance in mid-2020. The campaign drew in foreign backers on both sides (Egypt, the UAE and Russia supporting Haftar; Turkey backing the GNA), internationalizing Libya's civil war. The failed offensive entrenched the east-west territorial and institutional split that continues to underpin Libya's fragmented governance and fiscal architecture today.", 'Al Jazeera', 'https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2020/2/19/timeline-haftars-months-long-offensive-to-seize-tripoli'), (2021, "On 3 January 2021, the Central Bank of Libya devalued the dinar by about 70% (or roughly 13.3% in official year-on-year terms depending on the measure used), unifying the exchange rate after years of divergent rates operating under the country's rival east-west administrations. It was the central bank board's first full meeting in five years, reflecting how deep Libya's institutional fracture had become even over basic monetary functions. The devaluation aimed to align the official rate closer to the black market but caused an immediate socioeconomic shock, particularly for import-dependent households.", 'ReliefWeb / Central Bank of Libya', 'https://reliefweb.int/report/libya/libya-socioeconomic-impact-currency-devaluation-march-2021'), (2025, "The Central Bank of Libya devalued the dinar by a further 13.3% in April 2025 and by an additional 14.7% in January 2026, in successive attempts to close the persistent gap between the official and black-market exchange rates. The repeated devaluations reflect continued fiscal indiscipline and dollar scarcity even as oil output recovered to multi-year highs, showing that oil revenue growth alone has not resolved Libya's underlying monetary imbalances. Recurrent currency adjustments remain a leading indicator of unresolved governance splits between Libya's rival fiscal authorities.", 'The National / Central Bank of Libya', 'https://www.thenationalnews.com/business/economy/2026/04/22/libya-hits-record-output-and-approves-first-unified-budget-in-13-years/'), (2025, 'On 12 May 2025, heavy fighting broke out in Tripoli between the 444th Infantry Brigade and the Stability Support Apparatus after SSA commander Abdel Ghani al-Kikli was assassinated, followed by clashes between the Radaa Special Deterrence Force and the 444th Brigade before a truce took hold on 14 May; sporadic fighting recurred south of the capital in September 2025. The episode was one of dozens of intra-Tripoli militia clashes recorded since 2021, underscoring that even the internationally backed western government does not have a monopoly on force within its own capital. Recurring capital-city militia violence remains a persistent tail-risk for any investor or reconstruction financing tied to Tripoli-based institutions.', 'Human Rights Watch / Al Jazeera / ACLED', 'https://www.hrw.org/news/2025/05/20/libya-civilians-caught-militia-clashes'), (2025, "Libya's economy grew by a robust 13.4% in 2025, rebounding from a 2024 slowdown caused by the Central Bank of Libya's internal governance crisis, with oil output climbing to about 1.43 million barrels per day -- the highest level in over a decade -- and a target of 1.6 million bpd by end-2026. The rebound shows Libya's economy remains almost entirely a function of oil-sector performance and central-bank functionality rather than diversified growth. This oil-dependency, combined with recurring institutional disputes over who controls oil revenue, keeps Libya's fiscal outlook highly sensitive to both global oil prices and internal political bargaining.", 'The National / AfDB', 'https://www.thenationalnews.com/business/economy/2026/04/22/libya-hits-record-output-and-approves-first-unified-budget-in-13-years/')],
    'LKA': [(2017, "In July 2017 Sri Lanka agreed to lease the underperforming, Chinese-built Hambantota port to China Merchants Port Holdings for 99 years in exchange for $1.12 billion, with the deal finalized that December. Western and Indian commentators branded it a textbook case of Chinese 'debt-trap diplomacy,' though the lease proceeds were used to bolster reserves rather than being contingent on loan default, and scholarly assessments later disputed the simplest version of that narrative. The episode nonetheless became the defining reference point for China's economic leverage over Sri Lanka heading into the 2022 default.", 'CSIS / South China Morning Post', 'https://www.csis.org/analysis/game-loans-how-china-bought-hambantota'), (2019, "Coordinated Easter Sunday bombings of three churches and four hotels on April 21, 2019 killed more than 200 people and injured over 400, a shock that hit Sri Lanka's tourism-dependent economy hard. Monthly tourist arrivals collapsed from 166,975 in March to just 37,800 in May 2019, and the finance ministry estimated roughly $1.5bn in losses for the year as growth forecasts were slashed toward 18-year lows. Tourism, which directly contributes around 5% of GDP (more with spillovers), took years to recover, compounding the fiscal weakness that fed into the 2022 crisis.", 'CNBC / Diplomat', 'https://www.cnbc.com/2019/09/11/sri-lankas-economy-tourism-sector-after-easter-sunday-bombings.html'), (2022, "Mass protests known as the Aragalaya ('the struggle') erupted from March 2022 against the Rajapaksa government over acute fuel, gas, medicine and food shortages driven by mismanaged public finances and a 2019 tax-cut package. Sri Lanka suspended external debt payments in April 2022 -- its first-ever sovereign default -- and the protests forced Prime Minister Mahinda Rajapaksa's resignation in May and President Gotabaya Rajapaksa's resignation in July 2022. The crisis remains the reference point for Sri Lanka's entire subsequent IMF program and debt restructuring.", 'Wikipedia / NBC News', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2022_Sri_Lankan_protests'), (2023, "The IMF Executive Board approved a 48-month, roughly $3bn Extended Fund Facility for Sri Lanka on March 20, 2023 -- the country's 17th IMF program -- aimed at restoring macroeconomic stability and debt sustainability after the 2022 default. An immediate $333 million was disbursed, catalyzing further support from other development partners. The program has since anchored a multi-year, multi-creditor debt restructuring process that only reached substantial completion in mid-2024.", 'IMF / Al Jazeera', 'https://www.imf.org/en/news/articles/2023/03/20/pr2379-imf-executive-board-approves-under-the-new-eff-arrangement-for-sri-lanka'), (2024, "Sri Lanka's external debt restructuring progressed through 2023-24 in stages: a domestic debt operation in August 2023, Agreements in Principle with China EXIM Bank and the Official Creditor Committee (co-chaired by India, Japan and France) in October and November 2023 respectively, and final agreements with China EXIM plus signed Memoranda of Understanding with the OCC in June 2024. The restructuring -- complicated by an unusually diverse creditor base including novel bond instruments -- was assessed by the IMF as sufficient to restore debt sustainability. It represents one of the most complex sovereign restructurings completed in recent years and a template study for other distressed South Asian debtors.", 'IMF Working Paper', 'https://www.imf.org/-/media/files/publications/wp/2025/english/wpiea2025175-source-pdf.pdf'), (2025, "By end-2024 Sri Lanka's real GDP had recovered about 40% of the output lost during its 2018-2023 crisis, with growth averaging 4.3% since the recovery began in Q3 2023. The IMF completed the third review of Sri Lanka's EFF on February 28, 2025, and by April 2026 staff had reached a staff-level agreement on the combined fifth and sixth reviews, indicating the program remained broadly on track through the current period. Tourism earnings of $3.17bn in 2024 (up 53% from 2023) and steady remittance growth supported the recovery, though a devastating Cyclone Ditwah in December 2025 prompted renewed calls from economists for temporary debt-service relief.", 'IMF / Peoples Dispatch', 'https://www.imf.org/en/news/articles/2025/02/28/pr25053-sri-lanka-imf-completes-the-3rd-rev-under-the-eff'), (2019, "Gotabaya Rajapaksa, a former wartime defence secretary, won Sri Lanka's presidential election on November 16, 2019 with 52.25% of the vote against Sajith Premadasa's 42%, running on a security and economic-reform platform months after the Easter Sunday bombings. His administration went on to enact sweeping tax cuts in late 2019 that sharply eroded government revenue, directly seeding the fiscal collapse that culminated in the 2022 default. This election is a critical antecedent in Sri Lanka's debt crisis narrative, illustrating how populist fiscal policy choices compounded pre-existing external vulnerabilities.", 'Al Jazeera / The Week', 'https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2019/11/17/gotabaya-rajapaksa-wins-sri-lanka-election'), (2021, "In April 2021 Sri Lanka's government imposed a sudden nationwide ban on chemical fertilizers and pesticides to become the first country attempting a full organic-farming transition, ostensibly for health reasons but also to conserve scarce foreign exchange; the policy caused rice yields to fall 32% and tea production 18% (costing an estimated $425 million in lost tea exports), pushing overall food output down 40-50% before the ban was reversed in November 2021. The self-inflicted agricultural crisis compounded Sri Lanka's foreign-reserve shortage and worsened the food-security crisis that fed into the 2022 economic collapse. It stands as a stark example of how abrupt policy experimentation can amplify an external solvency crisis into a domestic humanitarian one.", 'Foreign Policy / S&P Global', 'https://foreignpolicy.com/2022/03/05/sri-lanka-organic-farming-crisis/'), (2022, "President Gotabaya Rajapaksa fled Sri Lanka by military jet on July 13, 2022 amid mass protests over the economic collapse, resigning from exile in Singapore the next day — the first Sri Lankan president to leave office mid-term — after which Parliament elected Ranil Wickremesinghe president on July 20, 2022. The unprecedented, constitutionally-mediated transfer of power avoided a full institutional breakdown and enabled the technocratic government that subsequently negotiated the IMF program and debt restructuring. The episode demonstrates both the acute fragility exposed by the Aragalaya protests and the relative resilience of Sri Lanka's constitutional succession mechanisms under extreme stress.", 'Washington Post / NPR', 'https://www.washingtonpost.com/world/2022/07/14/sri-lanka-protests-president-rajapaksa-resign/'), (2024, "Anura Kumara Dissanayake of the Marxist-rooted National People's Power (NPP) alliance won Sri Lanka's presidential election on September 21, 2024 with 42.31% of the vote, marking a historic break from the Rajapaksa-Wickremesinghe political establishment that had dominated since independence — a stunning turnaround for a party that won just 3.84% of the vote in 2020. His victory reflected public anger over the 2022 crisis and demand for anti-corruption reform, but also raised investor uncertainty about the new government's commitment to continuing the IMF program's austerity terms. The election is a landmark test of whether a historically marginal left-wing party can maintain fiscal discipline while in power.", 'Asia Society / ISAS', 'https://asiasociety.org/policy-institute/sri-lankas-political-shift-dissanayakes-2024-victory-marks-new-era'), (2024, "In snap parliamentary elections on November 14, 2024, Dissanayake's NPP coalition won a historic 159 of 225 seats — a two-thirds supermajority unprecedented for any Sri Lankan party — crushing the SJB opposition (40 seats) and giving the new government a strong mandate to pursue constitutional and economic reforms without needing cross-party negotiation. The scale of the landslide, mobilized by anti-establishment sentiment since the 2022 crisis, reduces near-term political fragmentation risk but concentrates significant power in a party with limited prior governing experience. This supermajority is a key variable for assessing the durability and pace of Sri Lanka's post-crisis reform agenda.", 'Al Jazeera / ISAS', 'https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2024/11/16/need-a-change-sri-lankas-leftist-win-sparks-hopes-bridges-old-divides'), (2024, "Following completion of Sri Lanka's international sovereign bond restructuring, Fitch upgraded its rating to 'CCC+' from 'Restricted Default' in December 2024, with Moody's (to Caa1) and S&P (to CCC+/C, exiting 'selective default') following in 2025, reflecting reduced default risk, fiscal consolidation, and 5% GDP growth in 2024 (4.8% in H1 2025). Despite the upgrades, Fitch's October 2025 affirmation flagged still-elevated government debt and high interest costs as continuing constraints. The rating trajectory illustrates a textbook post-restructuring recovery path, though Sri Lanka remains solidly speculative-grade and dependent on continued IMF program compliance.", 'Bloomberg / Business Standard', 'https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2024-12-23/moody-s-joins-fitch-in-upgrading-sri-lanka-on-debt-restructuring')],
    'MAR': [(2020, "On December 10, 2020, the United States issued a proclamation recognizing Moroccan sovereignty over Western Sahara, reversing decades of U.S. policy, as part of a package in which Morocco agreed to normalize relations with Israel under the Abraham Accords framework. The recognition strengthened Morocco's diplomatic hand on its central territorial dispute with the Polisario Front and Algeria, though it remains contested by the UN and most of the international community, keeping Western Sahara a live source of geopolitical and investment risk for firms operating in the disputed territory.", 'U.S. State Department / Federal Register', 'https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/FR-2020-12-15/pdf/2020-27738.pdf'), (2022, "In March 2022, Spain reversed a 42-year-old policy and endorsed Morocco's Western Sahara autonomy proposal as 'serious, credible and realistic,' repairing a bilateral rift and cementing Spain's position as Morocco's top single trading partner (bilateral trade reached €22.7 billion in 2025). The rapprochement mattered because Spain and the EU as a whole (33.7% of Morocco's 2025 goods trade) are Morocco's dominant commercial partners, and any diplomatic rupture with Madrid or Brussels over Western Sahara directly threatens market access for Morocco's largest export sectors.", 'Atalayar / Yabiladi', 'https://en.yabiladi.com/articles/details/201251/morocco-spain-trade-reaches-billion-2025.html'), (2023, 'A magnitude 7.0 earthquake struck Al-Haouz province on September 8, 2023, killing thousands (Al-Haouz alone recorded roughly 1,684 deaths) and triggering an estimated 0.24% hit to 2023 GDP, concentrated in a -10.2% regional GDP loss for Al-Haouz. Morocco launched a five-year, MAD 120 billion (~$11.7bn) reconstruction program, partly financed via a 2025 Eurobond issuance, illustrating how a natural-disaster shock can force redirection of fiscal resources even as the government pursued unrelated big-ticket World Cup infrastructure spending — a tension that resurfaced in the 2025 Gen Z protests.', 'Policy Center for the New South', 'https://www.policycenter.ma/publications/assessing-economic-impacts-al-haouz-earthquake-damages-and-recovery-strategy'), (2024, "The IMF approved Morocco's second precautionary Flexible Credit Line in April 2025 (following a first FCL of $5bn in April 2023), sized at about $4.5bn — a deliberate reduction reflecting Morocco's 'gradual exit strategy' from Fund support. Morocco remains the only MENA country eligible for the FCL, reserved for IMF members with very strong fundamentals and track records, underscoring that unlike Egypt, Tunisia, or Jordan, Morocco's engagement with the IMF is precautionary insurance rather than crisis financing.", 'IMF', 'https://www.imf.org/en/news/articles/2025/04/02/pr25085-morocco-imf-executive-board-approves-new-two-year-flexible-credit-line-arrangement'), (2024, "French President Macron made a state visit to Rabat in October 2024 after formally backing Morocco's Western Sahara autonomy plan in a July 2024 letter to King Mohammed VI, marking a 'major strategic turning point' that followed Spain's 2022 shift and further isolated Algeria's opposing position. In parallel, Morocco's CDG sovereign fund partnered with China's Gotion High-Tech in November 2024 on an EV battery gigafactory (initial ~$1.3bn, scaling toward $6.5bn), part of a broader push — alongside Renault's 2025-2030 investment plan and Stellantis's €1.2bn Kenitra expansion — to cement Morocco as Europe's automotive manufacturing hub, with automotive exports hitting a record $16bn in 2024.", 'Maroc.ma / Morocco World News', 'https://www.maroc.ma/en/news/france-moroccan-sahara-major-strategic-turning-point'), (2025, "Morocco raised €2 billion (upsized from a planned €1.5bn on over €6.75bn in demand) in its first Eurobond sale since 2020, explicitly earmarked to help fund reforms and 2030 World Cup infrastructure projects, reflecting continued market confidence despite regional turbulence. The successful, oversubscribed issuance illustrated Morocco's comparatively strong capital-markets access relative to Tunisia (locked out of Eurobond markets) or Egypt (dependent on Gulf deposits), a key differentiator in MENA sovereign risk.", 'Bloomberg / Morocco World News', 'https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2025-02-20/morocco-plans-euro-debt-in-first-foreign-bond-sale-in-two-years'), (2025, "Starting September 27, 2025, the leaderless 'Gen Z 212' movement staged Morocco's largest protests in years across 30+ cities after eight women died in childbirth at an unrenovated public hospital, chanting 'stadiums are here, but where are the hospitals?' in direct reference to lavish 2030 World Cup and 2025 AFCON stadium spending. Clashes left three protesters dead and hundreds of security personnel injured; the government responded with a 16% ($15bn) increase in health and education spending and pledges to build/renovate dozens of hospitals — a rare instance of street pressure forcing a fiscal reallocation in a monarchy with otherwise tight control over public discourse.", 'CNN / Carnegie Endowment', 'https://www.cnn.com/2025/10/01/world/morocco-youth-protest-world-cup-spending-latam-intl'), (2024, "The European Court of Justice's Grand Chamber ruled on October 4, 2024, upholding its 2021 finding that the EU-Morocco trade and fisheries agreements are invalid insofar as they apply to Western Sahara without the consent of its people, ordering the arrangements to be wound down within a year. The ruling injects legal uncertainty into a significant channel of Morocco's EU trade (notably agricultural and fisheries exports) and keeps the Western Sahara dispute a live variable for Morocco's European trade relationships even as bilateral political ties with EU states have warmed.", 'Euronews / EUR-Lex', 'https://www.euronews.com/my-europe/2024/10/04/eu-top-court-upholds-cancellation-of-eu-morocco-trade-deals-over-disputed-western-sahara'), (2025, "S&P Global Ratings upgraded Morocco to 'BBB-/A-3' in 2025, restoring the kingdom's investment-grade status, citing an expected ~4% average GDP growth through 2028, a budget deficit falling toward 3% of GDP by 2026, and a current account deficit contained near 2.1% of GDP. Moody's (Ba1) and Fitch (BB+) rate Morocco just below or at the investment-grade threshold with stable-to-positive outlooks, materially lowering Morocco's sovereign borrowing costs relative to regional peers.", 'S&P Global Ratings', 'https://www.spglobal.com/ratings/en/regulatory/article/-/view/sourceId/101648244'), (2025, "Morocco approved roughly MAD 380bn ($41bn) in infrastructure spending tied to co-hosting the 2030 FIFA World Cup with Spain and Portugal, covering high-speed rail (Kenitra-Marrakech), highway expansion, a 115,000-seat stadium, and a planned 40,000 new hotel rooms across six host cities. The spending program is a major growth and employment driver but also raises public debt and execution-risk questions given Morocco's simultaneous fiscal consolidation commitments under its IMF-monitored framework.", 'AGBI / Construction Briefing', 'https://www.agbi.com/infrastructure/2025/10/morocco-approves-41bn-in-world-cup-infrastructure-spending/'), (2023, "OCP Group (Morocco's majority state-owned phosphate giant) launched a $13bn 2023-2027 green investment plan targeting 100% renewable energy by 2027 and green-ammonia self-sufficiency by 2032, later partnering with Fortescue and Engie on green hydrogen/ammonia production potentially worth $32.5bn in approved projects nationally. As the world's largest phosphate exporter, OCP's decarbonization push is central to Morocco's bid to position itself as a green-energy and fertilizer export hub for Europe, but depends on sustained access to capital and stable energy-sector policy.", 'OCP Group / Africa Energy Portal', 'https://africa-energy-portal.org/news/morocco-approves-325b-green-hydrogen-mega-projects'), (2022, "A prolonged multi-year drought cut Morocco's usable rainfall roughly in half (from ~12bn to ~5bn cubic meters annually) and pushed reservoir levels to historic lows (32.8% of capacity by early 2022), slowing GDP growth to 1.3% in 2022 versus 7.9% in 2021 and again dragging 2024 growth down to 3.2%. Since agriculture still employs about 45% of Morocco's workforce, recurring drought remains one of the most consistent downside risks to growth forecasts and rural political stability, distinct from the 2023 Al-Haouz earthquake shock.", 'World Bank / AGBI', 'https://www.worldbank.org/en/news/press-release/2022/07/20/moroccan-economy-slows-in-wake-of-drought-and-commodity-price-rises')],
    'MDV': [(2023, "Mohamed Muizzu, backed by a pro-China coalition and the 'India Out' campaign opposing the presence of Indian military personnel, won the Maldives' presidential run-off on September 30, 2023 with 54% of the vote. In November 2023 he formally demanded the roughly 80 Indian military personnel stationed in the Maldives leave the country, sharply souring what had been a close India-Maldives security and economic relationship. The pivot set the stage for a subsequent debt and tourism-diplomacy crisis that ultimately forced Muizzu to partially repair ties with New Delhi by 2024-2025.", 'Al Jazeera / Diplomat', 'https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2023/9/30/maldives-opposition-candidate-muiz-wins-presidential-runoff'), (2024, "Maldives' total public and publicly guaranteed debt rose to about $9.4bn (over 134% of GDP) by Q4 2024, with total debt having grown from roughly $3bn in 2018 to $8.2bn by March 2024; China and India are the two largest external creditors, holding roughly $1.3bn and $130 million respectively of the external debt stock. Usable foreign exchange reserves fell below $65 million by December 2024, and the Maldives faced roughly $600 million in external debt service in 2025 and about $1bn in 2026 -- prompting widespread comparisons to Sri Lanka's pre-default trajectory. Unlike Sri Lanka, however, Maldives has so far avoided both default and a full IMF program.", 'ORF / Business Standard', 'https://www.tribuneindia.com/news/china/chinas-lending-practices-push-maldives-toward-sovereign-default'), (2024, "A diplomatic spat after Indian social-media users criticized Muizzu-aligned officials sparked a #BoycottMaldives campaign in India in early 2024, and Indian tourist arrivals -- historically the Maldives' largest single source market -- fell roughly 42%, costing the tourism-dependent economy up to an estimated $2bn. Tourism directly accounts for almost 30% of Maldives' GDP and over 60% of foreign-currency earnings, making the spat unusually consequential for the balance of payments. The episode illustrates how directly India-Maldives political friction can translate into hard-currency stress for an economy this tourism-concentrated.", 'East Asia Forum / Rising Powers Initiative', 'https://eastasiaforum.org/2024/05/09/maldives-india-political-fallout-tests-tourism-ties/'), (2024, "Maldives settled a $25 million coupon payment on its $500 million sovereign sukuk (Islamic bond) in October 2024 amid widespread fears of what would have been the world's first sovereign sukuk default, as Fitch downgraded the country's rating from CCC to CC. The narrow avoidance of default briefly steadied sentiment, though the sukuk continued trading at deeply distressed levels (around $64.72) into early 2025 before recovering. It marked the first of several near-miss debt-service events that defined Maldives' 2024-2026 crisis management.", 'Xinhua / Corporate Maldives', 'https://english.news.cn/20241007/ae535a2c2e3d4ab0b768337079a74cf4/c.html'), (2025, "India extended a Rs 4,850 crore (about $554 million) line of credit to the Maldives and signed an amendatory agreement in 2025 reducing Maldives' annual debt repayment obligations to India by 40%, alongside a separate rollover of India's $50 million treasury-bill subscription. In October 2025 China separately finalized a bilateral debt-relief arrangement providing a five-year deferral on Chinese debts. Together, the near-simultaneous Indian and Chinese relief packages show both regional powers actively competing to be Male's creditor-of-choice rather than letting Maldives default or turn to a full IMF program.", 'News on Air / Deccan Herald', 'https://www.tribuneindia.com/news/india/india-extends-financial-lifeline-to-maldives-with-rs-832-crore-aid'), (2026, "The Maldives fully repaid its $500 million sovereign sukuk plus roughly $25 million in profit as it matured in 2026, successfully avoiding what would have been the first-ever sovereign default on an Islamic bond. The sukuk's market price had climbed from a distressed $64.72 in early April 2025 to $87.44 by July 2025 as investor confidence in Male's repayment capacity -- bolstered by Indian and Chinese bilateral relief -- improved. Despite this, the IMF's 2026 Article IV mission (completed June 2026) continued to flag the financing gap and debt trajectory as unresolved, with no formal IMF program yet in place.", 'Maldives Voice / IMF', 'https://www.imf.org/en/news/articles/2026/06/16/pr-26208-maldives-imf-staff-completes-2026-article-iv-mission'), (2020, "The COVID-19 pandemic caused an unprecedented 32-33.5% contraction in Maldives' GDP in 2020, as tourist arrivals were halted between March 26 and July 15, 2020 and Q2 2020 GDP fell 51.6% year-on-year; poverty climbed from 2.1% to 7.2% of the population. Given tourism directly accounts for roughly a quarter of GDP and half of livelihoods, this was the sharpest shock in the country's modern economic history, exceeding even the 2004 tsunami's impact. The episode remains the benchmark case for Maldives' single-sector concentration risk and its extreme sensitivity to global travel disruptions.", 'ISAS / ADB', 'https://www.isas.nus.edu.sg/papers/covid-19-economic-impact-and-challenges-for-the-maldives/'), (2020, "In May 2020, the Chinese Eximbank loan financing the $200 million China-Maldives Friendship Bridge (Sinamalé Bridge) — a flagship Belt and Road project whose cost had doubled from initial estimates despite being downsized from six to four lanes — was restructured through the G20's Debt Service Suspension Initiative amid Maldives' pandemic-driven fiscal strain. Former President Solih explicitly blamed the BRI for saddling Maldives with excessive debt and enabling corruption. The episode is a concrete data point in assessing China's debt exposure to Maldives and the broader debate over BRI project transparency and value-for-money in small island states.", 'AidData / Heritage Foundation', 'https://china.aiddata.org/projects/38037/'), (2024, "Following President Muizzu's 2023 campaign pledge to remove foreign military presence, India completed the phased withdrawal of roughly 75 military personnel from the Maldives by May 10, 2024, replacing them with civilian technical staff to continue operating gifted aviation platforms. The withdrawal marked the culmination of Muizzu's 'India Out' agenda and a symbolic recalibration of Malé's traditionally India-centric security posture. It illustrates how quickly bilateral security arrangements can shift with a change of government, a factor relevant to assessing continuity risk in India-Maldives strategic and financial relations.", 'Al Jazeera / Business Today', 'https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2024/3/12/india-troops-begin-maldives-pullout-after-pro-china-leaders-order-reports'), (2024, "Maldives' foreign currency reserves fell to a record low of $371.2 million in September 2024, dangerously thin against external debt service needs of $600-700 million in 2025 and over $1 billion in 2026; reserves partially recovered only after a $400 million currency swap arrangement with India's Reserve Bank. This near-crisis level of reserves, against total public and publicly guaranteed debt of $9.4 billion (over 134% of GDP) by late 2024, exemplifies Maldives' razor-thin external liquidity buffer and continued dependence on ad hoc bilateral support rather than durable market access.", 'Maldives Independent / Ratings agencies', 'https://www.newsonair.gov.in/mma-reports-decline-in-foreign'), (2024, "Fitch downgraded Maldives to 'CC' in mid-2024 and Moody's followed with a downgrade to Caa2 (from Caa1), explicitly citing 'materially' rising default risk given low reserves and a narrowing window to secure financing for imminent external debt obligations. The rating actions came even as the government publicly vowed to avoid default, with sovereign bond prices rallying on hopes of external support. This rapid, back-to-back agency downgrade cycle is a critical marker of Maldives' position at the acute end of the sovereign distress spectrum, just above outright default.", "Moody's Ratings / Adhadhu", 'https://ratings.moodys.com/ratings-news/428179'), (2022, 'India committed a $500 million financing package (a $400 million line of credit plus $100 million grant) in August 2020 for the Greater Male Connectivity Project — a 6.74km bridge and causeway linking Malé to Villingili, Gulhifalhu, and Thilafushi — with construction breaking ground in August 2022 in a joint virtual ceremony between PM Modi and President Solih. Designed to dwarf the Chinese-built 1.4km Sinamalé Bridge, the project reflects India-China strategic competition for infrastructure influence in the Maldives, financed on more concessional terms than comparable Chinese lending. Its progress (or delay) under the more China-friendly Muizzu government is a useful indicator of how domestic political alignment shapes major infrastructure financing outcomes.', 'IndBiz / Inventiva', 'https://indbiz.gov.in/us-500-million-grant-shared-by-india-for-the-greater-male-connectivity-project-in-favour-of-infrastructure-development/')],
    'NPL': [(2015, "A magnitude-7.8 earthquake struck central Nepal in April 2015, killing nearly 9,000 people and causing an estimated one-third of GDP in damages; a new constitution was subsequently ratified in September 2015 but was rejected by the India-linked Madhesi community in the southern plains. The constitutional dispute triggered an unofficial Indian border blockade from late 2015 into 2016 that shut roughly 2,200 industries, left 400,000 people jobless, and caused an estimated $5bn in national financial losses, cutting projected 6% growth to about 2%. The compounding earthquake-blockade shock remains a formative reference point for Nepal's vulnerability to both natural disasters and its dependence on India for trade transit.", 'ResearchGate / Conciliation Resources', 'https://www.c-r.org/accord/nepal/uncertain-aftermath-political-impacts-2015-earthquakes-nepal'), (2022, "The IMF Executive Board approved a $395.9 million (SDR 282.42 million), 38-month Extended Credit Facility arrangement for Nepal on January 12, 2022, Nepal's first-ever IMF program of this kind, aimed at supporting pandemic recovery and preserving macroeconomic stability. The program provided a policy anchor through a period of import restrictions and reserve pressure in 2022. Multiple successful reviews since have kept Nepal current on IMF-linked reforms even through the 2025 political upheaval.", 'IMF', 'https://www.imf.org/en/news/articles/2022/01/13/pr2206-nepal-imf-executive-board-approves-us-million-ecf-arrangement'), (2024, "Nepal and India signed a long-term power trade agreement in January 2024 for Nepal to export up to 10,000 megawatts of hydroelectricity to India over ten years, dramatically expanding on earlier ad hoc export arrangements. By mid-2025 India had raised approved imports of Nepali power to over 1,000 MW, and Nepal began its first-ever electricity exports to Bangladesh (40 MW) in June 2025. The deal illustrates hydropower's emergence as Nepal's most strategically important growth lever alongside remittances.", 'Kathmandu Post', 'https://kathmandupost.com/national/2024/01/04/nepal-india-ink-10-year-10-000mw-power-trade'), (2025, "Nepal's government banned 26 major social media platforms (including Facebook, YouTube, WhatsApp and Instagram) on September 4, 2025 for failing to register locally, compounding years of youth grievance over corruption, nepotism and limited jobs. Police killed 19 protesters on September 8, 2025, and the Oli-led government collapsed within 24 hours; interim Prime Minister Sushila Karki was sworn in on September 12, 2025. Estimated collateral economic damage reached about $21bn -- roughly half of Nepal's annual GDP -- with nearly 10,000 people left jobless overnight and the tourism sector alone absorbing about $178 million in damages.", 'Britannica / The New Humanitarian', 'https://www.britannica.com/event/2025-Nepalese-Gen-Z-Protests'), (2026, "Parliamentary elections held March 5, 2026 in the wake of the Gen-Z uprising saw the newly formed Rastriya Swatantra Party (RSP) win a decisive plurality over Nepal's traditional parties, and RSP leader Balen Shah was sworn in as prime minister on March 27, 2026. Despite the scale of 2025's political and economic disruption, Nepal avoided broader macroeconomic instability, helped by strong remittance inflows, record-high foreign exchange reserves, and low inflation. The transition marks the most significant realignment of Nepali party politics in decades and a live test of whether a first-time governing party can sustain IMF-linked fiscal discipline.", 'India Tribune / ISAS', 'https://www.indiatribune.com/public/year-ender-2025-rocked-by-gen-z-protests-and-political-instability-nepal-stares-economic-slowdown'), (2015, "Nepal promulgated a new constitution on September 20, 2015 establishing a federal republic, but Madhesi and Tharu groups in the southern Terai protested the statute as discriminatory, with 57 killed in unrest; India then imposed what Nepal called an economic blockade at the key Birgunj border crossing, halting fuel, medicine, and essential goods for roughly 135 days. Coming just months after the devastating 2015 earthquakes, the blockade compounded an already severe humanitarian and reconstruction crisis and exposed Nepal's near-total trade dependence on India. It remains a foundational episode in assessing Nepal's structural vulnerability to Indian trade and transit leverage.", 'CS Monitor / International Crisis Group', 'https://www.csmonitor.com/World/Asia-South-Central/2015/0928/Nepal-accuses-India-of-an-economic-blockade-as-border-trade-freezes-up'), (2021, "PM KP Sharma Oli unilaterally dissolved Nepal's House of Representatives in December 2020 amid an intra-party power struggle, a move widely deemed unconstitutional; after losing a confidence vote in May 2021 he dissolved parliament a second time, only for the Supreme Court to reinstate it on July 12, 2021, ruling the dissolution unconstitutional. This period of executive overreach and judicial pushback exemplified the chronic governmental instability that has plagued Nepal's post-2015 federal republic, with frequent coalition realignments undermining continuity of economic policy. It remains a relevant marker for assessing political-institutional risk in a country that has cycled through numerous prime ministers in under a decade.", 'Al Jazeera / ORF', 'https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2021/7/12/nepals-supreme-court-reinstates-dissolved-parliament'), (2022, "Nepal's November 2022 general election produced a hung parliament; Maoist Centre leader Pushpa Kamal Dahal ('Prachanda'), despite winning only 32 of 275 seats, engineered a last-minute coalition switch to ally with KP Oli's UML bloc and was sworn in as prime minister on December 26, 2022, ahead of larger vote-getter Nepali Congress. The episode again illustrated Nepal's pattern of small parties acting as kingmakers and frequent post-election government reshuffling, which complicates long-term investment and reform planning. Prachanda's own coalition later collapsed before his term was due to end, reinforcing the pattern of instability.", 'The Diplomat / CNN', 'https://thediplomat.com/2022/12/pushpa-kamal-dahal-heads-new-government-in-nepal/'), (2024, "Nepal's remittance inflows reached 26.6% of GDP in 2023 (about $11 billion) and rose further to 28.6% of GDP in FY2024-25 (Rs1.72 trillion, up 19% year-on-year), with over 7% of the population working abroad — a ratio more than five times the global average. This extreme reliance makes Nepal's economy highly sensitive to labor-destination-country conditions (notably Gulf states and Malaysia) and currency movements, while also being a key structural support for its balance of payments and poverty reduction. The scale of remittance dependence is a central variable in any assessment of Nepal's external vulnerability and growth model sustainability.", 'World Bank / Nepal Rastra Bank via nepalnews.com', 'https://english.nepalnews.com/s/long-reads/206401/'), (2024, "Unprecedented rainfall on September 27-28, 2024 triggered catastrophic floods and over 130 landslides across Nepal, killing more than 250 people (37 in the Kathmandu Valley alone), displacing over 8,400 people, and destroying 25 bridges, 37 highways, and 11 hydropower stations, with property damage estimated at Rs17 billion or more. It was the heaviest rainfall event recorded in the Kathmandu Valley since modern hydrological monitoring began in 1970. The disaster highlights Nepal's acute exposure to climate-driven infrastructure risk, with direct implications for hydropower revenue (a key export earner) and fiscal contingent liabilities from disaster response and reconstruction.", 'ReliefWeb / Copernicus Emergency Management', 'https://reliefweb.int/disaster/fl-2024-000177-npl'), (2025, "After Gen-Z-led protests over corruption and a since-reversed social media ban forced PM KP Oli's government to collapse in September 2025 (with parliament itself set ablaze), former Chief Justice Sushila Karki was sworn in on September 12, 2025 as Nepal's first female head of government, selected via an unprecedented Discord poll among protest organizers that drew over 7,700 votes. Karki's interim mandate was to investigate state violence and corruption and organize elections, held in March 2026. The novel, extralegal selection process — while restoring short-term stability — raises longer-term questions about institutional legitimacy and governance continuity that matter for sovereign risk assessment.", 'NPR / Wikipedia (Karki interim cabinet)', 'https://www.npr.org/2025/09/20/nx-s1-5545760/nepal-protests-gen-z'), (2026, "Facing geopolitical tensions, global supply-chain disruption, and concerns over the impact on remittance-linked trade preferences, Nepal formally sought in 2026 to defer its long-planned graduation from Least Developed Country status from November 2026 to November 2029, citing inadequate trade readiness and institutional capacity. Graduation would end Nepal's duty-free market access and concessional financing eligibility, a major trade-finance risk factor given its heavy reliance on remittances and narrow export base. The deferral request reflects continued fragility in Nepal's structural transformation despite meeting the UN's income and human-asset graduation thresholds.", 'Kathmandu Post', 'https://kathmandupost.com/money/2026/05/22/nepal-moves-to-defer-ldc-graduation-until-2029')],
    'OMN': [(2011, 'Beginning in January 2011, protests inspired by the Arab Spring erupted across Oman, most violently in the industrial port city of Sohar, where demonstrators demanding jobs, an end to corruption, and political reform clashed with security forces, leaving at least one protester dead and dozens arrested amid government buildings set ablaze. Sultan Qaboos responded swiftly with concessions -- dismissing cabinet ministers, creating 50,000 new public-sector jobs, introducing unemployment stipends, and raising civil-service salaries -- which quelled most unrest by mid-2011. The episode, unusually violent for typically stable Oman, entrenched a pattern of using state largesse to buy social peace that has repeatedly complicated subsequent fiscal-consolidation efforts.', 'Fanack', 'https://fanack.com/oman/history-of-oman/omans-arab-spring/'), (2020, "Sultan Qaboos bin Said, who had ruled Oman since 1970 and had no children, died on January 10, 2020, at age 79; the royal family's ruling council selected his cousin Haitham bin Tariq as successor via a sealed letter Qaboos had left in case no consensus was reached. The succession -- the first in half a century -- occurred just as oil prices were about to collapse amid COVID-19 and the Saudi-Russia price war, thrusting the new sultan immediately into managing Oman's most severe fiscal crisis in decades.", 'France 24', 'https://www.france24.com/en/20200111-sultan-qaboos-of-oman-dies-aged-79'), (2020, "In response to a fiscal crisis that saw Oman's deficit reach 15.8% of GDP in 2020 and public debt climb from about 15% of GDP in 2015 toward roughly 81% by 2020, Sultan Haitham's government launched a Medium-Term Fiscal Plan (MTFP) 2020-2024 built on three pillars: containing spending, boosting non-oil revenue, and reducing debt. The plan prioritized cutting non-essential spending over public services and broadening the tax base rather than raising rates, a markedly different approach from Bahrain's repeated VAT-rate increases. Successive credit downgrades through 2020 reflected market skepticism at the plan's outset that would later prove overly pessimistic.", 'Oman Observer', 'https://www.omanobserver.om/article/8495/Front%20Stories/omans-medium-term-fiscal-plan-2020-2024-explained'), (2024, "Oman's fiscal consolidation achieved its clearest validation in September 2024 when S&P upgraded the sultanate back to investment grade (BBB- from BB+), its first return to investment-grade status in seven years, citing reduced public debt following lower spending and a return to fiscal surplus. Public debt fell to about 34% of GDP by end-2024 from roughly 68% at end-2020, and government expenditure dropped to 29% of GDP compared with an average above 41% during 2016-2020. Fitch followed with its own upgrade to BBB- (investment grade) in 2024, confirming a market consensus shift on Oman's creditworthiness for the first time since 2017.", 'MEED', 'https://www.meed.com/oman-recovers-investment-grade-credit-rating'), (2025, "Moody's raised Oman's credit rating to investment grade (Baa3 from Ba1) with a stable outlook in July 2025, becoming the second of the three major agencies (after S&P) to restore Oman's sovereign rating to investment territory, citing structural reforms and strengthened fiscal and macroeconomic fundamentals including reduced public debt. The upgrade completed the rehabilitation of Oman's credit standing after the deep downgrades of 2020, transforming Oman from the GCC's most fiscally troubled state into a rare Gulf example of successful post-oil-shock fiscal consolidation.", 'Oman Observer', 'https://www.omanobserver.om/article/1173296/business/economy/moodys-upgrades-omans-credit-rating-to-investment-grade'), (2016, "China's Oman Wanfang consortium committed roughly $10.7 billion in 2016 to co-develop the China-Oman Industrial Park within the Duqm Special Economic Zone, envisioned as a Belt-and-Road-linked port, industrial park, and residential city positioned at a strategic transshipment point between Africa, Asia, and Europe. Progress has been slower than initially announced, with much of the designated industrial park land still undeveloped by the mid-2020s even as the port and refinery components advanced. Duqm nonetheless remains Oman's flagship attempt to diversify beyond Muscat-centric development and to capture Chinese and other foreign direct investment tied to regional logistics and downstream petrochemicals.", 'HKTDC Belt and Road Portal', 'https://beltandroad.hktdc.com/en/insights/bri-backed-oman-economic-zone-attracts-raft-global-investors'), (2025, "The IMF's 2025 Article IV consultation, concluded January 2026, found Oman's growth accelerating to 2.3% year-on-year in H1 2025 with low inflation, continuing a run of fiscal surpluses that began in 2022 and had by then lowered public debt further from the 2020 peak. The Fund's assessment reinforced the market view -- already reflected in Oman's 2024-2025 upgrades to investment grade by S&P, Fitch, and Moody's -- that Oman's post-2020 fiscal consolidation represents one of the more durable structural adjustments among oil-dependent Gulf economies, though continued oil-price dependence leaves the improvement vulnerable to a renewed downturn.", 'IMF', 'https://www.imf.org/en/news/articles/2026/01/14/pr-2604-oman-imf-executive-board-concludes-2025-article-iv-consult'), (2021, "Oman introduced a 5% value-added tax in 2021 as part of its Medium-Term Fiscal Plan, after the IMF had urged its expeditious introduction; the central government balance improved by 12.8 percentage points of GDP to a 3.2% deficit that year, aided by higher hydrocarbon revenue and expenditure restraint. Central government debt fell from 69.7% of GDP at end-2020 to 62.9% at end-2021. VAT introduction marked Oman's first broad-based non-oil revenue tool and became the template for the fiscal consolidation that drove its later rating upgrades.", 'Middle East Institute', 'https://www.mei.edu/publications/balancing-books-deep-dive-fiscal-policy-and-diversification-oman'), (2021, "In April 2021 Oman announced measures allowing foreign investors to fully own 100% of the value of shares in local businesses, part of Sultan Haitham's post-Qaboos push to attract international capital under Vision 2040. The reform aimed to diversify Oman's investor base beyond hydrocarbon-linked domestic capital. It signaled a structural policy shift toward openness that rating agencies later cited when restoring Oman's investment-grade status.", 'Fanack', 'https://fanack.com/economy/features-insights/omans-privatization-between-opposition-and-expansion~242072/'), (2022, "Oman posted a budget surplus of 7.5% of GDP in 2022 on high oil prices, and Sultan Haitham directed the windfall toward debt repayment, cutting national debt from 68% of GDP to 35% by 2024. This rapid deleveraging was the direct catalyst for Oman regaining investment-grade ratings from S&P and Moody's in 2024-2025. It represents one of the fastest sovereign debt-reduction trajectories among Gulf oil exporters this decade.", 'Foreign Policy', 'https://foreignpolicy.com/2026/01/12/haitham-bin-tariq-oman-gulf-oil-diplomacy/'), (2023, "In the second half of 2023 Oman's government executed a buyback of Eurobonds and a sukuk, alongside prepayment of syndicated loans, actively retiring external debt ahead of schedule. This proactive liability management, funded by the 2022-23 oil-price windfall, materially reduced Oman's external financing needs and interest burden. It was a key technical step underpinning the 2024 S&P and 2025 Moody's upgrades to investment grade.", 'IMF', 'https://www.elibrary.imf.org/view/journals/002/2024/031/article-A001-en.xml'), (2024, "Oman's $8.5bn, 230,000 barrel-per-day OQ8 Duqm refinery -- a joint venture with Kuwait's KPC building on the 2016 China-Oman Duqm SEZ framework -- completed construction in December 2022, began trial operations in 2023, and reached full capacity in February 2024. The refinery diversifies Oman's hydrocarbon value chain beyond crude exports into refined products, supporting Vision 2040's industrialization goals. It stands as the flagship delivered project from Oman's decade-long Duqm Special Economic Zone strategy.", 'The Energy Year', 'https://theenergyyear.com/articles/omans-oq8-duqm-refinery-project-highlight/'), (2026, "Oman approved a $29.77bn general state budget for 2026 targeting roughly 4% economic growth, with the IMF upgrading its 2026 growth forecast to 3.7%; 2025 hydrocarbon revenue of RO8.481bn (70% of state revenue) benefited from oil averaging $72/bbl versus a budgeted $60/bbl. Continued reliance on hydrocarbons for the majority of revenue, even after years of VAT and debt reforms, illustrates the limits of Oman's diversification progress to date. The budget nonetheless reflects the more resilient fiscal position that underpinned Oman's return to investment grade.", 'News on Air (India)', 'https://www.newsonair.gov.in/oman-approves-29-77-billion-budget-for-2026-sets-target-of-4-economic-growth')],
    'PAK': [(2013, "The IMF approved a three-year, $6.64bn Extended Fund Facility for Pakistan in September 2013 under the newly elected Nawaz Sharif government, aimed at averting a balance-of-payments crisis amid chronic twin deficits. The program, completed in September 2016, is generally regarded by the IMF itself as Pakistan's only fully successful arrangement, having rebuilt reserves and stabilized growth. It set the template -- and the recurring dependency -- for the seven-plus IMF programs Pakistan has entered since independence.", 'IMF', 'https://www.imf.org/en/countries/pak/faq'), (2019, "Facing another reserves crisis, Pakistan entered a 39-month, roughly $6bn Extended Fund Facility with the IMF in July 2019 under Prime Minister Imran Khan, aimed at restoring macroeconomic sustainability. The program was repeatedly stalled by missed fiscal and energy-pricing targets and required multiple augmentations before formally lapsing. Its rocky implementation set up the deeper 2022 crisis when Khan's government reversed fuel subsidy cuts just before his ouster.", 'IMF', 'https://imf.org/en/News/Articles/2019/12/19/pr19477-pakistan-imf-executive-board-completes-review-of-pakistans-extended-fund-facility'), (2022, 'Imran Khan was removed as prime minister in April 2022 via a no-confidence vote, triggering a political crisis that compounded an already deteriorating economy. Foreign exchange reserves fell to around $9bn by late May 2022 (under two months of import cover), and the State Bank devalued the rupee by roughly 28% against the dollar by December as inflation and fiscal pressure mounted. The instability delayed IMF disbursements for months and pushed Pakistan to the edge of default the following year.', 'GIS Reports Online / Wikipedia', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pakistani_political_crisis_(2022%E2%80%93present)'), (2022, 'Catastrophic monsoon floods from June to October 2022 submerged roughly a third of the country, displaced millions, and caused an estimated $30bn in damages and economic losses against reconstruction needs of over $16bn, according to a joint government-World Bank-ADB-UN assessment. The disaster is estimated to have shaved about 2.2 percentage points off FY22 GDP and pushed 8.4-9.1 million more people into poverty. It became a central justification Islamabad has since used in IMF talks for fiscal flexibility, including again after the 2025 floods.', 'World Bank', 'https://www.worldbank.org/en/news/press-release/2022/10/28/pakistan-flood-damages-and-economic-losses-over-usd-30-billion-and-reconstruction-needs-over-usd-16-billion-new-assessme'), (2023, 'With inflation near 38% and reserves down to roughly $3bn, the IMF Executive Board approved a $3bn, 9-month Stand-By Arrangement on July 12, 2023 after months of brinkmanship that had pushed Pakistan to the edge of sovereign default. The deal required a return to a market-determined exchange rate, tighter monetary policy, and energy-sector reforms, and unlocked an immediate $1.2bn disbursement. It bought Islamabad just enough time to negotiate the larger, longer-dated arrangement that followed in 2024.', 'IMF', 'https://www.imf.org/en/news/articles/2023/07/12/pr23261-pakistan-imf-exec-board-approves-us3bil-sba'), (2024, "The IMF Executive Board approved a new 37-month, roughly $7bn Extended Fund Facility for Pakistan on September 25, 2024, with an immediate disbursement of about $1bn. For the first time the program formalized provincial fiscal commitments through a National Fiscal Pact, tying federal-provincial revenue sharing to program conditionality. This is Pakistan's 25th-ish IMF arrangement in total and the one currently anchoring the country's macro-stability narrative through 2026.", 'IMF', 'https://www.imf.org/en/news/articles/2024/09/27/pr-24343-pakistan-imf-concludes-2024-aiv-consultation-pakistan-approves-37-mo-extended-arr'), (2025, "India launched Operation Sindoor missile strikes on May 7, 2025 against alleged militant sites in Pakistan following the April 22 Pahalgam terror attack that killed 26 people in Indian-administered Kashmir; four days of cross-border strikes, drone exchanges, and shelling followed before a ceasefire. Pakistan's KSE-100 index fell roughly 5-7% intraday and around 13% cumulatively since the Pahalgam attack, while the Attari-Wagah trade corridor closure cost an estimated $132-168 million in bilateral trade. The episode underscored how quickly India-Pakistan tension can reprice Pakistani risk even without a formal war.", 'Stimson Center / Business Standard', 'https://www.stimson.org/2025/four-days-in-may-the-india-pakistan-crisis-of-2025/'), (2025, "A new border war erupted with the Afghan Taliban government from October 2025, following cross-border strikes each side blamed on the other's militant proxies; a Qatar-mediated October ceasefire collapsed by November 2025. Pakistan's exports to Afghanistan fell roughly 56% in early 2026 and bilateral trade dropped from about $2.46bn in 2024 to $1.77bn in 2025, with Pakistan estimated to have lost nearly $850 million from border closures between October 2025 and May 2026 as Afghan trade diverted to Iran and Central Asia. The conflict illustrates a new geopolitical-economic risk vector for Pakistan beyond its traditional India and IMF axes.", 'IANS Live / Modern Diplomacy', 'https://ianslive.in/afghanistans-new-trade-routes-leave-pakistans-border-economy-reeling--20260825144348'), (2022, "Pakistan exited the FATF 'grey list' in October 2022 after four years of enhanced monitoring, having completed 34 action items across two concurrent action plans targeting terror financing and money laundering. The delisting removed a persistent drag on foreign investment sentiment and correspondent banking relationships. It remains a reference point for how compliance reforms can be reversed by renewed instability, since re-listing risk resurfaces whenever governance or security concerns flare.", 'Al Jazeera', 'https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2022/10/21/must-stay-on-course-pakistan-is-removed-from-fatf-gray-list'), (2023, "The May 9, 2023 riots erupted after the paramilitary arrest of former PM Imran Khan on corruption charges, with PTI supporters attacking over 40 military installations including army headquarters in Rawalpindi and an air base in Mianwali, killing at least 10 people and causing roughly Rs2.5 billion in damage. The unprecedented assault on military symbols triggered a sweeping crackdown, mass arrests, and military trials of civilians that hardened the civil-military rift. The episode remains central to Pakistan's political risk premium, illustrating the fragility of the compact between the military establishment and elected civilian politics.", 'Al Jazeera / Wikipedia', 'https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2024/12/21/pakistan-jails-25-imran-khan-supporters-over-attacks-on-military-sites'), (2024, "Pakistan's February 8, 2024 general election produced a hung parliament, with PTI-aligned independents winning the most seats amid widespread allegations of rigging, poll-day violence, and delayed results; PML-N and PPP subsequently formed a coalition government under PM Shehbaz Sharif. The disputed mandate weakened the new government's political capital just as it needed to negotiate a fresh IMF program and push through unpopular fiscal reforms. Persistent questions about the election's legitimacy continue to feed into sovereign risk assessments of Pakistan's governance stability.", 'USIP / Reuters coverage via Commons Library', 'https://www.usip.org/publications/2024/02/understanding-pakistans-election-results'), (2024, "Parliament passed the 26th Constitutional Amendment in October 2024, stripping the Supreme Court of suo motu powers, capping the Chief Justice's tenure at three years, and giving a parliamentary committee (effectively the executive) the power to select the next Chief Justice from among the three most senior judges. Critics, including the International Commission of Jurists, called it a severe blow to judicial independence and a vehicle for executive-military control over the courts. The amendment matters for risk analysts because it weakens an independent check on government and military power precisely as Pakistan navigates IMF program conditionality that relies on credible institutions.", 'Dawn / ICJ', 'https://www.dawn.com/news/1866480'), (2025, "Between mid-2024 and mid-2025, all three major rating agencies upgraded Pakistan's sovereign rating on the back of IMF-supported fiscal consolidation: Moody's moved from Caa2 to Caa1 and then to B3 (August 2025), while Fitch and S&P both reached 'B-'. Agencies cited rebuilding foreign exchange reserves, improved debt affordability from lower domestic financing costs, and steady progress under the EFF program. The upgrades signal genuine near-term stabilization but leave Pakistan still deep in speculative-grade territory, dependent on continued IMF discipline and external rollovers from China, Saudi Arabia, and the UAE.", "Bloomberg / Moody's Ratings", 'https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2025-08-13/moody-s-upgrades-pakistan-rating-as-financial-conditions-improve'), (2025, 'In November 2025, President Zardari signed the 27th Constitutional Amendment, creating a new post of Chief of Defence Forces held concurrently by Army Chief Asim Munir, elevating him to Field Marshal for life with command over the nuclear Strategic Plans Division and president-like immunity from prosecution; removing him would require a two-thirds parliamentary majority versus a simple majority to remove an elected PM. Passed amid opposition protests from PTI, it formalizes military primacy over elected government at the constitutional level. For sovereign risk purposes, it cements a governance structure in which real power sits with an unaccountable military authority, raising long-run concerns about policy predictability and civilian oversight of economic decision-making.', 'Chatham House / Malay Mail', 'https://www.chathamhouse.org/2025/12/pakistans-27th-constitutional-amendment-moves-it-one-step-closer-authoritarian-rule')],
    'PSE': [
        (1994, "The Paris Protocol on Economic Relations, signed 29 April 1994 as Annex V to the Gaza-Jericho Agreement (part of the Oslo Accords), locked the Palestinian economy into a customs union with Israel: Israel controls all external borders and collects import taxes, VAT, and tariffs on Palestine's behalf, remitting them monthly as 'clearance revenue.' This framework -- now over three decades old, well past its original interim-period expiration -- still structures nearly all Palestinian trade and PA fiscal revenue today.", "Wikipedia (Protocol on Economic Relations) / B'Tselem", 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Protocol_on_Economic_Relations'),
        (2007, "A violent split between Fatah and Hamas fractured Palestinian governance into two separate administrations: the Fatah-led Palestinian Authority retained control of the West Bank while Hamas seized full control of the Gaza Strip after ousting Fatah forces. This division has never been durably reversed despite repeated 'unity government' attempts, and directly shapes the fragmented governance picture still unresolved in 2026.", 'Wikipedia / Council on Foreign Relations background', 'https://www.cfr.org/backgrounder/hamas'),
        (2025, "A joint World Bank-UN-EU Interim Rapid Damage and Needs Assessment (February 2025) put Gaza and West Bank reconstruction and recovery needs at roughly $53 billion, with about $30 billion in direct physical damage alone. A subsequent 'Final Gaza Rapid Damage and Needs Assessment' (released 2026) raised the estimate to $71.4 billion over a decade -- $35.2 billion in physical infrastructure damage plus $22.7 billion in economic/social losses -- including $26.3 billion needed in just the first 18 months, against a backdrop of over 371,000 housing units destroyed or damaged and the Gaza economy having contracted an estimated 84% cumulatively.", 'World Bank / UN / EU -- Gaza & West Bank Damage and Needs Assessments', 'https://www.worldbank.org/en/news/press-release/2025/02/18/new-report-assesses-damages-losses-and-needs-in-gaza-and-the-west-bank'),
        (2025, "Israel and Hamas signed a ceasefire agreement on 9 October 2025 in Egypt, implementing the first phase of a US-brokered 20-point plan; hostilities paused on 10 October, Hamas released its remaining living hostages, and Israel freed close to 2,000 Palestinian prisoners/detainees. World leaders including Trump, and the leaders of Turkiye, Egypt, and Qatar convened at a Sharm el-Sheikh summit on 13 October 2025 to formalize the arrangement, though the plan's later phases (disarmament, governance handover, reconstruction financing) remained unresolved well into 2026.", 'NPR / Times of Israel', 'https://www.npr.org/2025/10/13/g-s1-93207/hamas-releasing-israeli-hostages'),
        (2026, "Under UN Security Council Resolution 2803 (adopted alongside the ceasefire framework), a transitional 'National Committee for the Administration of Gaza' (NCAG) was established in January 2026 to eventually take over civilian administration from Hamas, overseen by a newly created, US-chaired 'Board of Peace.' In July 2026 Hamas announced it was dissolving its Gaza government to clear the way for this handover -- a move Israeli officials publicly disputed as incomplete, since Hamas retains disputed military capacity and Israel still holds roughly 70% of Gaza territory behind the ceasefire line.", 'Al Jazeera / Euronews', 'https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2026/7/6/hamas-announces-dissolution-of-gaza-governing-body'),
    ],
    'QAT': [(2017, 'On June 5, 2017, Saudi Arabia, the UAE, Bahrain, and Egypt severed diplomatic relations with Qatar and imposed a land, sea, and air blockade, accusing Doha of supporting terrorism and being too close to Iran -- a charge Qatar denied. Qatari imports fell by roughly 40% immediately after the blockade began, forcing Doha to reroute trade through Oman, Turkey, and Iran and to accelerate domestic food self-sufficiency projects. Qatar weathered the crisis by drawing on its sovereign wealth fund and diversifying supply chains, but the episode remains the starkest illustration of intra-GCC political risk to Qatari trade and investment flows.', 'Brookings', 'https://www.brookings.edu/articles/the-high-cost-of-high-stakes-economic-implications-of-the-2017-gulf-crisis/'), (2020, "The US and the Taliban signed the Doha Agreement in Qatar on February 29, 2020, under which Washington committed to a full troop withdrawal from Afghanistan within 14 months in exchange for Taliban counterterrorism guarantees and participation in intra-Afghan talks. The deal cemented Qatar's role as the preferred neutral venue and mediator for US dealings with groups Washington will not engage directly, a diplomatic function it later extended to hostage negotiations and evacuation logistics after the 2021 fall of Kabul. Doha's mediation 'brand' has become a genuine soft-power and diplomatic-insurance asset, complementing its LNG-based economic weight.", 'Washington Post', 'https://www.washingtonpost.com/world/asia_pacific/afghanistan-us-taliban-peace-deal-signing/2020/02/29/b952fb04-5a67-11ea-8efd-0f904bdd8057_story.html'), (2022, "Qatar hosted the FIFA World Cup from November to December 2022, the culmination of roughly $220 billion in infrastructure spending over the twelve years since its 2010 hosting award, including a new metro system, airport expansion, seven new stadiums, and an entirely new city at Lusail -- though most of this spending was tied to Qatar's broader National Vision 2030 development plan rather than the tournament alone. The event drew sustained international criticism over migrant-worker deaths and labor conditions under the kafala system, prompting reforms including a minimum wage and partial abolition of exit permits. It left Qatar with a permanently expanded, though partly underutilized, tourism and events infrastructure base.", 'Forbes', 'https://www.forbes.com/sites/mattcraig/2022/11/19/the-money-behind-the-most-expensive-world-cup-in-history-qatar-2022-by-the-numbers/'), (2023, "QatarEnergy's North Field expansion -- comprising the North Field East and North Field South projects, developed with TotalEnergies, Shell, ExxonMobil, ConocoPhillips, Eni, and Chinese state oil majors -- is set to lift Qatar's LNG production capacity from 77 to 126 million tonnes per annum by 2026, with a further North Field West phase targeting 142 mtpa by 2030. The expansion, the largest in Qatar's LNG history, is designed to lock in Qatar's position as a top global LNG exporter even as Australia and the US ramp up rival capacity. It represents the central pillar of Qatar's long-term revenue strategy and underwrites the Qatar Investment Authority's continued overseas asset growth.", 'Offshore Technology', 'https://www.offshore-technology.com/projects/north-field-expansion-project/'), (2023, "Within 48 hours of Hamas's October 7, 2023 attack on Israel, Qatar activated its long-standing back-channel relationship with Hamas's political leadership (hosted in Doha since 2012 at US request) to begin mediating a hostage-release and ceasefire framework alongside Egypt and the United States. A Qatari-mediated four-day truce beginning November 24, 2023 secured the release of 50 Israeli hostages and 150 Palestinian prisoners, later extended two additional days. Qatar's mediation role has drawn both international praise for enabling releases and criticism over its hosting of Hamas figures, illustrating the double-edged nature of Doha's 'indispensable mediator' strategy.", 'Human Security Centre', 'https://www.hscentre.org/latest-articles/qatars-role-2023-2024-israel-hamas-war/'), (2025, "Qatari, Egyptian, and US mediation produced a phased Gaza ceasefire and hostage-prisoner exchange that took effect January 19, 2025 and held (with interruptions) through much of the year, with Qatar and Egypt continuing joint mediation efforts into mid-2025 toward a further ceasefire framework. Doha's continued centrality to Gaza diplomacy reinforced its unique geopolitical utility to Washington despite periodic friction, particularly after Israel's unprecedented direct strike on Hamas leadership inside Qatar later in 2025.", 'NPR', 'https://www.npr.org/2025/03/12/nx-s1-5325721/israel-hamas-qatar-hostages-mediation'), (2025, "On September 9, 2025, Israeli fighter jets struck a Qatari government residential compound in Doha's Leqtaifiya district, targeting senior Hamas leaders meeting to discuss a US ceasefire proposal -- the first known Israeli attack on Qatari soil and the first direct strike by Israel on a GCC member state. The strike killed several Hamas officials and a Qatari security officer but failed to kill its primary targets; Qatar called it 'state terrorism,' and the attack drew international condemnation, including from the US. The episode was a watershed for Gulf security perceptions, undermining the assumption that hosting US-aligned mediators and a major US airbase (Al Udeid) guaranteed immunity from regional military action, with lasting implications for Qatar's risk premium and defense posture.", 'CNN', 'https://www.cnn.com/2025/09/10/middleeast/israel-strikes-hamas-qatar-explainer-intl-hnk'), (2018, "In July 2018 Moody's revised Qatar's outlook back to stable from negative, affirming the Aa3 rating and citing the resilience of Qatar's credit metrics through the first 13 months of the Saudi-led blockade -- reversing the negative-outlook action agencies had taken in mid-2017. The reversal signaled that Qatar's LNG revenue base and QIA reserves were sufficient to absorb the diplomatic rupture without lasting fiscal damage. It became an early proof point for Qatar's economic resilience narrative that shaped its 2021 blockade-ending negotiating position.", 'The Peninsula Qatar', 'https://thepeninsulaqatar.com/editorial/15/07/2018/Strong-credit-profile'), (2020, "In May 2020 the Qatar Investment Authority was reported to be in talks for a $7.6bn loan as the pandemic-driven oil and gas price collapse pressured even well-capitalized Gulf sovereign funds' liquidity. QIA's total AUM stood at roughly $295bn that year, illustrating that even Qatar's deep reserves needed active liquidity management during the 2020 shock. It highlighted how commodity-linked funding needs can surface even in the strongest-rated Gulf sovereigns during synchronized global downturns.", 'Al Jazeera', 'https://www.aljazeera.com/economy/2020/5/6/qatars-sovereign-wealth-fund-in-talks-for-7-6bn-loan-sources'), (2021, "QIA's balance sheet reached a record estimated $445bn by the end of 2021 on strong public and private equity returns, up sharply from roughly $295bn the prior year. The rebound reflected the broader 2021 global asset-price recovery and reinforced Qatar's position as one of the world's largest sovereign investors relative to population size. This scale of external assets remains a core reason Qatar's sovereign ratings stayed resilient through subsequent regional shocks.", 'Global SWF', 'https://globalswf.com/news/qia-reaches-us-445-billion-aum-gets-ready-to-take-off'), (2024, "In January 2024 Moody's upgraded Qatar's sovereign rating one notch to Aa2 from Aa3, citing marked improvement in fiscal metrics achieved over 2021-2023 on the back of high LNG prices and fiscal consolidation. The upgrade came as Qatar was simultaneously expanding LNG capacity and taking on a larger regional mediation role (Gaza, Hamas talks). It marked Qatar's strongest rating position of the past decade, underscoring the link between hydrocarbon windfalls and improved fiscal buffers.", 'Trading Economics', 'https://tradingeconomics.com/qatar/credit-rating/news/400986'), (2024, "In February 2024 Qatar's energy minister announced a further expansion of LNG production capacity by 16 million tonnes per annum for 2029-30, bringing total planned capacity to 142 mtpa -- an 84% increase from 2024 levels once fully built. Combined with the North Field East and South expansions, this cements Qatar's position as the world's largest LNG exporter for the coming decade. The scale of committed capital expenditure is a central input into Qatar's medium-term fiscal and current-account projections.", 'Rigzone', 'https://www.rigzone.com/insights/global-industry-insights-7/how-is-qatar-investing-in-lng-production-expansion-902'), (2025, "The first Train of the $13bn North Field East (NFE) expansion achieved first LNG in November 2025, ramping toward its 8 mtpa nameplate capacity through the first half of 2026, with Train 2 targeted for mid-2026. This is the first tranche of capacity actually coming online from the North Field expansion program announced in 2021-2024. Delivery on schedule is a key variable rating agencies watch given how central LNG volumes are to Qatar's revenue and current-account outlook.", 'The Middle East Insider', 'https://themiddleeastinsider.com/2026/04/22/qatar-lng-north-field-expansion-2026/')],
    'SAU': [(2011, "In February-March 2011, amid the Arab Spring, King Abdullah announced a package of social spending eventually totaling roughly $130 billion over several years, including public-sector salary increases, new housing units, and job creation, while simultaneously banning public demonstrations and deploying security forces to deter unrest. The measures, combined with a small troop contribution to the Saudi-led Peninsula Shield intervention in Bahrain the same month, preempted any large-scale domestic uprising. The episode illustrates the kingdom's default playbook for managing political risk: substituting fiscal largesse and repression for structural reform, a pattern still visible in the funding of Vision 2030 giga-projects today.", 'Al Jazeera', 'https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2011/3/18/saudi-monarch-announces-billions-in-handouts'), (2019, "On September 14, 2019, drone and missile strikes attributed by Washington and Riyadh to Iran (claimed by Yemen's Houthi movement) hit Saudi Aramco's Abqaiq processing facility and the Khurais oil field, temporarily knocking out roughly 5.7 million barrels per day, more than half of Saudi output and about 5% of global supply. Oil prices spiked by double digits in the aftermath, one of the largest sudden disruptions to global oil supply in decades, before Aramco restored production within weeks. The attack exposed the vulnerability of the kingdom's oil infrastructure to regional conflict just months before its landmark IPO, a risk factor that still shadows investor assessments of Saudi energy assets.", 'CNBC', 'https://www.cnbc.com/2019/09/20/oil-drone-attack-damage-revealed-at-saudi-aramco-facility.html'), (2019, "Saudi Aramco listed on the Tadawul exchange in December 2019 in the world's largest IPO to date, selling 1.5% of its shares to raise $25.6 billion and valuing the company at roughly $1.7 trillion. The listing was the centerpiece of Crown Prince Mohammed bin Salman's Vision 2030 strategy, intended to channel oil wealth into the Public Investment Fund (PIF) to finance economic diversification. It cemented Aramco as a hybrid instrument of state finance and capital-markets development, a template later followed by ADNOC Gas in the UAE.", 'CNN Business', 'https://edition.cnn.com/2019/12/05/investing/saudi-aramco-ipo-price'), (2020, "The collapse of OPEC+ talks in March 2020 triggered a Saudi-Russia oil price war just as COVID-19 lockdowns crushed demand, sending Brent crude briefly negative and blowing a hole in the kingdom's budget. In response, Riyadh tripled VAT from 5% to 15% effective July 1, 2020, suspended a cost-of-living allowance for public employees, and faced a projected record fiscal deficit near $112 billion for the year. The triple shock -- oil crash, pandemic, and fiscal repair -- forced the most aggressive austerity measures since the 2015-16 downturn and previewed the recurring tension between Vision 2030 spending ambitions and oil-price volatility.", 'CNBC', 'https://www.cnbc.com/2020/07/02/saudi-spending-soared-ahead-of-vat-but-triple-whammy-awaits-economy.html'), (2021, 'On January 5, 2021, Saudi Arabia, the UAE, Bahrain, and Egypt signed the Al-Ula Declaration ending the three-and-a-half-year blockade of Qatar that began in June 2017, restoring diplomatic ties and reopening land, sea, and air links. The reconciliation, brokered with US mediation, removed a major source of intra-GCC economic friction and paved the way for renewed Saudi-Qatari trade and investment flows. It signaled a broader Saudi foreign-policy pivot toward regional de-escalation that would later extend to Iran and, tentatively, Israel.', 'CNBC', 'https://www.cnbc.com/2021/01/06/end-of-qatar-blockade-is-a-win-for-the-region-saudi-foreign-minister.html'), (2023, "On March 10, 2023, Saudi Arabia and Iran agreed in Beijing-brokered talks to restore diplomatic relations after a seven-year rupture, reactivating a 2001 security cooperation pact. The China-mediated detente reduced the perceived risk of direct Saudi-Iran confrontation and was read by markets as lowering geopolitical risk premia on Gulf assets, even as proxy tensions in Yemen and Lebanon persisted. It also underscored Beijing's growing diplomatic weight in a region traditionally mediated by Washington, a shift relevant to Saudi Arabia's increasingly multi-aligned foreign policy.", 'International Crisis Group', 'https://www.crisisgroup.org/qna/middle-east-north-africa-saudi-arabia-iran-china/how-beijing-helped-riyadh-and-tehran-reach-detente'), (2024, "Bloomberg reported in April 2024 that Saudi Arabia had sharply scaled back the near-term ambitions of NEOM's flagship 'The Line' project, cutting the initially planned 170-kilometer linear city to roughly 2.4 kilometers by 2030 and reducing expected 2030 residents from 1.5 million to under 300,000, with the PIF yet to approve NEOM's 2024 budget. Further reporting through 2025 described workforce cuts, a construction freeze on large sections, and deferral of the full vision to a multi-decade timeline extending toward 2045. The scale-back is the clearest evidence yet that giga-project financing, oil-revenue constraints, and delivery timelines are colliding, a central risk to the credibility of Vision 2030.", 'Bloomberg', 'https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2024-04-05/saudis-scale-back-ambition-for-1-5-trillion-desert-project-neom'), (2024, "FIFA confirmed Saudi Arabia as sole host of the 2034 men's World Cup by acclamation on December 11, 2024, awarding the tournament despite human-rights groups warning of an 'unimaginable human cost' tied to migrant labor conditions. The award locks in another decade of state-directed infrastructure and tourism investment layered on top of Vision 2030 giga-projects, increasing the kingdom's exposure to construction-sector execution risk and reputational scrutiny. It also extends the pattern, seen with the Aramco IPO and NEOM, of using mega-events to signal economic transformation to international investors.", 'Al Jazeera', 'https://www.aljazeera.com/sports/2024/12/11/saudi-arabias-hosting-of-fifa-world-cup-2034-what-to-know-about-the-event'), (2025, 'With oil prices below the roughly $85-90/bbl fiscal breakeven Riyadh needs, Saudi Arabia raised $20 billion in international sovereign bonds during 2025, on top of a $37 billion borrowing plan announced in January to keep funding giga-projects, pushing public debt to about SAR 1.52 trillion ($405 billion) by end-2025 from SAR 1.22 trillion a year earlier. The PIF separately signed a $7 billion Islamic syndicated loan with 20 banks in early 2025. The rising reliance on debt markets rather than oil revenue or reserve drawdowns marks a structural shift in how Vision 2030 is financed, with net bond issuance forecast to hit a record $25 billion in 2026.', 'Bloomberg / AGBI', 'https://www.agbi.com/analysis/economy/2026/01/economists-unfazed-as-saudi-arabias-debt-rises-again/'), (2016, "S&P and Fitch cut Saudi Arabia's sovereign rating (S&P to A+, Fitch to A+) as the 2014-16 oil price collapse blew out the fiscal deficit and drained reserves, marking a three-notch cumulative downgrade since the oil slump began. The downgrades reflected doubts that the kingdom's reform agenda could offset deteriorating public and external balance sheets. It underscored how exposed Saudi credit metrics remain to oil-price swings even as Vision 2030 was being launched the same year.", 'Gulf News', 'https://gulfnews.com/amp/story/business%2Fsaudi-arabia-credit-rating-cut-by-sp-after-oil-prices-sink-1.1610436'), (2016, "Crown Prince Mohammed bin Salman launched Vision 2030 in April 2016, an 84-page plan to end Saudi Arabia's dependence on oil revenue by expanding the Public Investment Fund (PIF) and privatizing state assets including a stake in Aramco. The plan set the template for the following decade of megaprojects, PIF-led diversification and social liberalization. It remains the reference point against which the kingdom's fiscal credibility and reform delivery are judged by rating agencies and investors today.", 'PIF', 'https://www.pif.gov.sa/en/news-and-insights/press-releases/2016/vision-2030-reform-plan-announced/'), (2017, "In November 2017 MBS launched an anti-corruption purge, detaining roughly 380 royals, ministers and businessmen (including Prince Alwaleed bin Talal) at Riyadh's Ritz-Carlton hotel; the government said it seized over $100bn in settlements. While framed as anti-corruption, it was widely seen as a consolidation of power that unsettled private investors and the business elite. It signaled the concentration of economic decision-making around MBS that continues to define Saudi political risk.", 'Al Jazeera', 'https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2018/1/30/saudi-arabia-seizes-more-than-100bn-in-settlements'), (2018, "The October 2018 killing of journalist Jamal Khashoggi at the Saudi consulate in Istanbul triggered international sanctions threats, arms-sale suspensions by countries including Denmark, and a business boycott of that year's Future Investment Initiative forum. The Tadawul stock exchange saw turbulent trading and several Western firms distanced themselves from Riyadh. The episode remains a reference case for reputational and governance risk in Saudi sovereign-risk assessments, particularly around human-rights-linked capital flight and ESG screening.", 'CSIS', 'https://www.csis.org/analysis/after-killing-jamal-khashoggi-muhammad-bin-salman-and-future-saudi-us-relations'), (2026, "Saudi Arabia's Ministry of Finance projected the 2026 fiscal deficit would narrow to about SAR165bn (3.3% of GDP) from SAR245bn in 2025, even as OPEC+ unwinds 2.2 million bpd of prior production cuts and independent estimates (e.g., Goldman Sachs) put the true deficit closer to 6.6% of GDP. The gap between Saudi Arabia's fiscal breakeven oil price (roughly $90-96/bbl) and prevailing prices near $60-66/bbl has forced continued bond issuance and scaled-back megaproject spending (NEOM, Mukaab). This structural breakeven gap is the single largest driver of Saudi sovereign risk through the medium term.", 'AGBI / Al Jazeera', 'https://www.agbi.com/opinion/economy/2025/12/saudi-arabias-policy-trilemma-oil-debt-and-deficits-in-2026/')],
    'SYR': [(2011, "Peaceful protests that began in Damascus and Daraa in March 2011, fueled by a severe 2006-2010 drought and long-standing economic grievances under Bashar al-Assad, met a harsh military crackdown that by late 2011 had escalated into civil war. Real GDP fell an estimated 40-50% between 2011 and 2013 alone as the conflict destroyed physical capital, broke apart trade networks, and drove out foreign investment; the Syrian pound, worth about 47 to the dollar before the war, began a depreciation that would eventually run into the thousands. The uprising set in motion Syria's transformation from a lower-middle-income economy into one of the most conflict-devastated, sanctioned economies in the world.", 'World Bank', 'https://thedocs.worldbank.org/en/doc/ebb9b060753b7019705d1dafe9fe2e35-0280032021/original/April-22-Harun-OnderA-Decade-of-War-in-Syria-The-Economic-Side.pdf'), (2015, "By the mid-2010s the war had displaced more than half of Syria's pre-conflict population of roughly 21 million, pushing millions of refugees into Turkey, Lebanon and Jordan while others were internally displaced; more than 100,000 people had died. Destruction of oil infrastructure, agriculture and tourism -- historically major foreign-currency earners -- left Syria's GDP down by more than half between 2010 and 2020, and the government grew increasingly dependent on Iranian and Russian financial and military support to survive. The scale of displacement created one of the defining refugee crises of the 21st century and reshaped neighboring Lebanon's and Jordan's economies and politics.", 'Context / Thomson Reuters Foundation', 'https://www.context.news/socioeconomic-inclusion/syria-the-war-in-numbers'), (2020, "The U.S. Caesar Syria Civilian Protection Act took effect in June 2020, dramatically expanding secondary sanctions on anyone -- including firms in Gulf states or Lebanon -- doing business with the Assad government's oil, aviation, construction and engineering sectors. Banking channels to Syria were effectively severed and external investment and construction activity collapsed; child labor rose sharply, from 6.4% of children aged 5-17 in 2020 to 8.2% by 2023 (roughly 80,000 additional child workers), as households lost income. The Act also destabilized Lebanon's economy given the two countries' close banking and trade ties, showing how sanctions on Damascus radiated through the wider region.", 'Peoples Dispatch', 'https://peoplesdispatch.org/2025/12/23/five-years-of-coercion-what-did-caesar-do-to-syrians/'), (2023, "Compounded by the war and Lebanon's own 2019 financial collapse (Syrian elites had parked substantial wealth in Lebanese banks), the Syrian pound fell from roughly 1,200 to the dollar in January 2020 to several thousand by the early 2020s, hitting fresh record lows on the parallel market in mid-2023. Years of monetizing widening budget deficits between 2017 and 2019 had already fed the depreciation. The currency collapse eroded ordinary Syrians' purchasing power for imported staples just as sanctions and war damage curtailed domestic production, deepening poverty that by the mid-2020s affected roughly 90% of the population.", 'Al Jazeera', 'https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2023/7/19/syrian-pound-sinks-to-historic-low-on-the-parallel-market'), (2024, "In an 11-day offensive, Hayat Tahrir al-Sham (HTS) and allied factions swept out of Idlib and captured Damascus on December 8, 2024, ending Bashar al-Assad's 24-year rule as he fled to Russia; HTS leader Ahmed al-Sharaa was named interim president in January 2025 and a new transitional government took office in April 2025. The takeover of a sanctioned, war-shattered economy left Syria with an estimated $20-30bn in external debt -- the bulk (variously put at $16-50bn) owed to Iran and roughly $1.2bn to Russia -- alongside a shattered currency and a state apparatus requiring wholesale rebuilding. The transition immediately became the central variable determining whether Western and Gulf sanctions relief and reconstruction financing would materialize.", 'House of Commons Library / Amwaj Media', 'https://commonslibrary.parliament.uk/syria-what-is-the-situation-five-months-after-assads-fall/'), (2025, "Days after Assad's fall, sectarian violence erupted along Syria's coast: an ambush of new security forces by pro-Assad Alawite fighters on March 6, 2025 triggered a crackdown by government-aligned militias that killed an estimated 1,000-1,400+ people, mostly Alawite civilians, between March 6-17 -- an episode the UN later found to be widespread and systematic. A second wave of intercommunal violence struck the Druze-majority Suwayda province in July 2025, killing over 1,400 people amid clashes between government forces, Bedouin tribes and Druze factions. Both episodes badly damaged the transitional government's credibility with Western governments and investors weighing sanctions relief and reconstruction financing.", 'Amnesty International / OHCHR', 'https://www.ohchr.org/en/press-releases/2025/08/un-syria-commission-finds-march-coastal-violence-was-widespread-and'), (2025, "President Trump announced on May 13, 2025 that Washington would lift sanctions on Syria, followed by a Treasury general license and a Caesar Act waiver, then Executive Order 14312 on June 30 revoking most U.S. Syria sanctions; on December 18, 2025 Trump signed legislation formally repealing the Caesar Act itself. The EU had lifted most of its own Syria sanctions in May 2025, and the World Bank cleared roughly $15.5m in Syrian arrears to IDA in May 2025 (paid by Saudi Arabia and Qatar), restoring Syria's eligibility for new World Bank financing after more than a decade. The rapid unwinding of one of the world's most extensive sanctions regimes marked one of 2025's most consequential shifts in Middle East political economy.", 'Curtis LLP / World Bank', 'https://www.curtis.com/our-firm/news/u-s-repeals-the-caesar-act-in-latest-move-to-ease-syria-sanctions'), (2025, "The World Bank estimated in an October 21, 2025 report that Syria's post-conflict reconstruction needs range from $140bn to $345bn, with a best estimate of $216bn, reflecting roughly a third of pre-war gross capital stock destroyed, led by $52bn in infrastructure damage and $33bn in residential housing damage. An IMF staff team visited Damascus in November 2025 (its first mission since 2009) and again in February 2026 to help design fiscal and financial-sector reforms, while the IMF projected Syria's economy could grow more than 10% in 2026 on the back of returning refugees (roughly 1.5 million by 2025-26), agricultural recovery and expanding oil and gas output. The reconstruction bill dwarfs anything Gulf donors, Turkey or Western institutions have yet committed, making the financing gap the central open question for Syria's medium-term risk profile.", 'World Bank / The New Arab', 'https://www.worldbank.org/en/news/press-release/2025/10/21/syria-s-post-conflict-reconstruction-costs-estimated-at-216-billion'), (2021, 'The Syrian pound lost about 24% of its value in 2021 alone, following an April 2021 devaluation that came after the central bank governor was dismissed. This accelerated an already decade-long slide (the pound depreciated roughly 270-fold against the dollar between 2011 and 2023) driven by war costs, sanctions, and loss of oil and trade revenue. The currency collapse eroded real wages and savings for ordinary Syrians and entrenched a parallel/black-market FX system that persisted through the fall of the Assad government.', 'Arab Center Washington DC / Grokipedia', 'https://arabcenterdc.org/resource/the-collapse-of-the-syrian-pound-may-doom-the-syrian-economy/'), (2023, "The Syrian pound's collapse accelerated dramatically in 2023, losing roughly 141% of its value on the parallel market over the year and trading above 11,000 to the dollar by mid-year, versus about 1,200 in early 2020. Combined with the Caesar Act sanctions regime and the February 2023 earthquake, the currency crash pushed the vast majority of the population into poverty ahead of the Assad government's eventual collapse. It left the incoming HTS-led transitional authorities in December 2024 to inherit a nearly worthless currency and a shattered banking system.", 'Al Jazeera / Jusoor', 'https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2023/7/19/syrian-pound-sinks-to-historic-low-on-the-parallel-market'), (2025, "Beyond the Trump administration's sanctions-lifting announcement, the EU separately lifted most of its economic sanctions on Syria in May 2025, and Saudi Arabia and Qatar cleared Syria's arrears to the World Bank's International Development Association, restoring World Bank funding eligibility after a 14-year suspension. The World Bank then approved a $146 million emergency electricity grant in June 2025, with Syria seeking roughly $1 billion in support over three years. This reintegration into multilateral finance is a precondition for any meaningful reconstruction financing, though disbursement remains contingent on the transitional government's stability and governance track record.", 'World Bank / The National', 'https://www.thenationalnews.com/business/economy/2026/08/04/syrias-economic-recovery-accelerating-but-poverty-remains-widespread-imf-says/'), (2025, "In July 2025, days of sectarian clashes between Druze militias and Bedouin tribes in the southern city of Sweida killed at least 638 people according to the Syrian Observatory for Human Rights, drawing direct Israeli air strikes on Syrian government military headquarters in Damascus and on pro-government forces moving toward Sweida. A US-brokered ceasefire between Syria's interim authorities and Druze leaders followed, but the episode -- distinct from the earlier 2025 coastal Alawite violence -- exposed the transitional government's inability to control sectarian violence and Israel's willingness to intervene militarily inside Syria to protect minority communities. It signals continued fragmentation risk and the potential for renewed Israeli military involvement as a wildcard in Syria's post-Assad stabilization.", 'CNN / UN News', 'https://www.cnn.com/2025/07/15/middleeast/israel-strikes-syria-sectarian-clashes-druze-intl'), (2026, "By August 2026, the IMF described Syria's economic recovery as 'accelerating,' with growth benefiting from sanctions relief, improved rainfall boosting agriculture, expanding hydrocarbon output, and rising trade and services activity, and projected double-digit GDP growth for 2026 despite continued regional conflict. The IMF nonetheless stressed that poverty remains widespread and called for deeper structural reforms. The divergence between headline GDP recovery and persistent poverty is a key signal for assessing whether Syria's post-war rebound is broad-based or concentrated among elites and reconstruction-linked sectors.", 'IMF / The National', 'https://www.thenationalnews.com/business/economy/2026/08/04/syrias-economic-recovery-accelerating-but-poverty-remains-widespread-imf-says/')],
    'TUN': [(2011, "Tunisia's Jasmine Revolution (December 2010-January 2011) forced President Zine El Abidine Ben Ali to flee on January 14, 2011 after 23 years in power, becoming the spark for the wider Arab Spring across the region. The revolution ushered in a 2014 constitution and a decade of democratic transition, but chronic political instability, weak governments, and a bloated public payroll left Tunisia's underlying fiscal and current-account weaknesses unaddressed — vulnerabilities that would resurface acutely after 2020 and remain the core of Tunisia's sovereign risk today.", 'Journal of Democracy / Wikipedia', 'https://www.journalofdemocracy.org/articles/coup-in-tunisia-is-democracy-lost/'), (2015, "Terrorist attacks on the Bardo National Museum (March 18, 2015, 22 dead) and a Sousse-area beach resort (June 26, 2015, 38 dead, mostly European tourists) devastated Tunisia's tourism sector, which directly contributes over 7% of GDP and around 15% including indirect effects. Tourism revenue fell by roughly a third in 2015, at least 23 hotels closed with about 2,400 layoffs, and the Sousse attack alone was estimated to cost the sector $500 million — a stark illustration of how security shocks can rapidly convert into current-account and employment crises in a tourism-dependent economy.", 'Al Jazeera', 'https://www.aljazeera.com/economy/2015/7/29/tunisias-tourism-struggling-one-month-after-massacre'), (2016, "Tunisia signed a four-year, roughly $2.9 billion IMF Extended Fund Facility in 2016 to address post-revolution fiscal imbalances, requiring subsidy cuts and public-wage restraint that proved politically difficult to implement fully amid a fragile multi-party governing coalition. The program's incomplete implementation set a precedent for the credibility gap between Tunisia's IMF commitments and actual delivery that culminated in the collapse of talks under President Kais Saied in 2022-2023.", 'IMF Country Report / Bretton Woods Project', 'https://www.brettonwoodsproject.org/2018/07/the-political-cost-of-imf-programmes/'), (2021, "On July 25, 2021, President Kais Saied invoked Article 80 of the constitution to freeze parliament, strip lawmakers' immunity, and dismiss the government, followed by a September 2021 decree granting himself rule-by-decree powers — widely termed a 'self-coup' that dismantled the 2014 constitutional order. The move, initially popular amid pandemic-era governance paralysis, entrenched one-man rule and removed the institutional checks (independent parliament, competing party coalitions) that international creditors and rating agencies typically view as supportive of reform credibility, contributing to subsequent Moody's and Fitch downgrades.", 'Journal of Democracy / Al Jazeera', 'https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2021/12/13/tunisia-president-announces-referendum-and-elections'), (2023, "In April 2023, President Saied publicly rejected IMF 'diktats,' refusing to commit to subsidy cuts and restructuring of loss-making state-owned enterprises that the Fund had set as conditions for a roughly $1.9 billion Extended Fund Facility agreed in principle in October 2022. Talks have remained stalled since, compounded by the World Bank effectively suspending new lending after Saied's controversial 2023 comments about sub-Saharan migrants, leaving Tunisia locked out of both IMF and Eurobond financing and forcing reliance on domestic and central-bank funding instead.", 'Africanews / Foreign Policy', 'https://www.africanews.com/2023/04/07/tunisias-president-kais-saied-rejects-imf-diktats/'), (2023, "Facing default risk, Tunisia signed a July 2023 Memorandum of Understanding with the EU (backed by Italy and the Netherlands) worth about €1 billion in migration, budget, and energy support — including €105m for border/coastguard operations and €900m in longer-term aid — explicitly tied to curbing irregular migration across the central Mediterranean. The deal substituted EU migration-control money for the IMF financing Tunisia had forgone, but disbursement has been slow and contentious, illustrating how migration policy has become entangled with, and partly substitutes for, Tunisia's traditional multilateral financing channels.", 'Al Jazeera', 'https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2023/7/16/tunisia-and-eu-finalise-deal-on-migration'), (2024, "In February 2024 Tunisia's parliament amended a 2016 central-bank law to let the Central Bank of Tunisia directly finance the treasury with up to $2.25 billion, followed by a further $2.2 billion authorization in the December 2024 budget law — used mainly to service debt, including a $1 billion Eurobond maturing in January 2025. President Saied has framed this as 'financial sovereignty' from foreign (IMF) conditionality, having cut the foreign-debt share of the budget from 73.8% (2018) to 57.8% (2024), but critics warn central-bank monetization of the deficit risks inflation and undermines institutional independence — Tunisia's public debt passed 80% of GDP in 2025.", 'TIMEP / Carnegie Endowment', 'https://timep.org/2025/02/25/tunisias-2025-financial-gambles-taxes-loans-and-the-central-bank/'), (2024, "Kais Saied was re-elected in October 2024 with over 90% of the vote amid a field narrowed by disqualifications and the jailing of rival candidates; weeks later he appointed Fethi Nouri as the new central bank governor after 27 MPs proposed legislation to curb the bank's independence further. The re-election cemented a governance model built around central-bank-financed deficits rather than IMF-style structural reform, leaving Tunisia's risk outlook hostage to whether domestic financing and olive-oil/textile export windfalls can keep covering debt service without a return to international capital markets.", 'Reuters / BTI 2026 Tunisia', 'https://bti-project.org/en/reports/country-report/TUN'), (2022, "On July 25, 2022, President Kais Saied's proposed constitution passed in a low-turnout (~31%) referendum, with about 95% voting yes; the new charter replaced Tunisia's semi-presidential system with a hyper-presidential one, sharply curbing parliament's powers. Boycotted by major opposition parties, the vote cemented the one-man rule Saied had begun with his July 2021 emergency powers and set the institutional framework -- a weakened legislature and judiciary -- under which subsequent IMF negotiations and opposition crackdowns have played out.", 'Human Rights Watch / France 24', 'https://www.hrw.org/news/2022/07/14/qa-tunisias-constitutional-referendum'), (2023, "Following President Saied's February 2023 speech blaming sub-Saharan African migrants for a conspiracy to alter Tunisia's demographic makeup, mob attacks, evictions and mass arrests of Black African migrants spread nationally, prompting African Union condemnation. Months later, in July 2023 the EU signed a €105m migration-control Memorandum of Understanding with Tunis; the episode damaged Tunisia's international reputation and highlighted how migration has become entangled with the country's economic-aid relationship with Europe.", 'Al Jazeera / Middle East Eye', 'https://www.middleeasteye.net/news/tunisia-sub-saharan-africans-eu-fuelled-migration-crackdown'), (2023, "Moody's downgraded Tunisia to Caa2 (from Caa1) with a negative outlook in January 2023 and Fitch held it at CCC-, both reflecting a substantial risk of default absent a new financing agreement; Fitch later upgraded Tunisia modestly to CCC+ in September 2024, citing the government's continued (if strained) commitment to servicing external debt without an IMF program. Tunisia avoided default by repaying all its 2023 obligations, but did so largely through costly domestic borrowing that squeezed bank financing available to the private sector.", 'Reuters/Zawya / Investing.com', 'https://www.zawya.com/en/economy/north-africa/fitch-upgrades-tunisias-rating-to-b-outlook-stable-g2gx5ctv'), (2025, "Beginning May 2024 and continuing through 2025, Tunisian courts prosecuted dozens of opposition politicians, lawyers, and activists -- including Jaouhar Ben Mbarek, Ahmed Nejib Chebbi, and Ghazi Chaouachi -- in the 'Conspiracy Case,' alleging a plot to overthrow President Saied under a charge carrying the death penalty; an April 2025 verdict sentenced 37 defendants to 4-66 years, with further mass convictions in November 2025. Rights groups (Amnesty, HRW) call the case emblematic of a broader collapse in judicial independence, a factor increasingly cited in country risk and governance assessments of Tunisia.", 'Human Rights Watch / Amnesty International', 'https://www.hrw.org/news/2025/04/22/tunisia-harsh-sentences-conspiracy-case-sham-trial')],
    'YEM': [(2011, "Mass protests against President Ali Abdullah Saleh's 33-year rule, part of the broader Arab Spring, forced Saleh to sign a Gulf Cooperation Council-brokered deal in Riyadh on November 23, 2011, transferring power to his deputy Abd Rabbuh Mansur Hadi in exchange for immunity from prosecution. The transition failed to resolve the underlying economic grievances -- unemployment, fuel-subsidy disputes, regional inequality -- that had fueled the uprising, and the resulting power vacuum allowed Houthi forces to expand from their northern stronghold and eventually seize the capital in 2014. Yemen's 2011-2014 transitional period is best understood, in hindsight, as a fragile interlude between one political crisis and a far deeper civil war.", 'Al Jazeera', 'https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2011/11/24/yemens-saleh-agrees-to-transfer-power-2'), (2014, "Houthi (Ansar Allah) forces seized the capital Sanaa in September 2014, and by early 2015 had pushed toward Aden, prompting a Saudi-led coalition to intervene militarily in March 2015 on behalf of the internationally recognized government. Yemen's economy shrank an estimated 28% in 2015 alone as oil output -- previously the government's main revenue source -- collapsed, marking the start of one of the world's worst humanitarian and economic crises. The war fractured Yemen into a Houthi-controlled north (including Sanaa) and an internationally recognized government in the south (based in Aden), a split that persists in 2026.", "Sana'a Center for Strategic Studies", 'https://sanaacenter.org/publications/main-publications/23729'), (2016, "As oil-export revenue collapsed and the war cut off normal financing, the government's Central Bank of Yemen branch in Aden began printing new banknotes in 2016 to pay salaries, sharply expanding the money supply and fueling inflation in government-held areas; separately, the IMF's September 2014, three-year, $552.9m Extended Credit Facility arrangement could not complete its first review once war broke out and effectively lapsed by March 2016. The rial, devalued from about 214 to 250 per dollar in 2015, was unofficially trading near 370 by 2017 before the peg collapsed altogether. These parallel monetary failures set the stage for the currency's eventual bifurcation between the Houthi north and government south.", "Sana'a Center for Strategic Studies / IMF", 'https://sanaacenter.org/publications/perspectives-and-analyses/8674'), (2020, "In late 2020, Houthi authorities in Sanaa banned the use of newly printed banknotes issued by the government's Aden-based central bank, restricting their circulation to government-held areas and formally splitting Yemen into two currency zones with sharply diverging exchange rates. The Houthi-controlled north, which retains most of Yemen's population centers and commercial hubs along with the bulk of the original central bank's staff and records, has generally maintained a more stable (if administratively restricted) exchange rate than the war-strained, salary-printing government zone in the south. This monetary partition remains one of the clearest economic expressions of Yemen's de facto territorial division.", 'Middle East Eye', 'https://www.middleeasteye.net/news/houthis-maintain-price-yemeni-currency-their-areas'), (2022, "A UN-brokered truce held from April to October 2022, but when it expired without renewal, Houthi forces pivoted to economic warfare, striking government-held oil export terminals at Ash Shihr and Dhabba with drones in October-November 2022 and forcing an almost complete halt to government oil production and exports. The shutdown eliminated the internationally recognized government's primary hard-currency revenue source, worsening its ability to pay salaries and accelerating rial depreciation in government-held areas even as fighting itself remained largely frozen. The episode showed how the conflict's center of gravity had shifted from battlefield maneuver to a fight over fiscal survival.", "Sana'a Center for Strategic Studies", 'https://sanaacenter.org/the-yemen-review/november-2022/19204'), (2023, "Following the Gaza war's outbreak in October 2023, the Houthis began attacking commercial shipping in the Red Sea and Bab al-Mandab Strait from November 2023 onward, striking dozens of vessels over the following year and prompting major carriers including Maersk to reroute around the Cape of Good Hope. Oil shipments through the Red Sea corridor -- normally carrying about 15% of global seaborne trade -- fell from 9.3 million bpd in 2023 to 4.1 million bpd in 2024, while Yemen's own port of Hodeidah saw roughly a 15% drop in traffic that further squeezed the flow of essential imports into an already import-dependent country. International naval missions, including the EU's Operation Aspides, were deployed to protect shipping but did not fully restore normal trade patterns.", 'Council on Foreign Relations / Washington Institute', 'https://www.cfr.org/global-conflict-tracker/conflict/war-yemen'), (2025, "Yemen's rial, after years of continuous depreciation, appreciated sharply from roughly 2,900 to about 1,600 per dollar between mid-July and early August 2025 after the government established a new National Committee for Regulating Foreign Exchange Institutions (NCRFI) in July 2025 to curb speculative currency trading. Headline inflation correspondingly eased from about 35% year-on-year in July to roughly 6.25% by September 2025. Despite this improvement, the IMF's 2025 Article IV consultation (concluded in early 2026) still projected a slight 2026 GDP contraction and noted that real per-capita GDP had fallen 58% since 2015, underscoring how fragile any recovery remains amid continued north-south economic fragmentation.", 'IMF / World Bank', 'https://www.worldbank.org/en/news/press-release/2025/06/02/economic-fragmentation-and-external-shocks-hamper-yemen-s-recovery-path'), (2021, "By 21 July 2021, the rial fell past 1,000 per US dollar in government-held southern Yemen for the first time, a symbolic and practical threshold that made basic food and fuel unaffordable for many households. The depreciation reflected the Central Bank of Yemen's depleted foreign-currency reserves, the loss of oil-export revenue, and the effective bifurcation of the currency after Houthi authorities banned new banknotes in Houthi-held areas in December 2019. The widening gap between the Houthi-controlled north's and the internationally recognized government's south's currency zones remains a core obstacle to any unified fiscal recovery.", "Sana'a Center for Strategic Studies", 'https://sanaacenter.org/publications/analysis/89'), (2023, "In February 2023, Saudi Arabia deposited $1 billion into the Central Bank of Yemen's Aden branch to shore up the internationally recognized government's finances and support the rial, part of a broader Gulf effort to stabilize Yemen's economy amid the fragile UN-mediated truce. The deposit illustrated Yemen's near-total dependence on external, discretionary Gulf financing to avoid outright fiscal collapse, rather than any underlying improvement in domestic revenue generation. Such external life-support remains a defining and precarious feature of Yemen's sovereign risk profile.", 'Al Jazeera', 'https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2023/2/22/saudi-arabia-puts-1-billion-in-yemens-aden-based-central-bank'), (2023, "Following China's March 2023 brokered rapprochement between Saudi Arabia and Iran, Oman-mediated direct talks between Riyadh and the Houthis advanced through 2023, producing a major prisoner exchange in April 2023 even as a comprehensive settlement remained elusive. The diplomatic track, distinct from the earlier UN-mediated truce, showed how the regional Saudi-Iran detente created space for bilateral de-escalation, though talks stalled on translating humanitarian steps into a political settlement. The unresolved three-phase roadmap (humanitarian, military/economic, political) continues to leave Yemen's governance and reunification prospects unsettled.", 'Stimson Center / Foreign Policy', 'https://www.stimson.org/2023/peace-for-yemen-elusive-despite-iran-saudi-deal-and-saudi-houthi-talks/'), (2024, "Between November 2023 and January 2025, Houthi forces attacked more than 100 merchant vessels in the Red Sea and Gulf of Aden, sinking two ships and killing four sailors, forcing major shipping lines to reroute around southern Africa away from a corridor carrying about 15% of global seaborne trade. The US and allies established naval task forces (Operation Prosperity Guardian) and conducted direct strikes on Houthi targets in Yemen in response. The crisis demonstrated the Houthis' capacity to impose global economic costs from Yemeni territory, complicating any resolution of Yemen's civil conflict from becoming purely a domestic matter.", 'Congressional Research Service / gCaptain', 'https://www.congress.gov/crs_external_products/IF/PDF/IF12581/IF12581.16.pdf'), (2026, "The Houthis had paused Red Sea shipping attacks following the October 2025 Gaza ceasefire, but resumed strikes on Israel after the Israel-US war with Iran began on 28 February 2026, again threatening Red Sea shipping lanes as a spillover front of the broader Iran conflict. This reaffirmed the Houthis' role as an Iran-aligned actor capable of dragging Yemen into wider regional confrontations regardless of the state of Yemen's own internal peace process. Renewed regional entanglement raises the risk premium on any Yemeni economic normalization or reunification effort tied to a durable Saudi-Houthi settlement.", 'gCaptain', 'https://gcaptain.com/houthis-signal-renewed-red-sea-shipping-attacks-after-u-s-israeli-strikes-on-iran/')],
}

HISTORICAL_CONTEXT['TUR'] = [
    (2016, "On the night of 15-16 July, a faction of the Turkish military attempted to seize power, deploying tanks and F-16s against parliament and other state buildings; more than 300 people were killed and over 2,100 injured before the coup collapsed. Erdogan blamed the network of exiled cleric Fethullah Gulen and launched purges that, by 2025, had seen roughly 390,000 people detained or arrested and more than 125,000 civil servants and soldiers dismissed, reshaping the judiciary, military and civil service and cementing Erdogan's personal control over the state.", "Wikipedia / Al Jazeera", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2016_Turkish_coup_d%27%C3%A9tat_attempt"),
    (2017, "The coup attempt accelerated Erdogan's alliance with the nationalist MHP, enabling a narrowly-won April 2017 constitutional referendum (51.4% yes) that abolished the office of prime minister and created an executive presidency with expanded powers over the judiciary, budget and senior appointments. The new system took full effect after the June 2018 elections, marking Turkey's most significant governance change since the 1980 constitution.", "Al Jazeera", "https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2026/7/15/how-failed-2016-coup-reshaped-turkiyes-and-civil-military-relations"),
    (2018, "A diplomatic dispute over detained US pastor Andrew Brunson triggered a currency collapse: the lira lost roughly 40% of its value against the dollar in 2018 after President Trump doubled steel and aluminum tariffs on Turkey and sanctioned two Turkish ministers, compounding pre-existing strains from corporate dollar debt and inflation of 16-25%. Erdogan, who called interest rates 'the mother and father of all evil,' initially resisted tightening, but the central bank ultimately hiked its benchmark rate to 24% in September 2018 to stem the rout, exposing the fragility of Turkey's foreign-currency-dependent growth model.", "CBS News / CNBC", "https://www.cbsnews.com/news/turkey-us-tariffs-court-rejects-pastor-andrew-brunson-appeal-turkish-lira-crisis/"),
    (2023, "Twin earthquakes of magnitude 7.8 and 7.5 struck southeastern Turkey on 6 February, Turkey's deadliest disaster in modern history. Turkey's disaster agency AFAD confirmed 50,096 deaths by 20 March 2023, while a World Bank rapid damage assessment estimated $34.2 billion in direct physical damage (about 4% of 2021 GDP) and displaced roughly 1.25 million people.", "World Bank / Turkish Minute", "https://www.worldbank.org/en/news/press-release/2023/02/27/earthquake-damage-in-turkiye-estimated-to-exceed-34-billion-world-bank-disaster-assessment-report"),
    (2023, "Erdogan won a third presidential term in a 28 May runoff, defeating opposition leader Kemal Kilicdaroglu 52.1% to 47.9% after neither candidate cleared 50% in the 14 May first round. The result extended his rule into a third decade despite the worst cost-of-living crisis of his tenure and criticism over the earthquake response.", "France 24 / CNN", "https://www.france24.com/en/europe/20230528-%F0%9F%94%B4-live-turkey-s-erdogan-and-kilicdaroglu-face-off-in-second-round-presidential-vote"),
    (2023, "Days after his re-election, Erdogan reversed course on economic policy, appointing market-friendly Mehmet Simsek as finance minister and Hafize Gaye Erkan as central bank governor. The central bank raised its policy rate from 8.5% to 50% by early 2024 in a sharp return to monetary orthodoxy after years of low-rate policy that had driven inflation as high as 85.5% in 2022.", "CNBC / OSW Centre for Eastern Studies", "https://www.osw.waw.pl/en/publikacje/analyses/2025-07-09/turbulent-stabilisation-turkeys-economy-under-simseks-supervision"),
    (2024, "The 8 December fall of Bashar al-Assad's government to a rebel offensive led by Hayat Tahrir al-Sham (HTS) reshaped the regional order in Ankara's favor. Turkey was the most significant outside backer of the anti-Assad opposition; Ankara subsequently sought to shape post-Assad Syria's security architecture, including pressing for the dismantling of the Kurdish-led SDF.", "NPR / CFR", "https://www.npr.org/2024/12/27/nx-s1-5238277/turkey-expected-to-play-a-major-role-in-syria-following-fall-of-the-assad-regime"),
    (2025, "Jailed PKK founder Abdullah Ocalan called on 27 February 2025 for the group to dissolve and disarm after four decades of insurgency; the PKK declared a unilateral ceasefire in March and formally announced dissolution at its May congress. By mid-2026 the process had stalled, with PKK officials accusing Ankara of blocking further steps and conditioning continued cooperation on Ocalan's release.", "Wikipedia / The New Arab", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025_PKK%E2%80%93Turkey_peace_process"),
    (2025, "Istanbul Mayor Ekrem Imamoglu, Erdogan's most credible electoral challenger and the CHP's presumptive 2028 presidential candidate, was detained on 19 March 2025 on corruption and terror-related charges and formally jailed pending trial on 23 March. The arrest triggered the largest street protests in Turkey in over a decade and was widely characterized by international observers as politically motivated.", "NPR", "https://www.npr.org/2025/03/23/g-s1-55372/turkish-court-orders-key-erdogan-rival-jailed"),
]

HISTORICAL_CONTEXT['SDN'] = [
    (2021, "On October 25, 2021, General Abdel Fattah al-Burhan dissolved Sudan's power-sharing Sovereign Council, sacked the civilian cabinet, and detained Prime Minister Abdalla Hamdok, ending the fragile military-civilian transition that followed Omar al-Bashir's 2019 ouster. The coup triggered African Union suspension and Western aid freezes, and set the stage for the Burhan-Hemedti rivalry that erupted into war 18 months later.", "CNN / France24", "https://edition.cnn.com/2021/10/25/africa/sudan-coup-explained-intl-cmd"),
    (2023, "War broke out on April 15, 2023 when the RSF attacked the Merowe airstrip and deployed troops across Khartoum, following the collapse of talks on integrating the RSF into the regular army under a framework agreement meant to restore civilian rule. Fighting spread within hours to the capital and Darfur.", "Council on Foreign Relations", "https://www.cfr.org/global-conflict-tracker/conflict/power-struggle-sudan"),
    (2023, "From late April to November 2023, RSF and allied Arab militias carried out a systematic campaign against the non-Arab Masalit community in El Geneina, West Darfur, killing an estimated 10,000-15,000 people. The US State Department (January 2025) formally determined the RSF had committed genocide -- the first such US genocide finding since Darfur in 2004.", "Human Rights Watch / Al Jazeera", "https://www.hrw.org/report/2024/05/09/the-massalit-will-not-come-home/ethnic-cleansing-and-crimes-against-humanity-in"),
    (2024, "Famine was formally declared in Zamzam displacement camp, North Darfur (population ~500,000) on August 1, 2024 -- only the third famine determination globally since the IPC monitoring system began two decades earlier. By the December 2024-May 2025 window, roughly 638,000 people were in IPC Phase 5 and 24.6 million faced acute food insecurity.", "World Food Programme / IPC", "https://www.wfp.org/news/famine-confirmed-sudans-north-darfur-confirming-un-agencies-worst-fears"),
    (2025, "The Sudanese Armed Forces recaptured Khartoum in late March 2025, retaking the presidential palace, airport, and central bridges after nearly two years of RSF occupation. The recapture marked the war's first major territorial turning point in the SAF's favor, though the RSF retained most of Darfur and pockets of Kordofan.", "CNN / ACLED", "https://www.cnn.com/2025/03/27/africa/sudans-army-captures-khartoum-from-rsf-intl"),
    (2025, "On July 26, 2025, the RSF and allied Sudan Liberation Movement-North announced 'Tasis' ('Founding'), a 15-member presidential council led by Hemedti, governing a self-declared federal structure across RSF-held Darfur, parts of Kordofan, and Blue Nile. The move was rejected by the UN, African Union, and Arab League, deepening Sudan's de facto partition.", "Africanews / Sudan Tribune", "https://www.africanews.com/2025/07/03/sundan-fsr-paramilitaries-and-allies-declare-parallel-government/"),
    (2025, "RSF forces overran El Fasher, the last army-held city in Darfur, on October 26, 2025 after an 18-month siege. A UN fact-finding mission and Amnesty International concluded the RSF killed at least 6,000 people in the first 72 hours and found 'hallmarks of genocide,' including ethnic-based executions and mass sexual violence.", "UN News / OHCHR", "https://news.un.org/en/story/2026/02/1166997"),
    (2026, "By February 2026 the US-led 'Quad' (US, Egypt, Saudi Arabia, UAE) said both SAF and RSF had given preliminary approval to a peace roadmap combining a three-month humanitarian truce with a longer political transition, alongside a $1.5 billion humanitarian mobilization effort, while the war had displaced roughly 15.3 million people -- the world's largest displacement crisis. The roadmap did not hold: talks broke down by March 11, 2026 after a top SAF general rejected the ceasefire terms absent an RSF withdrawal from civilian sites, and Burhan has since publicly rejected the roadmap outright -- the war remained fully active through August 2026 with no revived ceasefire process.", "International Crisis Group / UNHCR / Horn Review", "https://www.crisisgroup.org/africa/sudan/breakthrough-us-and-three-arab-powers-agree-sudan-peace-roadmap"),
]

HISTORICAL_CONTEXT['SSD'] = [
    (2011, "South Sudan seceded from Sudan on July 9, 2011, following a January 2011 referendum in which more than 98% of voters backed independence, ending Africa's longest-running civil war that killed an estimated two million people. Independence made South Sudan the UN's 193rd member state, but it inherited weak institutions and unresolved border/oil-transit disputes with Khartoum.", "United States Institute of Peace / US State Department", "https://www.usip.org/programs/independence-south-sudan"),
    (2013, "In December 2013, President Salva Kiir (Dinka) fired First Vice President Riek Machar (Nuer), triggering a civil war that split the army along ethnic lines. Between December 2013 and August 2015 alone the war caused an estimated 400,000 deaths, and by 2018 had displaced roughly 4 million people.", "Council on Foreign Relations, Global Conflict Tracker", "https://www.cfr.org/global-conflict-tracker/conflict/civil-war-south-sudan"),
    (2018, "The Revitalized Agreement on the Resolution of the Conflict (R-ARCSS) was signed on September 12, 2018, ending large-scale fighting and providing for a power-sharing transitional government. Implementation proved chronically slow, and by 2025-2026 the government itself put cumulative implementation at only around 37%.", "Radio Tamazuj / RJMEC quarterly report", "https://www.radiotamazuj.org/en/news/article/south-sudan-says-37-peace-deal-progress-enough-for-elections"),
    (2023, "Sudan's own civil war crippled the Petrodar and GNPOC pipelines carrying roughly 70-90% of South Sudan's oil exports to Port Sudan. A rupture in the Petrodar pipeline in February 2024 cut exports from about 186,000 barrels/day in January 2024 to just 58,000 bpd by December 2024, driving a 24.5% GDP contraction in 2024.", "International Crisis Group", "https://www.crisisgroup.org/africa/south-sudan/south-sudan-edge-its-neighbours-war-disrupts-oil-exports"),
    (2024, "In September 2024 the government postponed South Sudan's first-ever general elections to December 22, 2026, extending the transitional period to February 2027 -- the fifth postponement since the vote was first envisioned. The government cited unresolved security arrangements, flooding, an incomplete census, and a funding shortfall.", "UN News", "https://news.un.org/en/story/2024/11/1156661"),
    (2025, "On March 26, 2025, government forces placed First Vice President Riek Machar under house arrest in Juba after clashes in Nasir, Upper Nile State, in which the Nuer 'White Army' militia overran a government garrison, killing an estimated 250 soldiers -- the most serious rupture of the 2018 peace framework since its signing.", "Al Jazeera / IGAD statement", "https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2025/3/27/un-warns-of-conflict-in-south-sudan-amid-reports-of-vp-riek-machars-arrest"),
    (2025, "On September 11, 2025, Kiir formally suspended Machar as First Vice President and charged him with murder, treason, and crimes against humanity, trying him before a newly created Special Court for National Crimes. By August 2026 the trial remained unresolved with Machar's FVP post left vacant.", "Human Rights Watch / JURIST", "https://www.hrw.org/news/2025/09/15/south-sudan-ensure-due-process-fair-trials-of-opposition"),
]

HISTORICAL_CONTEXT['ETH'] = [
    (2020, "In November 2020, federal forces under PM Abiy Ahmed launched a military offensive against the Tigray People's Liberation Front (TPLF) after TPLF forces attacked a federal army base. The conflict escalated into a two-year civil war involving Eritrean troops, with independent researchers estimating 385,000-600,000 total deaths.", "International Crisis Group", "https://www.crisisgroup.org/stm/africa/ethiopia/turning-pretoria-deal-lasting-peace-ethiopia"),
    (2022, "On November 2, 2022, Ethiopia's federal government and the TPLF signed the Pretoria Agreement, an African Union-brokered cessation of hostilities ending the Tigray War. The deal left Eritrean troop withdrawal and Western Tigray's disputed status unresolved -- issues that became fault lines for renewed instability in 2025-2026.", "Wilson Center", "https://www.wilsoncenter.org/blog-post/ethiopias-tigray-conflict-peace-deal-african-union"),
    (2023, "Ethiopia formally defaulted on its sole $1 billion sovereign Eurobond in December 2023, becoming the third African nation (after Zambia and Ghana) to default during the post-pandemic debt crisis. The government opted to restructure roughly $15 billion in external debt under the G20 Common Framework, a process that dragged on for over two years.", "African Business", "https://african.business/2024/01/finance-services/what-does-2024-hold-for-ethiopia-after-its-2023-default"),
    (2024, "On July 29, 2024, the National Bank of Ethiopia floated the birr, ending decades of a managed peg and letting the currency devalue roughly 30% overnight. The move unlocked a four-year, $3.4 billion IMF Extended Credit Facility plus a $16.6 billion three-year World Bank pledge, though by mid-2026 the birr had continued sliding.", "IMF", "https://www.imf.org/en/news/articles/2024/07/29/pr24291-ethiopia-imf-exec-board-approves-4yr-us3b-ecf-arr"),
    (2024, "On January 1, 2024, Ethiopia formally joined BRICS alongside Egypt, Iran, Saudi Arabia and the UAE, marking Addis Ababa's most significant diplomatic pivot toward a multipolar order and away from traditional Western partners.", "Konrad-Adenauer-Stiftung", "https://www.kas.de/en/country-reports/detail/-/content/ethiopia-s-brics-membership"),
    (2024, "On January 2, 2024, PM Abiy Ahmed signed a memorandum of understanding with Somaliland granting Ethiopia a 50-year lease on Somaliland coastline for a naval base and port, in exchange for potential recognition of Somaliland's independence. Somalia denounced the deal as a violation of its territorial sovereignty; follow-on talks remained stalled through mid-2026.", "Al Jazeera", "https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2024/1/6/why-is-somalia-angry-about-neighbouring-ethiopias-new-red-sea-port-deal"),
    (2025, "Ethiopia officially inaugurated the Grand Ethiopian Renaissance Dam (GERD) on September 9, 2025. The $4-5 billion, 5,150-megawatt dam more than doubles Ethiopia's electricity generation capacity, but Egypt and Sudan refused to recognize the completion as resolving the Nile water dispute absent a binding treaty.", "Reuters via African Business", "https://african.business/2026/01/energy-resources/gerd-trump-wades-back-into-egypt-ethiopia-dam-dispute"),
    (2026, "Fighting resumed between the Ethiopian National Defence Force and TPLF-aligned forces in western Tigray in mid-2026, driven by unresolved Pretoria Agreement provisions on TPLF disarmament and the disputed Western Tigray territory, raising the risk of drawing in Eritrea and Sudan.", "The Soufan Center", "https://thesoufancenter.org/intelbrief-2026-august-21/"),
]

HISTORICAL_CONTEXT['SOM'] = [
    (2022, "President Hassan Sheikh Mohamud, re-elected in May 2022, declared \"total war\" on al-Shabaab and launched an offensive in August 2022 that recaptured territory the group had held for over a decade. The offensive later stalled in 2023, foreshadowing al-Shabaab's 2025 reversal of most of these gains.", "Combating Terrorism Center at West Point", "https://ctc.westpoint.edu/somalias-stalled-offensive-against-al-shabaab-taking-stock-of-obstacles/"),
    (2023, "The UN Security Council unanimously adopted Resolution 2714 on December 1, 2023, lifting the 30-year arms embargo on the Federal Government of Somalia, while keeping the embargo on al-Shabaab intact. The move was meant to accelerate the buildup of the Somali National Army ahead of the AU mission drawdown.", "UN Security Council Press / SIPRI", "https://press.un.org/en/2023/sc15511.doc.htm"),
    (2023, "On December 13, 2023, the IMF and World Bank's IDA approved Somalia's Completion Point under the HIPC Initiative, delivering $4.5 billion in total debt-service relief and cutting external public debt from 64% to roughly 6% of GDP by end-2023 -- the most significant milestone in Somalia's post-conflict financial rehabilitation.", "IMF / World Bank Joint Press Release", "https://www.imf.org/en/news/articles/2023/12/13/pr23438-imf-and-world-bank-announce-us-4-5-billion-in-debt-relief-for-somalia"),
    (2024, "Ethiopia and Somaliland signed a Memorandum of Understanding on January 1, 2024, granting Ethiopia a 50-year lease on Somaliland's Red Sea coast near Berbera. Mogadishu called the deal an assault on its territorial integrity and rejected mediation for a year until Turkey brokered a formal Ethiopia-Somalia dialogue in December 2024.", "Al Jazeera / International Crisis Group", "https://www.crisisgroup.org/africa/ethiopia-somaliland/stakes-ethiopia-somaliland-deal"),
    (2024, "Turkey and Somalia signed a 10-year defense and economic cooperation agreement in February 2024, followed in March 2024 by an offshore hydrocarbon exploration deal giving Turkey's state energy firm TPAO rights to explore roughly 16,000 sq km of Somali territory, deepening Ankara's position as Somalia's dominant security and resource partner.", "Daily Sabah / Nordic Monitor", "https://nordicmonitor.com/2025/04/turkey-secures-exceptional-rights-in-somalia-oil-agreement-documents-show/"),
    (2025, "The African Union's ATMIS mission formally ended on December 31, 2024, replaced by the AU Stabilization and Support Mission in Somalia (AUSSOM), which immediately faced a funding shortfall of roughly half its $196 million 2025 need, raising the risk of a security vacuum as al-Shabaab regained ground.", "ISS Africa / IPI Global Observatory", "https://theglobalobservatory.org/2026/01/with-aussoms-funding-challenges-here-to-stay-what-are-the-options-for-the-missions-future-in-somalia/"),
    (2025, "Al-Shabaab launched a major counteroffensive in 2025 that rapidly retook most of the Hiraan and Middle Shabelle territory the government had captured in its 2022-23 campaign, largely erasing those gains as the incomplete AUSSOM troop transition left recaptured towns thinly defended.", "International Crisis Group", "https://www.crisisgroup.org/brf/africa/somalia/b212-new-chapter-same-stalemate-somalias-war-al-shabaab"),
    (2026, "Israel became the first UN member state to formally recognize Somaliland's independence on December 26, 2025. President Mohamud denounced it as a breach of Somalia's sovereignty, and the episode coincided with a domestic constitutional crisis over whether his term had legally been extended to five years.", "Al Jazeera / The Hill / Eastleigh Voice", "https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2026/2/8/somalias-mohamud-slams-israels-interference-rejects-base-on-somaliland"),
]

HISTORICAL_CONTEXT['DJI'] = [
    (2017, "China opened its first overseas military base in Djibouti in August 2017, a $590 million facility located a few miles from the US Camp Lemonnier, cementing Djibouti's role as host to the world's densest concentration of foreign military installations.", "Vivekananda International Foundation / Axios", "https://www.vifindia.org/article/2017/august/24/china-s-first-overseas-military-base-in-djibouti"),
    (2019, "Djibouti's public debt-to-GDP ratio rose from 35% in 2013 to 66% by 2019, driven overwhelmingly by Chinese Exim Bank loans financing the Addis Ababa-Djibouti railway and Doraleh port. The IMF classified Djibouti as being in debt distress, with over 70% of its external debt owed to China.", "IMF / LSE Africa Blog", "https://blogs.lse.ac.uk/africaatlse/2026/04/13/china-djibouti-and-the-limits-of-leverage/"),
    (2023, "Facing unsustainable debt service, Djibouti negotiated a four-year moratorium with China's Exim Bank in October 2023, capping combined payments at approximately $20 million total over 2024-27, even as Djibouti's total debt-to-GDP continued climbing toward 69% by end-2024.", "China-Global South Project / IMF", "https://chinaglobalsouth.com/analysis/djiboutis-chinese-debt/"),
    (2024, "Ethiopia signed a memorandum of understanding with Somaliland on 1 January 2024 for Red Sea coastline near Berbera. Djibouti, whose port handles over 80-90% of Ethiopia's overseas trade and roughly a quarter of its own national income from that dependency, strongly opposed the deal as a direct threat to its transshipment monopoly.", "International Crisis Group / The Africa Report", "https://www.crisisgroup.org/africa/ethiopia-somaliland/stakes-ethiopia-somaliland-deal"),
    (2024, "Houthi attacks on Red Sea shipping from late 2023 cut large-vessel traffic calling at Djibouti by roughly a third in H1 2024. Paradoxically, container throughput at the Doraleh Container Terminal rose about 77% as smaller vessels rerouted away from the Suez corridor through the Bab-el-Mandeb and into Djibouti's port network.", "Lloyd's List / AllAfrica", "https://www.lloydslist.com/LL1150516/Red-Sea-reroutings-uproot-traditional-transhipment-trends"),
    (2025, "Djibouti's parliament voted in October 2025 to remove the constitutional age limit of 75 for presidential candidates, widely understood as clearing the way for the 78-year-old Guelleh to seek a sixth term ahead of the scheduled April 2026 election.", "AllAfrica", "https://allafrica.com/stories/202510270030.html"),
    (2026, "Ismail Omar Guelleh was re-elected to a sixth term on 10 April 2026, winning 97.81% of the vote, extending his tenure past 27 years and reinforcing assessments that Djibouti's basing-and-transit economic model rests on continued single-party political continuity.", "Al Jazeera / Washington Post", "https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2026/4/11/djiboutis-president-guelleh-claims-sixth-straight-term-in-office"),
]

HISTORICAL_CONTEXT['ERI'] = [
    (2009, "The UN Security Council imposed sanctions on Eritrea (arms embargo, travel ban, asset freeze) via Resolution 1907, citing alleged support for al-Shabaab and a border dispute with Djibouti. The sanctions entrenched a decade of international isolation, reinforcing the government's siege mentality and justification for indefinite national service.", "UN Security Council / Washington Post", "https://www.washingtonpost.com/world/national-security/un-lifts-sanctions-on-eritrea-ending-nations-decade-of-international-isolation/2018/11/14/dd4c7e14-e825-11e8-bbdb-72fdbf9d4fed_story.html"),
    (2018, "Ethiopian PM Abiy Ahmed and President Isaias Afwerki signed a peace declaration in July 2018 ending the frozen 1998-2000 border war, restoring diplomatic ties, flights, and trade for the first time in 20 years. On November 14, 2018, the UN Security Council unanimously lifted the 2009 sanctions regime.", "UN Security Council Resolution 2444 / HRW", "https://www.hrw.org/news/2018/11/14/un-security-council-lifts-arms-embargo-eritrea"),
    (2020, "From November 2020, Eritrean Defense Forces entered Tigray alongside Ethiopian federal troops. Eritrean troops were implicated in the Axum massacre (late November 2020), where hundreds of civilians were killed -- among the war's worst documented atrocities. Abiy Ahmed did not publicly admit Eritrean involvement until 2021.", "Amnesty International / Wikipedia", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tigray_genocide"),
    (2021, "The US Treasury's OFAC sanctioned Eritrean Defense Forces Chief of Staff General Filipos Woldeyohannes under the Global Magnitsky program (August 2021), and in November 2021 designated additional Eritrean entities targeting actors prolonging the Ethiopian conflict.", "US Treasury OFAC / Federal Register", "https://ofac.treasury.gov/recent-actions/20210823"),
    (2022, "Amnesty International documented that Eritrean forces committed war crimes and possible crimes against humanity in Tigray both immediately before and after the November 2022 Cessation of Hostilities Agreement, including extrajudicial executions -- evidence Eritrea, excluded from the Pretoria peace process, continued operations independent of Addis Ababa's timeline.", "Amnesty International", "https://www.amnesty.org/en/latest/news/2023/09/eritrean-soldiers-committed-war-crimes-and-possible-crimes-against-humanity-in-the-tigray-region-after-signing-of-agreement-to-end-hostilities/"),
    (2025, "Eritrea formally withdrew from IGAD on December 12, 2025, having only rejoined in June 2023 after a 16-year suspension. Asmara's foreign ministry said IGAD had 'forfeited its legal mandate,' reflecting Eritrea's preference for bilateral over multilateral diplomacy amid rising tension with Ethiopia over Red Sea access.", "Eritrea Ministry of Information (Shabait) / IGAD", "https://shabait.com/2025/12/12/press-release-eritrea-has-withdrawn-its-membership-from-igad/"),
    (2026, "In February 2026, Abiy Ahmed publicly acknowledged for the first time that Eritrean forces were 'responsible for mass killings, destroying homes, and looting factories' in Tigray. The admission landed amid a broader collapse in bilateral relations, with both states massing troops along their shared frontier.", "JURIST", "https://www.jurist.org/news/2026/02/ethiopia-acknowledges-eritrean-troops-role-in-tigray-war-as-regional-tensions-rise/"),
]

