LIVE_CONFLICTS = [
    {'name': '2026 Iran-Israel-US War', 'status': 'Active / unresolved', 'type': 'Interstate War', 'impact': 'Critical', 'stats': [('War start (2026 phase)', 'February 28, 2026 — Operation Epic Fury'), ('Total killed, all sides (est., late Aug 2026)', '~8,993 to 18,327 (wide range; sources conflict)'), ('Iranian Supreme Leader killed', 'Ali Khamenei, Feb 28, 2026; succeeded by Mojtaba Khamenei, Mar 8, 2026'), ('Brent crude peak vs pre-war (~$70)', 'reported spikes to $109-$140/bbl across 2026 escalations'), ('IMF 2026 global growth forecast cut', '3.1% to 3.0% (July 2026), citing Iran war energy shock'), ('Potential cost of resumed full-scale war (CFR/WTO analysis)', 'up to $2.2 trillion to world economy'), ('Qatar LNG export capacity destroyed', '~17% (Iranian strike on gas facility)'), ('Israel 2026 war-cost estimate (Finance Ministry)', '~35 billion shekels (~$11.5bn)')], 'groups': 'IRGC; Mojtaba Khamenei (Supreme Leader since March 8, 2026); Israel Defense Forces; U.S. CENTCOM; U.S. envoys Steve Witkoff and Jared Kushner; Oman (lead mediator on Hormuz safe-passage talks); Qatar (mediator, hosts talks with Gulf PM Sheikh Mohammed bin Abdulrahman); Gulf Cooperation Council states (Saudi Arabia, UAE, Kuwait, Bahrain, Qatar — targeted by Iranian retaliatory strikes).', 'affected': ['IRN', 'ISR', 'SAU', 'QAT', 'ARE', 'KWT', 'BHR', 'OMN', 'IRQ'], 'summary': 'The current war traces back to a 12-day conflict in June 2025, when Israel struck over 900 Iranian targets on June 13, killing 30+ senior officials and 11 nuclear scientists, prompting Iranian ballistic-missile retaliation and a June 22 U.S. strike on the Fordow, Natanz, and Isfahan nuclear sites using GBU-57 bunker-buster bombs; a US-Qatar-mediated ceasefire held from June 24, 2025. Tensions reignited fully on February 28, 2026, when the U.S. and Israel launched roughly 900 strikes in 12 hours ("Operation Epic Fury"), killing Supreme Leader Ali Khamenei and dozens of officials — though a strike also hit a girls\' school near a naval base in Minab, killing about 170 civilians. Iran retaliated with ballistic missiles against Israel and Gulf states; Mojtaba Khamenei was confirmed as the new Supreme Leader by the Assembly of Experts on March 8, 2026. Iran asserted control over the Strait of Hormuz, closing it to shipping from around March 4, 2026, an event the IEA characterized as the largest oil-supply disruption in market history. A ceasefire brokered by Trump took effect June 14, 2026, reopening Hormuz, but it collapsed by July 10 after mutual accusations of violations; Iranian strikes on U.S. missile-defense sites in Jordan killed three American service members on July 17-18. A 60-day U.S.-Iran deadline expired August 17, 2026 with no resolution; Iran and Oman have negotiated technical details of a proposed "safe passage" shipping corridor, but as of August 26, 2026 Tehran maintains the Strait remains closed pending U.S. concessions on frozen funds and blockade terms, and Trump has floated declaring Hormuz U.S. territory. Casualty estimates vary widely by source (Iranian, Israeli, and independent trackers diverge sharply) but range from roughly 9,000 to over 18,000 killed across all parties as of late August 2026.', 'market_impact': "Brent crude, which traded near $70 before the war, spiked in stages: to $80-82 by March 2, past $109 amid the Hormuz closure, and reportedly above $120-140 (a post-2008 high) during subsequent escalations, before partially retracing on ceasefire news each time it was announced. The IMF cut its 2026 global growth forecast to 3.0% (from 3.1%) in July, citing lingering energy-shock effects, and CFR/WTO analysis has floated a resumption of full-scale war costing the world economy as much as $2.2 trillion. Regional GDP hits have been severe: Saudi Arabia's growth is projected roughly 3 percentage points lower, the UAE about 5 points lower, and IMF modeling flagged potential 14% GDP contractions for Kuwait and Qatar if the war had continued through April 2026; an Iranian strike destroyed about 17% of Qatar's LNG export capacity. Pakistan faces compounding pressure from Gulf remittance losses worth 4-5% of GDP plus higher fuel and food costs. Israel's own war-related budget cost is estimated at roughly 35 billion shekels ($11.5bn), including 22bn shekels for security, layered on top of a 2026 defense budget raised to about $34.7bn; the IMF nonetheless still projects Israel outgrowing every G7 economy in 2026 on reserve call-ups and war spending, even as its Finance Ministry trimmed its own 2026 growth estimate to 4.7% from 5.2%. Markets reacted sharply: the Tel Aviv TA-125 surged nearly 6% to a record high on the day of the initial U.S.-Israel strikes and is up about 11% year-to-date after gaining more than 50% in 2025, while Lockheed Martin and RTX shares were up over 17-19% through July 2026 on Pentagon restocking demand tied to this war and the Russia-Ukraine conflict combined. Washington has approved over $16bn in new arms sales to Jordan, Kuwait, and the UAE as a partial offset.", 'sources': [('Al Jazeera — Iran war live updates, Aug 26, 2026', 'https://www.aljazeera.com/news/liveblog/2026/8/26/iran-war-live-iran-says-hormuz-remains-closed-despite-oman-rout-deal'), ("CFR — The Iran War's Global Economic Impact", 'https://www.cfr.org/articles/the-iran-wars-global-economic-impact'), ('Al Jazeera — IMF cuts 2026 world growth forecast citing Iran war fallout', 'https://www.aljazeera.com/economy/2026/7/9/imf-cuts-2026-world-growth-forecast-citing-iran-war-fallout'), ('IMF — Middle East and Central Asia Regional Economic Outlook, April 2026', 'https://www.imf.org/-/media/files/publications/reo/mcd-cca/2026/english/text.pdf'), ('CNBC — A timeline of how the Iran war shook oil prices', 'https://www.cnbc.com/2026/04/21/oil-price-iran-war-middle-east.html'), ("CFR — Trump's Iran Deal Has Collapsed", 'https://www.cfr.org/articles/trumps-iran-deal-has-collapsed-leaving-the-u-s-with-few-good-options'), ("CNBC — Israel's economy and financial markets are booming despite Iran war", 'https://www.cnbc.com/2026/04/30/israel-economy-stock-market-tel-aviv-trump-netanyahu-gdp-us-inflation.html')]},
    {'name': 'Red Sea Shipping Crisis & Houthi-Saudi Blockade', 'status': 'Active, re-escalating', 'type': 'Unconventional', 'impact': 'Critical', 'stats': [('Merchant vessels targeted since Nov 2023', '100+ (4 sunk, 1 seized, 8+ seafarers killed)'), ('Suez Canal weekly containership transits, pre-crisis vs. mid-Jan 2026', '~80/week to ~26/week'), ('Suez Canal annual revenue, 2023 vs. 2024', '$9.4bn to $3.9bn (~58% decline)'), ('Suez Canal revenue, FY2025/26', '$4.67bn (+23% y/y) before the July 2026 Saudi blockade'), ('War-risk insurance premium, pre- vs. post-blockade (July 2026)', '~0.3% to ~0.75% of vessel value'), ('Freight rate increase, Asia-Europe / Asia-US East Coast', '+25-40% / +15-25%'), ('Est. Egypt tourism + Suez combined loss (UNDP, 2023-25)', '$9.9bn (medium) to $13.7bn (high intensity)'), ('Potential global oil supply cut if Bab el-Mandeb fully closes', '~7%')], 'groups': 'Houthis / Ansar Allah (spokesman Yahya Saree); Saudi Arabia (blockade target, evaluating retaliatory options); UK Maritime Trade Operations (UKMTO); EU Operation Aspides (Red Sea naval escort mission); Egypt / Suez Canal Authority; Iran (alleged pressure on Houthis to close Bab el-Mandeb).', 'affected': ['YEM', 'EGY', 'SAU', 'ARE', 'QAT', 'ISR'], 'summary': 'The crisis began November 19, 2023, when Houthi forces seized the cargo ship Galaxy Leader off Yemen, launching a campaign against shipping linked to Israel that expanded to over 100 targeted merchant vessels, four ships sunk, one seized, and at least eight seafarers killed. Attacks paused from roughly November 11, 2025 (following the October 2025 Gaza ceasefire) through late February 2026, but the Houthis renewed a ban on Israeli-linked shipping in June 2026 as the Iran-Israel-U.S. war reignited, reportedly under Iranian pressure to close the Bab el-Mandeb if U.S. strikes on Iran continued. On July 20, 2026, the Houthis escalated dramatically, declaring a full naval blockade on Saudi Arabia itself under a "siege for a siege" doctrine, citing a Saudi-imposed embargo on Yemen; they have since claimed missile attacks on multiple Saudi-linked tankers, including the Wafa and Daisy near the Yanbu Red Sea export hub (~4 million bpd) in early August 2026. Suez Canal traffic, which ran roughly 80 containerships a week before the crisis, had recovered only to about 26 a week by mid-January 2026 before the Saudi blockade announcement dealt a fresh blow; most container carriers still default to Cape of Good Hope routing for Asia-Europe and Asia-U.S. East Coast trades as of August 2026. Egypt\'s Suez Canal Authority revenue, which had rebounded 23% year-on-year to $4.67bn in FY2025/26 as tensions eased, is now at renewed risk given the Saudi blockade and the unresolved Iran war. As of late August 2026, the situation remains volatile with no durable ceasefire covering Red Sea shipping.', 'market_impact': "The Suez Canal's revenue collapse illustrates the scale of disruption: from a record $9.4bn in 2023 to just $3.9bn in 2024 (roughly a 58% drop) as Houthi attacks diverted traffic around the Cape of Good Hope, before a partial 23% rebound to $4.67bn in FY2025/26 during a lull. War-risk marine insurance premiums, which had eased to roughly 0.3% of a vessel's value, jumped to around 0.75% following the July 2026 Houthi blockade declaration on Saudi Arabia. Freight rates have been pushed up 25-40% on Asia-Europe routes and 15-25% on Asia-U.S. East Coast routes as carriers continue Cape diversions, which add 10-14 days per voyage. UNDP estimated the combined Suez Canal and Egyptian tourism revenue loss at $9.9bn (medium-intensity scenario) to $13.7bn (high-intensity scenario) across the 2023-24 and 2024-25 fiscal years. Analysts have warned that a full closure of the Bab el-Mandeb Strait — through which most Saudi oil bound for Asia transits — could cut global oil supply by roughly 7%, given Saudi Arabia's Red Sea export capacity of about 4 million barrels per day from the Yanbu hub alone. BIMCO and shipping insurers have flagged that premiums could rise sharply again if the July 2026 Saudi blockade escalates into sustained attacks on tankers rather than episodic strikes. Jordan's economy, heavily reliant on regional trade routes and tourism (accounting for about 18.3% of GDP), has faced elevated shipping costs and investor caution, though its tourism sector still grew over 7% in 2025 despite the disruption.", 'sources': [("Bloomberg — Yemen's Houthis to Impose Maritime Blockade on Saudi Arabia", 'https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2026-07-20/yemen-s-houthis-vow-to-impose-maritime-blockade-on-saudi-arabia'), ('Atlantic Council — What the Houthi blockade on Saudi Arabia means for the global economy', 'https://www.atlanticcouncil.org/dispatches/the-houthis-just-announced-a-blockade-on-saudi-arabia-what-does-it-mean-for-the-global-economy/'), ('Insurance Journal — Red Sea War Insurance Costs Rise After Houthi Blockade', 'https://www.insurancejournal.com/news/international/2026/07/21/878345.htm'), ('Al Jazeera — Saudi condemns Houthi blockade: how will the rest of the world be impacted?', 'https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2026/7/21/saudi-condemns-houthi-blockade-how-will-the-rest-of-the-world-be-impacted'), ('Egyptian Streets — Suez Canal Revenue Rises 23 Percent in FY2025/2026', 'https://egyptianstreets.com/2026/06/30/suez-canal-revenue-rises-23-percent-in-the-2025-2026-fiscal-year-as-regional-tensions-ease/'), ('AGBI — Suez Canal revenue down $10bn due to regional conflicts', 'https://www.agbi.com/transport/2026/03/suez-canal-revenue-down-10bn-in-regional-conflict/'), ('FDD Long War Journal — Houthis escalate attacks in Red Sea and on Saudi Arabia', 'https://www.longwarjournal.org/archives/2026/08/houthis-escalate-attacks-in-red-sea-and-on-saudi-arabia-riyadh-evaluates-options.php')]},
    {'name': 'Gaza War Aftermath & Fragile Ceasefire', 'status': 'Ceasefire holding but repeatedly violated', 'type': 'Sectarian', 'impact': 'Critical', 'stats': [('Trump 20-point plan unveiled / Phase one agreed', 'Sept 29, 2025 / Oct 9, 2025'), ('Gaza deaths (Ministry of Health, July 12, 2026)', '73,231 killed; 173,686 injured'), ('Independent academic estimate (early 2026)', '~75,200 violent deaths + ~8,540 excess non-violent deaths'), ('Hostages recovered over course of war', '168 alive, 87 deceased; last remains returned Jan 26, 2026'), ('Gaza reconstruction cost estimate (World Bank/UN/EU RDNA, Apr 2026)', '$71.4 billion over a decade'), ('Total verified damage and economic losses', '$57.9 billion'), ('First-18-months reconstruction need', '$26.3 billion'), ('Est. Egypt tourism + Suez revenue loss, FY2023-25 (UNDP)', '$9.9bn-$13.7bn')], 'groups': 'Hamas; Israel / IDF; the U.S.-led Board of Peace (chaired by Trump, high representative Nikolay Mladenov); a proposed technocratic Palestinian administrative committee; Egypt (Rafah crossing, mediator); Qatar and Turkey (mediators); World Bank, UN, and EU (joint damage assessment authors).', 'affected': ['PSE', 'ISR', 'EGY', 'JOR', 'LBN'], 'summary': 'Following two years of war after the October 7, 2023 Hamas attack, President Trump unveiled a 20-point peace plan on September 29, 2025, and Israel and Hamas agreed to its first phase on October 9, 2025, covering a hostage-for-prisoner exchange, partial Israeli withdrawal, expanded humanitarian aid, and the reopening of the Rafah crossing. The exchange concluded by January 26, 2026, when the remains of the last Israeli hostage were returned, bringing the total to 168 hostages recovered alive and 87 returned deceased over the course of the war. The U.S. declared the start of "phase two" on January 16, 2026, intended to establish a technocratic Palestinian administrative committee overseen by a Trump-chaired "Board of Peace" and to begin reconstruction planning, but implementation has stalled badly: most phase-one commitments were not fully realized, and Israel has refused further withdrawal until Hamas fully disarms. A UN-EU-World Bank Rapid Damage and Needs Assessment released in April 2026 found $57.9bn in total damage and economic losses and put reconstruction needs at $71.4bn over a decade, including $26.3bn required in just the first 18 months — up sharply from a $53bn estimate given in February 2025. Israel escalated strikes on Gaza in early August 2026 despite the ceasefire framework, drawing public criticism on August 26, 2026 from Nikolay Mladenov, the Board of Peace\'s high representative, who warned the U.S. Security Council that failure to hold the line risks a renewed war. Gaza\'s Ministry of Health reported 73,231 killed and 173,686 injured as of July 12, 2026, while independent academic casualty analyses published in early 2026 estimated the true toll at over 75,200 violent deaths plus roughly 8,540 excess non-violent deaths. As of late August 2026 the ceasefire remains formally in place but fragile, with reconstruction barely begun and no agreed timeline for full Israeli withdrawal or Hamas disarmament verification.', 'market_impact': "The reconstruction bill itself is now the dominant economic figure: the World Bank/UN/EU joint assessment's $71.4bn total need (against $57.9bn in verified damage) makes Gaza's rebuild, per Oxfam's analysis, roughly seven times costlier than all three previous Gaza reconstruction efforts (2009, 2012, 2014) combined, with $26.3bn needed in the first 18 months alone for housing, infrastructure, and essential services. Regionally, Egypt bore some of the heaviest indirect costs: UNDP estimated combined Suez Canal shipping and tourism revenue losses of $9.9bn to $13.7bn across the 2023-24 and 2024-25 fiscal years as the war depressed both canal traffic (via associated Houthi Red Sea attacks) and visitor confidence, even though Egyptian tourist arrivals ultimately proved more resilient than initially feared. Jordan, where tourism represents about 18.3% of GDP, saw arrivals and investment sentiment dampened through 2023-24 before recovering to 7%+ tourism growth in 2025. On the Israeli side, the Bank of Israel estimated the first two years of the broader multi-front war (Gaza plus Lebanon) cost the economy roughly 177 billion shekels (over $57bn) in lost output, even as the Finance Ministry separately doubled the 2026 defense budget baseline to about $34.7bn (112 billion shekels) to sustain multi-front operations. Reconstruction financing remains a major open question: donor pledges from Gulf states and international financial institutions have been discussed but full disbursement is contingent on political and security guarantees — including Hamas disarmament — that have not yet been met, leaving the $71.4bn recovery plan largely unfunded as of August 2026.", 'sources': [('World Bank — New Report Assesses Damages, Losses and Needs in Gaza and the West Bank', 'https://www.worldbank.org/en/news/press-release/2025/02/18/new-report-assesses-damages-losses-and-needs-in-gaza-and-the-west-bank'), ('Al Jazeera — Rebuilding Gaza to cost $71bn, must include Palestinians, report finds', 'https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2026/7/23/rebuilding-gaza-to-cost-71bn-must-include-palestinians-report-finds'), ("NPR — Official leading Trump's Gaza ceasefire effort criticizes Israel for its attacks", 'https://www.npr.org/2026/08/27/g-s1-140400/israel-gaza-ceasefire'), ('Al Jazeera — US declares phase two of Gaza ceasefire, but what did phase one deliver?', 'https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2026/1/16/us-declares-phase-two-of-gaza-ceasefire-but-what-did-phase-one-deliver'), ('PBS News — Palestinian death toll in Gaza tops 73,000, officials say', 'https://www.pbs.org/newshour/world/palestinian-death-toll-in-gaza-tops-73000-officials-say-as-israel-strikes-despite-ceasefire'), ("CNN — Israel rejects Trump's Gaza peace plan, saying no withdrawal until Hamas disarms", 'https://www.cnn.com/2026/08/09/middleeast/israel-gaza-trump-netanyahu-plan-intl'), ('UNDP — Egypt faces mounting socio-economic strain from the Gaza war', 'https://www.undp.org/arab-states/press-releases/egypt-faces-mounting-socio-economic-strain-gaza-war-says-undp')]},
    {'name': "Syria's Post-Assad Transition", 'status': 'Fragile but consolidating -- US lifted terrorism designation Aug 2026', 'type': 'Political Instability', 'impact': 'Significant', 'stats': [("Civil war duration before Assad's fall", '13 years (2011-2024)'), ('Rebel offensive to capture Damascus', '~11 days'), ('Transition timeline set by interim constitution', '5 years to elections'), ('World Bank reconstruction cost estimate, Oct 2025', '$216bn (range $140-345bn)'), ('Share of pre-conflict capital stock destroyed', '~1/3'), ('Investment commitments attracted in 2025', '$28bn (incl. $14bn MOUs at Aug 2025 Damascus ceremony)'), ('Registered Syrian refugees remaining in Jordan, Apr 2026', '~411,760 (down sharply from pre-2025)'), ('Lebanon-to-Syria cross-border return movements by Jun 22, 2026', '464,562+')], 'groups': "Hay'at Tahrir al-Sham (HTS) / transitional government under Ahmed al-Sharaa, remnant Assad-era militias, Kurdish SDF", 'affected': ['SYR', 'LBN', 'JOR', 'IRQ'], 'summary': "The Assad regime fell on December 8, 2024 after a rapid rebel offensive led by Hay'at Tahrir al-Sham captured Damascus, ending a 13-year civil war. Ahmed al-Sharaa was declared transitional president in January 2025, and an interim constitutional declaration took effect in March 2025, setting out a 5-year path to elections. One year on, the transition remains disputed — no single national armed force yet exists, and localized violence (including renewed hostilities from March 2026) has continued. On the sanctions side, Washington repealed the Caesar Act via the FY2026 National Defense Authorization Act in December 2025 and the EU has lifted most broad-based economic restrictions from the Assad era, though targeted sanctions on regime-linked individuals and heavy compliance risk for banks and contractors remain — meaning sanctions relief is real but partial, not a clean reopening. Consolidation accelerated sharply in late August 2026: the US formally removed Syria's 47-year State Sponsor of Terrorism designation on August 24, 2026 (Trump notified Congress of the rescission on July 8, triggering a 45-day review); the same week saw formal dissolution of the Kurdish-led SDF and its integration into state institutions (following a January 2026 government offensive that captured roughly 80% of former SDF-held northeast Syria and a January 30 ceasefire setting up the merger), a new Kurdish-rights decree repealing 1962 Hasakah-census-era restrictions, and the launch of international electronic payment systems. None of this amounts to a resolved transition — the underlying fragility (ISIS threat, intercommunal tensions, 15.6 million people in need per CFR's tracker) persists — but it marks real, dated consolidation milestones rather than a static 'fragile, ongoing' holding pattern.", 'market_impact': "The World Bank's October 2025 assessment put Syria's reconstruction need at roughly $216bn (a range of $140-345bn, which the Bank itself calls a likely underestimate; Syrian officials have cited figures as high as $600-900bn), with infrastructure alone accounting for 48% of the roughly $108bn in direct physical damage assessed so far. Investors have moved faster than the sanctions picture might suggest — Syria drew about $28bn in investment commitments in 2025, including $14bn in MOUs signed at a single August 2025 Damascus ceremony, led by Turkey ($11bn in energy/infrastructure), Saudi Arabia ($6.4bn), and the UAE ($800m for Tartus port development) — though MOUs are non-binding and actual disbursement will take years. For neighbors, the fiscal picture is now shifting: the World Bank estimates hosting Syrian refugees costs Lebanon's economy roughly $1.5bn a year, but returns have accelerated sharply since renewed Lebanon-Syria border hostilities began March 2, 2026 (464,562+ cross-border movements by late June), and Jordan's registered Syrian refugee population has fallen to roughly 411,760 by April 2026. That easing of the refugee-hosting burden is genuine relief for Lebanon's and Jordan's budgets, but the 2026 regional refugee response plan is itself operating on a $2.8bn ask — already cut 40% from 2025 — leaving those still displaced, and the returnees rebuilding inside Syria, with materially less international support than a year earlier.", 'sources': [('Wikipedia: Fall of the Assad regime', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fall_of_the_Assad_regime'), ('Belfer Center, Harvard Kennedy School', 'https://www.belfercenter.org/research-analysis/external-states-and-syrias-challenge-reunification-under-transitional-president'), ('UK House of Commons Library: Syria one year after Assad', 'https://commonslibrary.parliament.uk/research-briefings/cbp-10430/'), ("World Bank: Syria's Post-Conflict Reconstruction Costs Estimated at $216 Billion", 'https://www.worldbank.org/en/news/press-release/2025/10/21/syria-s-post-conflict-reconstruction-costs-estimated-at-216-billion'), ('The Arab Weekly: Syria hopes for full lifting of US sanctions to push ahead with $216bn reconstruction', 'https://thearabweekly.com/syria-hopes-full-lifting-us-sanctions-push-ahead-216-billion-reconstruction'), ('EUAA: Syria Country Focus — Impact on Civilian Population, Returns Abroad', 'https://www.euaa.europa.eu/coi/syria/2025/country-focus/45-impact-violence-civilian-population/456-returns-abroad'), ('Al Jazeera: US removes Syria from state sponsor of terrorism list', 'https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2026/8/24/us-removes-syria-from-state-sponsor-of-terrorism-list'), ('SANA: Syria news roundup, week of Aug 22-28, 2026', 'https://sana.sy/en/syria/2339045/')]},
    {'name': 'Sudan Civil War', 'status': 'Active — Sudan itself is now a tracked country; this entry covers the regional spillover into neighboring states', 'type': 'Civil War', 'impact': 'Significant', 'stats': [('People requiring humanitarian assistance (early war)', '~25 million'), ('Forcibly displaced', '~12 million'), ('Refugees fleeing across borders', '3 million+'), ('Sudanese refugees hosted by Egypt (incl. unregistered)', '~1.2 million'), ('Registered with UNHCR in Egypt, as of Jan 2025', '890,000+'), ('South Sudan oil export volumes, Jan 2024 vs Dec 2024', '186,000 bpd -> 58,000 bpd'), ('South Sudan 2024 GDP contraction (pipeline disruption)', '~24.5%'), ('Newly arrived Sudanese children out of school (UNICEF/World Bank)', '54%'), ('Refugee families left without cash assistance after 2025 funding cuts', '~50,000 families / 164,000 people')], 'groups': 'Sudanese Armed Forces (SAF) under Abdul Fattah al-Burhan, Rapid Support Forces (RSF) under Mohamed Hamdan Dagalo', 'affected': ['SDN', 'EGY', 'SSD'], 'summary': "Sudan's civil war (erupted Apr 15, 2023, between the Sudanese Armed Forces and the Rapid Support Forces after a power struggle following their joint 2021 coup) is one of the world's largest displacement crises — roughly 12 million people forcibly displaced, with over 3 million fleeing across borders. Egypt has become the single largest host, sheltering an estimated 1.2 million Sudanese, of whom more than 890,000 were formally registered with UNHCR as of January 2025. South Sudan has suffered the most severe direct economic spillover: a February 2024 rupture of the Sudan-transiting Petrodar oil pipeline cut South Sudan's export volumes from 186,000 to 58,000 barrels/day by end-2024, driving a 24.5% GDP contraction and triple-digit inflation, since South Sudan is landlocked and has no alternative export route at scale. Chad (not separately tracked by this tool) has also absorbed over 700,000 Sudanese refugees and is reportedly implicated as a transshipment route for weapons reaching the RSF. A UNICEF/World Bank socioeconomic assessment found 54% of newly arrived Sudanese children in Egypt are out of school, and UNHCR was forced to cut monthly cash assistance in 2025, leaving roughly 50,000 vulnerable refugee families — about 164,000 people — without reliable means to cover rent or food.", 'market_impact': "The refugee influx lands directly on top of Egypt's own currency and Suez Canal-driven fragility rather than in isolation: absorbing over a million additional residents strains subsidized bread, fuel, and school places at the exact moment Egypt's government is trying to hold the line on IMF-mandated subsidy reform. For South Sudan, the pipeline disruption is not a slow-burn drag but a direct, quantifiable fiscal shock — oil normally accounts for 80-95% of government revenue, so the 2024 export collapse translated almost mechanically into the year's 24.5% GDP contraction and currency crisis. Because international humanitarian funding for the Sudan response has been cut rather than expanded through 2025, more of the refugee-hosting cost is being absorbed directly by host communities and states rather than offset by donor aid.", 'sources': [('Wikipedia: Sudanese civil war (2023-present)', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sudanese_civil_war_(2023%E2%80%93present)'), ('Wikipedia: Humanitarian impact of the Sudanese civil war', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Humanitarian_impact_of_the_Sudanese_civil_war_(2023%E2%80%93present)'), ('UNHCR: Egypt Sudan Situation — Country Socioeconomic Profile, Jan 2025', 'https://data.unhcr.org/en/documents/download/114065'), ("International Crisis Group: South Sudan on the edge as its neighbour's war disrupts oil exports", 'https://www.crisisgroup.org/africa/south-sudan/south-sudan-edge-its-neighbours-war-disrupts-oil-exports'), ('World Bank: South Sudan Macro Poverty Outlook', 'https://thedocs.worldbank.org/en/doc/bae48ff2fefc5a869546775b3f010735-0500062021/related/mpo-ssd.pdf')]},
    {'name': 'Israel-Hezbollah War & Lebanon Front', 'status': 'Ceasefire re-established Apr 2026, fragile', 'type': 'Sectarian', 'impact': 'Critical', 'stats': [('Original ceasefire effective / expiry', 'Nov 27, 2024 / originally March 2, 2026'), ('UNIFIL-recorded Israeli airspace violations (to Feb 2026)', '10,000+ violations; 1,400 military activities in Lebanon'), ('Killed / displaced in March-April 2026 escalation', '2,000+ killed; 1 million+ displaced by mid-April'), ('New ceasefire agreed', 'April 16, 2026'), ('World Bank 2026 Lebanon GDP forecast (Aug 21, 2026)', '-6.4% contraction (10.4 points below no-conflict scenario)'), ('2025 GDP growth (pre-escalation rebound)', '+4.2%'), ('Combined conflict losses since 2023 (Finance Ministry est.)', 'up to $20 billion'), ('2024 conflict total economic cost', '$14bn ($6.8bn physical damage + $7.2bn economic losses)'), ('2026 forecast inflation', '17.5%')], 'groups': 'Hezbollah (Secretary-General Naim Qassem); Israel Defense Forces; Lebanese Armed Forces (LAF); UNIFIL (UN peacekeeping force monitoring the ceasefire); Lebanese President Joseph Aoun; Lebanese PM Nawaf Salam; Lebanese Finance Minister Yassine Jaber; United States (ceasefire guarantor/mediator).', 'affected': ['LBN', 'ISR'], 'summary': 'The November 27, 2024 Israel-Hezbollah ceasefire, brokered by the U.S. and France, required Hezbollah\'s withdrawal and disarmament south of the Litani River but was plagued by violations from the outset: UNIFIL recorded over 10,000 Israeli airspace violations and 1,400 military activities inside Lebanese territory between November 2024 and February 2026, while Israel was separately accused of at least 2,036 ceasefire violations killing at least 15 people, including a Lebanese Army officer, by January 2026. Lebanon\'s army declared phase one of south-of-Litani disarmament complete around January 8, 2026, though Israel called progress "far from sufficient," and a four-month timeline for phase two was set on February 17, 2026. The truce collapsed into open warfare in March 2026 as the Iran-Israel war widened the front: fighting killed over 2,000 people in Lebanon and displaced more than one million by mid-April 2026, prompting a new Israel-Lebanon ceasefire on April 16, 2026. That framework proved fragile too — Hezbollah rejected a further ceasefire refinement on June 4, 2026 — and Israeli strikes have continued through the summer, including an August 15, 2026 wave that killed at least 11 people (among them three children) in Ansar and Deir ez-Zahrani in Nabatieh district and killed a senior Hezbollah commander, Abu Hassan Alaa. On August 5, 2026, Lebanese President Joseph Aoun and PM Nawaf Salam tasked the Lebanese Armed Forces with devising a plan for a full state monopoly on arms by the end of 2026, a step Beirut says is close to complete south of the Litani but still contested further north. As of late August 2026, the ceasefire nominally holds but Israeli strikes and Hezbollah threats of retaliation continue on a near-weekly basis, with no durable resolution of the disarmament question.', 'market_impact': "The World Bank's August 21, 2026 assessment is the sharpest figure available: it projects Lebanon's economy will contract 6.4% in 2026 because of the renewed war — a swing of 10.4 percentage points versus a no-conflict counterfactual — reversing an estimated 4.2% GDP rebound in 2025 that had been driven by improving consumption, investment, and tourism. Lebanese Finance Minister Yassine Jaber has estimated the combined direct and indirect losses from conflict since 2023 (spanning the 2024 and 2026 escalations) could reach $20 billion. For the 2024 round alone, the World Bank/Lebanese government put total economic cost at $14 billion, split between $6.8 billion in physical damage and $7.2 billion in economic losses from lost productivity and forgone revenue; housing losses alone were $4.6 billion and tourism losses $3.6 billion, sectors that remain the most exposed to any renewed escalation. Inflation is forecast to climb to 17.5% in 2026, driven by supply disruptions, higher shipping costs, and rising oil prices linked to the broader regional war, further eroding household purchasing power in an economy still recovering from its 2019-2023 financial collapse. The March 2026 escalation compounded this by displacing over a million people and further damaging housing stock and infrastructure that had only begun to be repaired after 2024, while suppressing tourism and domestic demand at a moment when both had been recovering. Lebanon's ability to attract reconstruction financing remains hostage to progress on Hezbollah disarmament, since major donors and Gulf states have conditioned support on the Lebanese state re-establishing a monopoly on arms — the same benchmark the LAF was tasked with completing by year-end 2026.", 'sources': [("World Bank — Renewed Conflict Derails Lebanon's Fragile Economic Recovery", 'https://www.worldbank.org/en/news/press-release/2026/08/21/renewed-conflict-derails-lebanon-s-fragile-economic-recovery'), ("Al Jazeera — World Bank projects war-hit Lebanon's economy to contract by 6.4 percent", 'https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2026/8/22/world-bank-projects-war-hit-lebanons-economy-to-contract-by-6-4-percent'), ('Al Jazeera — Israeli strikes kill 11 in southern Lebanon as attacks intensify', 'https://www.aljazeera.com/amp/news/2026/8/15/israeli-strike-kills-seven-in-south-lebanon-as-attacks-intensify'), ('Bloomberg — Israel Strikes Lebanon as US Prepares Fresh Iran Measures', 'https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2026-08-16/israel-strikes-lebanon-as-end-of-us-iran-ceasefire-looms'), ('Times of Israel — Lebanese PM says country close to completing Hezbollah disarmament south of Litani River', 'https://www.timesofisrael.com/lebanese-pm-says-country-close-to-completing-hezbollah-disarmament-south-of-litani-river/'), ('NPR — Hezbollah rejects ceasefire deal agreed on by Israel and Lebanon', 'https://www.npr.org/2026/06/04/g-s1-125942/israel-lebanon-ceasefire'), ("CNN — Lebanon says first phase of plan to disarm Hezbollah completed, but Netanyahu warns progress 'far from sufficient'", 'https://www.cnn.com/2026/01/08/middleeast/hezbollah-disarmament-israel-lebanon-conflict-intl')]},
    {'name': "Libya's Rival Governments Standoff", 'status': 'Frozen stalemate, no resolution in sight', 'type': 'Political Instability', 'impact': 'Limited', 'stats': [('Unified national budget (April 11, 2026)', 'LYD 190bn (~$29.6bn) -- first since 2013'), ('NOC ring-fenced allocation', 'LYD 12bn'), ('Oil production, early 2026', '>1.52 million bpd -- highest in 13 years'), ('TotalEnergies/Chevron deal (Jan 24, 2026)', '~$20bn, 25-year term, targeting +850,000 bpd'), ('IMF 2026 GDP growth forecast', '~4.4%'), ('2026 fiscal balance forecast', 'Surplus of ~2.2% of GDP'), ('Historic blockade cost (2020)', '>$9.8bn lost revenue, ~92% export drop'), ("Russia's Africa Corps personnel", '~2,000-2,500')], 'groups': "Government of National Unity (GNU, Tripoli) under PM Abdul Hamid Dbeibah; Government of National Stability (GNS) backed by the House of Representatives; Khalifa Haftar's Libyan National Army (LNA) and his son Saddam Haftar; UNSMIL (UN Support Mission in Libya); Russia's Africa Corps (Wagner successor); Turkey (GNU-aligned); Egypt and UAE (LNA-aligned); National Oil Corporation (NOC); TotalEnergies and Chevron as new concession holders.", 'affected': ['LBY'], 'summary': "Libya has remained split since 2014 between the UN-recognized Government of National Unity (GNU) in Tripoli, led by PM Abdul Hamid Dbeibah, and the eastern Government of National Stability (GNS), backed by the House of Representatives and Khalifa Haftar's Libyan National Army (LNA). Elections planned for December 2021 have never been held; the High National Elections Commission's target of mid-April 2026 also lapsed amid continued fragmentation over an electoral framework and whether a new unified interim government should organize the vote. In a rare breakthrough, U.S.-brokered talks produced Libya's first unified national budget since 2013, enacted April 11, 2026 at LYD190 billion (about $29.6bn), with LYD12bn ring-fenced directly to the National Oil Corporation (NOC) to protect production. Western militias reportedly view the budget as the financial baseline for a U.S.-backed roadmap that would keep Dbeibah as PM while elevating Saddam Haftar (Khalifa's son) to the presidency -- a sign of elite bargaining rather than genuine reunification. Meanwhile Russia has deepened its foothold in the east: its 'Africa Corps' (successor to Wagner) has grown to an estimated 2,000-2,500 personnel, expanding air-base infrastructure at sites like Brak al-Shati (where personnel grew from 300 to 450 since December 2024) and running naval traffic through Tobruk. On the energy side, Libya signed a 25-year, roughly $20bn development deal with TotalEnergies and Chevron on January 24, 2026, aiming to add up to 850,000 bpd of capacity, and by early 2026 national oil output exceeded 1.52 million bpd, its highest in 13 years. As of August 2026, the UN Security Council's monthly reporting still frames Libya as caught 'between internal impasse and international interference,' with rival militias and endemic corruption perpetuating the status quo despite the fiscal truce.", 'market_impact': "Libya's economy remains almost entirely oil-dependent, and the political detente has had measurable fiscal effects: the IMF's April 2026 Article IV concluding statement projects real GDP growth of about 4.4% in 2026 (other forecasts put it as high as 6.5%), with the fiscal balance flipping to a surplus of roughly 2.2% of GDP in 2026 on the back of rising oil revenue, contingent on security and output holding. The unified LYD190bn ($29.6bn) budget agreed in April 2026 -- the first since 2013 -- includes a LYD12bn NOC allocation explicitly meant to de-risk production from factional revenue disputes. Historically, blockades have been extremely costly: the 2020 Haftar-led blockade alone cost Libya more than $9.8bn in lost revenue and cut crude exports by roughly 92%, while a 2022 shutdown of oil installations was estimated to cost the country around $60 million per day -- figures that still anchor investor risk pricing on any future closure. Against that backdrop, the $20bn TotalEnergies-Chevron 25-year deal (announced January 24, 2026, targeting up to 850,000 bpd of new capacity) and a fresh round of exploration licenses issued to foreign firms (Al Jazeera, February 11, 2026) signal improving international sentiment, with S&P Global describing oil majors 'flocking' back to Libya for 2026. Total production above 1.52 million bpd is the highest level in 13 years, per NOC statements. Nonetheless, disputes over revenue-sharing and the risk of renewed export blockades remain the central threat cited by analysts to sustained investment, and Russia's expanding Africa Corps presence -- including infrastructure works at multiple air bases -- adds a geopolitical risk premium that is distinct from, but compounds, the purely fiscal/political fragmentation risk.", 'sources': [('IMF, Libya 2026 Article IV Concluding Statement', 'https://www.imf.org/en/news/articles/2026/04/10/mcs-04102026-libya-staff-concluding-statement-of-the-2026-article-iv-consultation-mission'), ('Security Council Report, Libya Monthly Forecast (August 2026)', 'https://www.securitycouncilreport.org/monthly-forecast/2026-08/libya-69.php'), ('S&P Global, Commodities 2026: Oil majors flock to Libya', 'https://www.spglobal.com/energy/en/news-research/latest-news/crude-oil/121925-commodities-2026-oil-majors-flock-to-libya-as-sentiment-improves'), ('Al Jazeera, Libya issues rare oil exploration licences to foreign firms', 'https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2026/2/11/libya-issues-rare-oil-exploration-licences-to-foreign-firms'), ('The National, Libya grapples with disastrous finances in spite of oil boom', 'https://www.thenationalnews.com/business/economy/2026/02/01/libya-grapples-with-disastrous-finances-in-spite-of-oil-boom/'), ('Africa Defense Forum, Africa Corps Work at Libyan Air Bases Raises Concerns', 'https://adf-magazine.com/2026/08/africa-corps-work-at-libyan-air-bases-raises-concerns/'), ('Middle East Council on Global Affairs, Libya: Trapped Between Internal Impasse and International Interference', 'https://mecouncil.org/publication/libya-trapped-between-internal-impasse-and-international-interference/')]},
    {'name': '2026 Pakistan-Afghanistan War', 'status': 'Active, following Feb 2026 escalation', 'type': 'Interstate War', 'impact': 'Significant', 'stats': [('Afghan civilians killed, Q1 2026', '372 killed, 397 injured (UN/OHCHR)'), ('People displaced (2026)', '>115,000'), ('Bilateral trade decline, 2024->2025', '$2.46bn -> $1.77bn (-28%)'), ('Pakistani exporter losses since border closure', '~$4.5bn'), ('Monthly export loss, early 2026', '$177m/month (-56%)'), ('Torkham border closure duration', '>4 months from mid-Oct 2025'), ('Containers/trucks stranded (Jan-Feb 2026)', '10,000-12,000'), ('Estimated daily trade loss', '~$6m/day (PKR 50bn/month)')], 'groups': 'Pakistan military and government (PM Shehbaz Sharif); Afghan Taliban government (Islamic Emirate of Afghanistan); Tehreek-e-Taliban Pakistan (TTP), the proximate trigger for Pakistani strikes; China (lead mediator via Urumqi talks and its Special Envoy for Afghanistan); Qatar and Turkey (October 2025 Doha ceasefire brokers); OHCHR/UN human rights monitors documenting civilian casualties.', 'affected': ['PAK', 'AFG'], 'summary': "Cross-border conflict between Pakistan and the Taliban-led Afghan government began with skirmishes in October 2025, after Pakistan conducted an airstrike in Kabul targeting the leader of Tehreek-e-Taliban Pakistan (TTP), which Islamabad accuses Kabul of sheltering. A Qatar/Turkey-brokered Doha ceasefire in October 2025 held only tenuously and collapsed in February 2026. On February 26, 2026, Afghan forces launched a cross-border offensive, and Pakistan responded on February 27 with coordinated air and ground strikes on military installations in Kabul, Kandahar, and border areas. In March 2026, a Pakistani airstrike hit a drug rehabilitation facility in Kabul, contributing heavily to a death toll the UN put at 372 Afghan civilians killed and 397 injured in the first three months of 2026 alone (OHCHR/UN reporting, per Al Jazeera). A fragile ceasefire in March 2026 has repeatedly frayed, with both sides accusing each other of new attacks as recently as late April 2026 (Kunar province and South Waziristan incidents). China has emerged as the key mediator, hosting talks in Urumqi beginning April 1, 2026, after which Pakistan and Afghanistan agreed on April 8 to 'explore a comprehensive solution,' though a durable settlement remains elusive. Sporadic clashes have continued into mid-2026 -- including a June 10, 2026 incident where Pakistan claimed 26 Afghan fighters killed while the Taliban said 13 people, including children, died in Pakistani air raids. As of August 2026, more than 115,000 people have been displaced by the fighting, and the underlying dispute -- Pakistan's demand that Kabul act against the TTP versus Afghan objections to Pakistani strikes on its territory -- remains unresolved despite ongoing Chinese-mediated diplomacy.", 'market_impact': "The conflict has devastated bilateral trade: total Pakistan-Afghanistan trade fell 28%, from $2.46bn in 2024 to $1.77bn in 2025, and Pakistani exporters have lost an estimated $4.5bn since the Torkham border closure began, according to Business Recorder reporting. Pakistani exports were declining by $177 million per month as of early 2026, a 56% drop from pre-closure levels. The Pakistan-Afghanistan Joint Chamber of Commerce and Industry estimated monthly losses of PKR 50 billion (roughly $6 million a day) during the January-February 2026 peak closure period, with 10,000-12,000 trucks and containers stranded at the main crossings. The Torkham crossing -- the single most important land trade route between the countries -- was shut to all commercial movement for more than four months from mid-October 2025, paralyzing supply chains on both sides. More than 4,000 daily-wage workers, drivers, porters, and service providers directly tied to cross-border trade lost their livelihoods during the closures. Individual exporters have reported catastrophic losses from spoiled and expired perishable stock -- one cited roughly $400,000 in losses from damaged goods alone. Beyond bilateral trade, the conflict disrupts a key artery for Afghan transit trade to Pakistani ports (Karachi) and complicates Pakistan's own domestic security spending, layering onto an economy already managing an IMF program and a fragile external-financing position.", 'sources': [('Al Jazeera, Over 370 Afghans killed in Pakistan conflict in first 3 months of 2026: UN', 'https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2026/5/12/over-370-afghans-killed-in-pakistan-conflict-in-first-3-months-of-2026-un'), ('Al Jazeera, Ceasefire at risk as Pakistan and Afghanistan report cross-border attacks', 'https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2026/4/27/ceasefire-at-risk-as-pakistan-and-afghanistan-report-cross-border-attacks'), ('Al Jazeera, Pakistan, Afghanistan hold talks in China to end months of conflict', 'https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2026/4/2/pakistan-afghanistan-hold-talks-in-china-to-end-months-of-conflict'), ('OHCHR, Afghan-Pakistani border: UN experts urgently call for lasting peace', 'https://www.ohchr.org/en/press-releases/2026/03/afghan-pakistani-border-un-experts-urgently-call-lasting-peace'), ('Business Recorder, Afghan border closure inflicts USD4.5bn loss on Pak exporters', 'https://www.brecorder.com/news/40436004/afghan-border-closure-inflicts-usd45bn-loss-on-pak-exporters-report'), ('GPB/NPR, Pakistan-Afghanistan border closures paralyze trade along a key route', 'https://www.gpb.org/news/2026/02/10/pakistan-afghanistan-border-closures-paralyze-trade-along-key-route'), ("The Diplomat, Why China's Quiet Mediation Could Pave the Way for Easing Pakistan-Afghanistan Tensions", 'https://thediplomat.com/2026/04/why-chinas-quiet-mediation-could-pave-the-way-for-easing-pakistan-afghanistan-tensions/')]},
    {'name': 'India-Pakistan Kashmir Crisis', 'status': 'Ceasefire since May 2025, relations still frozen', 'type': 'Territorial Dispute', 'impact': 'Critical', 'stats': [('Pahalgam attack toll (April 22, 2025)', '26 civilians killed'), ('KSE-30 single-day drop post-strikes', '-7.2%'), ('KSE-30 cumulative decline since Pahalgam', '-14.2%'), ('Sensex/Nifty50 move', '~-0.5% to -0.58%'), ('India FY2026-27 defense budget', 'Rs 7.85 trillion (~$87bn), +15%'), ('Pakistan FY2025-26 defense budget', 'Rs 2.55 trillion (~$9bn), +20%'), ('Pakistan GDP impact estimate from IWT suspension', '-1.5% to -2%'), ('PaJK unrest deaths (June-Aug 2026, contested)', '21-89 depending on source')], 'groups': 'Government of India (PM Modi) and Jammu & Kashmir CM Omar Abdullah; Government of Pakistan (PM Shehbaz Sharif); The Resistance Front (linked by India to Lashkar-e-Taiba), blamed for the Pahalgam attack; Joint Awami Action Committee (JAAC), the banned protest coalition in Pakistan-administered Kashmir; United States (May 2025 ceasefire broker).', 'affected': ['IND', 'PAK'], 'summary': "The current crisis traces to the April 22, 2025 terror attack in Pahalgam, Indian-administered Jammu & Kashmir, which killed 26 civilians (25 Indian and one Nepali tourist) and was claimed by a group India links to Pakistan-based Lashkar-e-Taiba. India responded by suspending the 1960 Indus Waters Treaty on April 23, 2025, closing the Attari-Wagah border, and expelling diplomats. On May 7, 2025, India launched Operation Sindoor, striking nine locations in Pakistan and Pakistan-administered Kashmir -- the first time India crossed the international boundary since 1971. Four days of cross-border artillery, drone, and missile exchanges followed (May 6-10, 2025), with Pakistan reporting eight citizens killed in Indian strikes and India reporting 13 civilians killed by Pakistani shelling, before a US-brokered ceasefire took hold on May 10, 2025. Through the first half of 2026, ties remained severely strained in what analysts describe as 'armed coexistence,' with Jammu & Kashmir Chief Minister Omar Abdullah continuing to press for restored statehood. A second front opened in mid-2026: in Pakistan-administered Kashmir (PaJK/Azad Kashmir), the Joint Awami Action Committee (JAAC) -- a grassroots coalition of traders, students, and civil society groups -- led sustained protests over governance and electoral reform that Islamabad banned as a 'proscribed organisation' on June 5, 2026. A July 27, 2026 crackdown on a JAAC-led march from Rawalakot toward Muzaffarabad killed at least 21 protesters, with contested tolls from various sources ranging up to 60-89 dead by August amid a communications blackout. PaJK elections in early August 2026 proceeded amid boycotts and further unrest. As of late August 2026, the Indus Waters Treaty remains suspended and the underlying Kashmir dispute -- now compounded by PaJK's internal political crisis -- shows no sign of resolution.", 'market_impact': "Financial markets reacted asymmetrically to the May 2025 crisis: Pakistan's KSE-30 index plunged 7.2% in a single-day selloff after Indian strikes, forcing a trading halt, and had shed a cumulative 14.2% since the Pahalgam attack; India's Sensex and Nifty50, by contrast, moved only marginally (down roughly 0.5-0.6% at the worst point, later recovering). India's suspension of the Indus Waters Treaty threatens Pakistan's agricultural base directly: the Indus system irrigates roughly 80% of Pakistani farmland, agriculture employs close to 70% of the rural workforce, and analysts project the water disruption could shave 1.5-2% off Pakistan's GDP, with an estimated 40% of Punjab and Azad Kashmir crop area (wheat and rice) affected by reduced Chenab flows. Hydropower -- a third of Pakistan's electricity supply -- is similarly exposed. The closure of the Attari-Wagah crossing also disrupted third-country trade, including India's Afghanistan trade route (2024-25 exports to Afghanistan of $264.15m and imports of $591.49m, including $358m in dry fruit imports, per Indian trade data). Both governments have responded with sharp defense-spending increases: India's FY2026-27 budget allocated a record Rs7.85 trillion (~$87bn) to defense, a 15% jump that makes India the world's fourth-largest military spender; Pakistan raised its defense budget 20% to Rs 2.55 trillion (~$9bn) for FY2025-26, explicitly citing the conflict with India. The PaJK unrest since June 2026 has additionally triggered a communications blackout that has disrupted local commerce and tourism in an already economically marginal region.", 'sources': [('CFR, Global Conflict Tracker: Conflict Between India and Pakistan', 'https://www.cfr.org/global-conflict-tracker/conflict/conflict-between-india-and-pakistan'), ('Carnegie Endowment, Military Lessons from Operation Sindoor', 'https://carnegieendowment.org/research/2025/10/military-lessons-from-operation-sindoor?lang=en'), ('Stimson Center, Four Days in May: The India-Pakistan Crisis of 2025', 'https://www.stimson.org/2025/four-days-in-may-the-india-pakistan-crisis-of-2025/'), ("Business Standard, Explained: What India's Indus Waters Treaty suspension means for Pakistan", 'https://www.business-standard.com/external-affairs-defence-security/news/india-suspends-indus-waters-treaty-pakistan-impact-agriculture-power-125042400376_1.html'), ("The Diplomat, India's Defense Budget Jumps 15 Percent", 'https://thediplomat.com/2026/02/indias-defense-budget-jumps-15-percent/'), ('The Diplomat, Pakistan Wields Iron Fist Against Protesters in Pakistan-administered Jammu and Kashmir', 'https://thediplomat.com/2026/08/pakistan-wields-iron-fist-against-protesters-in-pakistan-administered-jammu-and-kashmir/'), ('Al Jazeera, Pakistan-administered Kashmir elections and protests - all you need to know', 'https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2026/8/3/pakistan-administered-kashmir-elections-and-protests-all-you-need-to-know')]},
    {'name': 'Balochistan Insurgency & CPEC Attacks', 'status': 'Sharply escalating through 2025-2026', 'type': 'Terrorism', 'impact': 'Significant', 'stats': [('Chinese nationals killed in CPEC-linked attacks, 2021-2024', '20 killed, 34 injured, across 14 attacks'), ('Jaffar Express hijacking hostages (March 11, 2025)', '~450 taken, ~346 rescued'), ('Jaffar Express hijacking deaths', '~30 militants killed, 20+ security/passengers killed'), ('July 4-8, 2026 attack wave deaths', '42 killed'), ('CPEC total pledged investment (through 2030)', '~$60-65bn'), ('Gwadar airport cost (2025)', '~$230 million')], 'groups': "Balochistan Liberation Army (BLA), the primary insurgent group targeting CPEC and Chinese nationals; Tehreek-e-Taliban Pakistan (TTP), joint operational partner in the July 2026 attack wave; Pakistani military and Frontier Corps; China (as CPEC's principal financier and target of attacks on its nationals); Task Force 88 (Pakistan Navy's dedicated CPEC security unit).", 'affected': ['PAK'], 'summary': "The Balochistan insurgency dates to the late 1940s, rooted in Baloch grievances that the province's natural gas and mineral resources are extracted by the Pakistani state without local benefit. Since roughly 2015, the Balochistan Liberation Army (BLA) -- designated a terrorist organization by Pakistan, the US, and the EU -- has directed much of its campaign at China-Pakistan Economic Corridor (CPEC) infrastructure and Chinese nationals, with 14 attacks between 2021 and late 2024 killing 20 Chinese citizens and injuring 34. The insurgency's most prominent recent escalation was the March 11, 2025 hijacking of the Jaffar Express: BLA militants derailed the Quetta-Peshawar train near Mashkaf in Bolan district and took roughly 450 passengers hostage, triggering a multi-day military operation that killed around 30 militants and freed over 340 hostages, while at least 20+ security personnel and passengers died -- an event Chatham House called 'a watershed in the Balochistan insurgency.' In late January-early February 2026, coordinated BLA attacks struck schools, hospitals, banks, police stations, and a high-security prison across multiple Balochistan districts. From July 4-8, 2026, a further wave of joint BLA-TTP coordinated assaults on security forces killed 42 people and exposed what analysts called severe counter-insurgency deficiencies -- limited territorial reach, slow response times, and an inability to protect CPEC-critical infrastructure. A bombing in Jiwani in mid-2026 arrived the same week Pakistan's own military disclosed the 42 additional deaths province-wide, reinforcing the sense that the security narrative around CPEC is now overtaking the investment narrative. As of August 2026, Gwadar -- CPEC's flagship port project -- is widely described as a 'no-go zone' for normal commercial activity, and analysts at RFE/RL and Jamestown describe the attacks as directly testing the China-Pakistan 'iron brotherhood.'", 'market_impact': "China has pledged roughly $60-65bn to CPEC infrastructure -- ports, highways, railways, power plants -- through 2030, but the security deterioration is now visibly constraining new commitments: analysts note that if the current environment persists, China is likely to slow the pace of new investment, reassess existing projects, and demand stronger security guarantees before releasing further capital. Pakistan built a dedicated Task Force 88 naval unit specifically for CPEC maritime security, an ongoing recurring cost. The Gwadar airport, inaugurated in 2025 at a cost of roughly $230 million, remains commercially underused because the surrounding city is effectively a security lockdown zone rather than an open economic hub -- one analysis bluntly termed Gwadar 'a security project rather than a commercial one.' The Jaffar Express hijacking forced Pakistan Railways to temporarily suspend the entire Quetta-Peshawar line linking Balochistan to Punjab and Sindh, disrupting freight and passenger corridors nationally, though a precise dollar cost of that suspension has not been officially quantified. Every year that Gwadar's security narrative persists, competing regional ports in the Gulf and South Asia consolidate market share that Gwadar was designed to capture, representing a longer-run opportunity cost to Pakistan's transit-trade ambitions. The compounding effect of the Jan-Feb 2026 attack wave and the July 2026 42-death escalation has made insurance and security costs for CPEC-linked projects a growing line item, even as the headline $60bn+ investment figure remains formally on the books.", 'sources': [('Chatham House, The hijacking of a train marks a watershed in the Balochistan insurgency', 'https://www.chathamhouse.org/2025/03/hijacking-train-marks-watershed-balochistan-insurgency'), ("The Diplomat, Jaffar Express Hijacking Exposes Pakistan's Failing Strategy in Balochistan", 'https://thediplomat.com/2025/03/jaffar-express-hijacking-exposes-pakistans-failing-strategy-in-balochistan/'), ("RFE/RL (via GlobalSecurity.org), Attacks On Chinese Projects Test China-Pakistan 'Iron Brotherhood' In Balochistan", 'https://www.globalsecurity.org/wmd/library/news/pakistan/2026/pakistan-260725-rferl01.htm'), ('Jamestown Foundation, Amid Geopolitical Tensions, Baloch Militant Attacks Undermine Sino-Pakistan Projects', 'https://jamestown.org/amid-geopolitical-tensions-baloch-militant-attacks-undermine-sino-pakistan-projects/'), ('ICPS, The July 2026 Balochistan Attacks Expose the Limits of Coercive Approach in Pakistan', 'https://www.icpsnet.org/index.php/comments/july-2026-balochistan-attacks-expose-limits-coercive-approach-pakistan'), ("GIS Reports, China's BRI faces a test in Balochistan", 'https://www.gisreportsonline.com/r/china-balochistan/')]},
    {'name': 'Iran-Aligned Militia Attacks on US Forces in Iraq', 'status': 'Recurring, intensified during the 2026 Iran war', 'type': 'Unconventional', 'impact': 'Significant', 'groups': "Kataib Hezbollah (dominant IRI member); Asaib Ahl al-Haq (~10,000 members, Iran-backed but outside the IRI umbrella per US Defense Dept.); Kataib Sayyid al-Shuhada; Harakat Hezbollah al-Nujaba; Islamic Resistance in Iraq (umbrella coalition); Popular Mobilization Forces (PMF), the state-sanctioned structure some militias operate within; Iraqi government (PM Mohammed Shia' al-Sudani); Kurdistan Regional Government (KRG); US CENTCOM; Saudi Arabia's Armed Forces (joint July 2026 strikes); HKN Energy (US operator of the Sarsang field).", 'affected': ['IRQ', 'IRN'], 'stats': [('Attempted militia attacks on US citizens/facilities, Feb-April 2026', '>600'), ('US strikes in Iraq reported April 7, 2026', '138 attacks; 73 PMF, 10 army, 3 police, 6 civilians killed'), ('Kurdish oil output cut (Sarsang strikes)', '>200,000 bpd'), ('Kurdish exports to Ceyhan, pre-war vs mid-2026', '~240,000 bpd -> 40,000-60,000 bpd'), ('Iraq oil revenue, March vs April 2026', '$1.96bn -> $950m'), ('PMF personnel / 2024 budget', '~238,000 personnel / ~$3.4bn'), ('IRGC drone attacks on Saudi Arabia (72 hrs, late July 2026)', '>30'), ('US troops in Iraq (2026), planned withdrawal', '~2,500; targeted Sept. 2026 withdrawal')], 'summary': "Iran-aligned Shia militias -- organized loosely under the Islamic Resistance in Iraq (IRI) umbrella, including Kataib Hezbollah, Kataib Sayyid al-Shuhada, and Harakat Hezbollah al-Nujaba (with the separately-aligned Asaib Ahl al-Haq also active) -- have targeted US forces in Iraq intermittently since the October 2023 start of the Gaza war, drawing on the state-funded Popular Mobilization Forces (PMF) structure, which had roughly 238,000 personnel and a $3.4bn budget as of 2024. The conflict entered a new, far more intense phase on February 28, 2026, when Israeli and US strikes killed Iran's supreme leader and other senior officials, triggering the broader '2026 Iran war': Iran retaliated with missile and drone strikes on Israel, US bases, and US allies, and closed the Strait of Hormuz. Kataib Hezbollah explicitly joined the fighting, and from February through April 2026 Iran-aligned militias carried out more than 600 attempted attacks on US citizens and facilities inside Iraq. Iraq's Kurdistan Region bore the heaviest toll, with drone strikes hitting the Sarsang oil field (operated by US firm HKN Energy) in early March and April, cutting Kurdish oil output by more than 200,000 bpd. On April 7, 2026, the Iraqi government reported that US strikes across the country had killed 73 PMF members, 10 Iraqi army soldiers, 3 federal police officers, and 6 civilians in 138 separate attacks; a Pakistan-mediated US-Iran ceasefire took effect April 7-8, followed by a US naval blockade of Iran on April 13 after follow-up Islamabad talks failed to produce a durable deal. Fighting resumed in July 2026: on July 28, US CENTCOM and Saudi Arabia's armed forces struck Iranian-backed militia sites in eastern Iraq after more than 30 IRGC-directed drone attacks on Saudi Arabia in 72 hours. Iraqi PM Sudani has tied a planned US troop withdrawal (targeted for September 2026, per a 2024 bilateral agreement, from roughly 2,500 troops currently) to prospects for disarming the pro-Iran factions, though as of August 2026 that disarmament has not materialized and militia attacks continue intermittently.", 'market_impact': "With roughly 90% of Iraqi state revenue derived from oil exports, the conflict has directly hit government finances: monthly oil revenue collapsed from $1.96bn in March 2026 to just $950m (1.2 trillion dinars) in April 2026, and by August 2026 Iraq was struggling to cover public-sector salary payments because oil revenue no longer covers the budget, per The National's August 4, 2026 reporting. The Kurdistan Region has been hit hardest: drone strikes on the Sarsang field cut Kurdish output by more than 200,000 bpd, and Kurdish crude flows to the Ceyhan export terminal in Turkey fell from roughly 240,000 bpd before the war to just 40,000-60,000 bpd by mid-2026, as many Kurdish oilfields shut down entirely amid the security crisis. Compounding the physical damage, PMF-influenced Iraqi courts have issued rulings deeming independent Kurdish oil exports via Turkey illegal, effectively closing off an alternate revenue channel for the KRG on legal rather than purely security grounds -- a dynamic the Middle East Institute called 'a self-inflicted wound and a gift to Tehran.' The PMF itself represents a major fiscal liability to Baghdad in its own right, with an official ~$3.4bn annual budget (2024 figure) even before combat-related damage is counted. The July 28, 2026 US-Saudi joint strikes followed more than 30 IRGC-directed drone attacks on Saudi Arabia within 72 hours, illustrating how the Iraq-based militia threat has begun imposing costs regionally, including on Gulf energy infrastructure and shipping-insurance premiums tied to the broader Strait of Hormuz campaign that ran alongside this conflict.", 'sources': [("FDD's Long War Journal, US, Saudi Arabia strike Iranian-backed militias in Iraq after militia attacks", 'https://www.longwarjournal.org/archives/2026/07/us-saudi-arabia-strike-iranian-backed-militias-in-iraq-after-militia-attacks.php'), ("Atlantic Council, Iraq's oil export vulnerability exposes the cost of unresolved disputes", 'https://www.atlanticcouncil.org/blogs/menasource/iraqs-oil-export-vulnerability-exposes-the-cost-of-unresolved-disputes/'), ('The National, Iraq struggles with July salary payments as oil revenue fails to cover budget', 'https://www.thenationalnews.com/news/mena/2026/08/04/iraq-struggles-with-july-salary-payments-as-oil-revenue-fails-to-cover-budget/'), ("Middle East Institute, Iraq's oil paralysis: A self-inflicted wound and a gift to Tehran", 'https://mei.edu/commentary/iraqs-oil-paralysis-a-self-inflicted-wound-and-a-gift-to-tehran/'), ("Rudaw, The largest collapse of Iraq's oil sector in modern history", 'https://www.rudaw.net/english/analysis/07052026-amp'), ("FPRI, Militias and Iraq's Role in a Multifaceted Regional War", 'https://www.fpri.org/article/2026/03/militias-and-iraqs-role-in-regional-conflict/'), ('Congressional Research Service, Iraq (updated June 8, 2026)', 'https://www.congress.gov/crs_external_products/IF/PDF/IF10404/IF10404.53.pdf')]},
    {'name': 'Egypt-Ethiopia Nile Dam (GERD) Dispute', 'status': "Unresolved, no binding agreement despite dam's completion", 'type': 'Territorial Dispute', 'impact': 'Limited', 'groups': "Ethiopia (PM Abiy Ahmed's government, Ethiopian Electric Power); Egypt (President Abdel Fattah al-Sisi); Sudan (Gen. Abdel Fattah al-Burhan); United States (Trump-led mediation effort); Eritrea (Egypt's deepening strategic alignment); Kenya and Somalia (attended GERD inauguration as regional allies of Addis Ababa).", 'affected': ['EGY', 'ETH', 'SDN'], 'stats': [('GERD reservoir capacity', '74 billion cubic meters'), ('GERD generation capacity', '>5,000 MW'), ('GERD inauguration date', 'September 9, 2025'), ('Egypt agriculture share of GDP (World Bank, 2020)', '~14.5%'), ('Egyptians employed in agriculture', '~1/4 of 102.3 million population'), ('Modeled water allocation loss (rapid 3-yr filling scenario)', '~51.3% +/- 2.6%'), ('Modeled food production decline (same scenario)', '~38.5% +/- 2.2%'), ('New Ethiopian dams announced March 2026', '3 dams (Karadobi, Mandaya, Beko Abo) at ~$3.5bn each (~$10.5bn total)')], 'summary': "The Grand Ethiopian Renaissance Dam (GERD), on the Blue Nile roughly 30km from the Sudanese border at Guba, reached its full 74 billion cubic meter reservoir capacity and was officially inaugurated on September 9, 2025, with PM Abiy Ahmed calling it 'a great achievement for all black people' before African leaders including Kenya's William Ruto and Somalia's Hassan Sheikh Mohamud. Egypt's President Abdel Fattah al-Sisi immediately called the dam an 'existential threat' to Egypt's water security and, in a letter to the UN Security Council, described the inauguration as a 'unilateral measure that violates international law.' With the GERD complete, the dispute has shifted to Ethiopia's next phase of Blue Nile development: in late March 2026, Addis Ababa unveiled plans for three additional dams -- Karadobi, Mabil (Mandaya), and Beko Abo -- each projected at roughly $3.5bn, reviving projects first proposed years earlier and testing whether Egypt can convert more than a decade of failed opposition to GERD into real leverage over what comes next. On January 16, 2026, US President Trump wrote to Sisi offering to revive American mediation, proposing a framework built around guaranteed water releases during droughts and dry periods to protect Egypt and Sudan while still letting Ethiopia generate substantial hydropower revenue; Sisi welcomed the offer and Sudan's army chief, General Abdel Fattah al-Burhan, backed it as well. Egypt's strategy has centered on diplomacy, international pressure, and coordination with Sudan rather than attempting to physically stop construction, while also deepening its alignment with Eritrea as a regional counterweight to Addis Ababa. As of August 2026, Al Jazeera's reporting frames the live question as whether Egypt -- having failed to prevent or shape GERD itself -- can use this next round of dam proposals to finally secure binding rules on dam operation, drought management, water releases, and information-sharing that have eluded it for over a decade of negotiations.", 'market_impact': "Agriculture remains central to Egypt's exposure: the sector accounted for roughly 14.5% of Egypt's GDP as of World Bank 2020 data and employs close to a quarter of the country's 102.3 million people, meaning any material reduction in Nile flow translates directly into rural livelihoods and food-security risk. Academic hydro-economic modeling (a coupled Eastern Nile river-system and Computable General Equilibrium model of Egypt's economy) found that under a rapid, uncoordinated 3-year GERD filling scenario, Egypt's annual Nile water allocation could fall by roughly 51% (+/-2.62 percentage points), agricultural land use by about 53% (+/-2.44 points), and food production by roughly 38% (+/-2.18 points) relative to baseline -- figures that underscore why Cairo treats dam-filling speed and coordination as an economic security issue, not just a diplomatic one, even though Ethiopia's actual multi-year filling process moderated the worst-case outcomes. Separately, analysts note GERD alone has the technical potential to cut Nile flow to Egypt by up to 25% in an unfavorable operating scenario. The same modeling literature (published in Nature Communications) finds that coordinated, cooperative operation of GERD -- as opposed to unilateral Ethiopian control -- could actually increase Egypt's GDP and reduce its irrigation deficits relative to a non-cooperative baseline, which is the economic logic underpinning Trump's January 2026 mediation offer centered on guaranteed drought-period releases. On the Ethiopian side, the three newly announced dams (Karadobi, Mandaya, Beko Abo) represent a combined roughly $10.5bn incremental hydropower investment pipeline, adding further leverage -- and further downstream risk -- to the basin's water economics. GERD itself, at 74 bcm storage and over 5,000 MW generation capacity, gives Ethiopia a substantial new source of export electricity revenue to regional grids, a major upside that is largely orthogonal to Egypt's downstream agricultural exposure.", 'sources': [("Al Jazeera, Ethiopia's PM Abiy Ahmed says mega dam GERD on the Nile 'now complete'", 'https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2025/7/3/ethiopias-pm-abiy-ahmed-says-mega-dam-gerd-on-the-nile-now-complete'), ("Al Jazeera, After GERD, can Egypt shape Ethiopia's next Nile dams?", 'https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2026/8/21/after-gerd-can-egypt-shape-ethiopias-next-nile-dams'), ('Washington Post, Trump offers to restart US mediation in Nile River dispute between Egypt and Ethiopia', 'https://www.washingtonpost.com/politics/2026/01/16/trump-nile-egypt-ethiopia-us-mediation/df1eb362-f329-11f0-a4dc-effc74cb25af_story.html'), ('Bloomberg, Trump Offers to Mediate Egypt-Ethiopia Nile Dam Dispute', 'https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2026-01-16/trump-offers-to-mediate-egypt-ethiopia-nile-dam-dispute'), ("Mongabay, Ethiopia's Renaissance mega-dam fuels energy hopes and regional anxiety", 'https://news.mongabay.com/2026/02/ethiopias-renaissance-mega-dam-fuels-energy-hopes-and-regional-anxiety/'), ('Nature Communications, Collaborative management of the Grand Ethiopian Renaissance Dam increases economic benefits and resilience', 'https://www.nature.com/articles/s41467-021-25877-w'), ('Horn Review, As Ethiopia Doubles Down on the Nile, Egypt Deepens Its Eritrea Alignment', 'https://hornreview.org/2026/04/22/as-ethiopia-doubles-down-on-the-nile-egypt-deepens-its-eritrea-alignment/')]},
    {'name': 'Western Sahara Conflict & Algeria-Morocco Rupture', 'status': 'Low-intensity conflict since the 2020 ceasefire collapse; diplomatic track shifting toward Morocco through 2025-2026', 'type': 'Territorial Dispute', 'impact': 'Limited', 'groups': 'Moroccan Royal Armed Forces, Polisario Front / Sahrawi Arab Democratic Republic government-in-exile (Algeria-backed), Algerian government', 'affected': ['MAR', 'DZA'], 'stats': [('Years since the 1991 UN ceasefire collapsed', '6 (since Nov 2020)'), ('Years of full Algeria-Morocco diplomatic rupture', '5 (since Aug 2021)'), ("UN Security Council resolution endorsing Morocco's autonomy plan", 'Resolution 2797, Oct 31, 2025'), ('Countries Morocco says now back its autonomy plan (Oct 2025)', '118, incl. US, France, Spain, Germany'), ("Morocco's annual Maghreb-Europe pipeline transit fees lost", '~€50m/year, plus 800m m³ of subsidized gas')], 'summary': "Morocco and the Polisario Front fought an open war over Western Sahara from 1975 until a 1991 UN-brokered ceasefire, which held for nearly three decades pending a self-determination referendum that was never held. The Polisario declared the ceasefire over in November 2020 after Moroccan forces moved to clear a blockade at the Guerguerat border crossing, and intermittent low-intensity exchanges — Polisario rocket and drone fire, Moroccan strikes — have continued along Morocco's defensive sand berm since. The conflict has increasingly moved to the diplomatic track and shifted toward Morocco: the UN Security Council's Resolution 2797 (Oct 31, 2025) endorsed Rabat's 2007 autonomy proposal as the negotiating basis rather than an independence referendum, the EU aligned behind that position in January 2026, and previously secret Madrid talks in February 2026 brought Morocco, the Polisario, Algeria, and mediators together for the first time in years. Algeria and the Polisario continue to reject any outcome short of a full referendum, and Algeria — which hosts the Polisario's government-in-exile and the Sahrawi refugee camps near Tindouf — has made this the central issue in its own foreign policy.", 'market_impact': "The dispute triggered a full Algeria-Morocco diplomatic rupture in August 2021, when Algiers cut ties, closed its airspace and land border to Morocco, and ordered state energy firm Sonatrach to stop supplying gas through the Maghreb-Europe Pipeline (GME), which had carried Algerian gas across Morocco to Spain since 1996. That single decision cost Morocco roughly €50m a year in transit fees plus 800 million cubic meters of gas it had received at a discounted, stable price for its own power stations, forcing Rabat to lean more heavily on LNG imports and a separate Spain-Morocco gas arrangement. The rupture has stayed diplomatic and economic rather than reigniting open war, but it has hardened North Africa's two largest economies into rival blocs — closing off intra-Maghreb trade and investment integration that would otherwise be a natural growth lever for both, and phosphate exports from the Western Sahara territory itself (mined by Morocco's state operator OCP) remain a recurring target of activist-led boycotts and legal challenges from firms sourcing it, given the territory's disputed status.", 'sources': [('Human Rights Watch: World Report 2026 — Morocco and Western Sahara', 'https://www.hrw.org/world-report/2026/country-chapters/morocco-and-western-sahara'), ('International Crisis Group: Western Sahara', 'https://www.crisisgroup.org/middle-east-north-africa/north-africa/western-sahara'), ("Council on Foreign Relations: Morocco's Victory on the Western Sahara", 'https://www.cfr.org/articles/moroccos-victory-western-sahara'), ('Atalayar: Polisario Front and Algeria reject US resolution on Western Sahara', 'https://www.atalayar.com/en/articulo/politics/polisario-front-and-algeria-reject-us-resolution-on-western-sahara/20251027160000219827.html'), ("The New Arab: Why Algeria's gas pipeline closure will prove costly for all", 'https://www.newarab.com/analysis/why-algerias-gas-pipeline-closure-will-prove-costly-all'), ('VOA: Diplomatic Dispute Between Algeria and Morocco Prompts Energy Crisis in Spain', 'https://www.voanews.com/a/diplomatic-dispute-between-algeria-and-morocco-prompts-energy-crisis-in-spain/6298213.html')]},
]


